import{T as e}from"./index-BYqmtl73.js";var t=e({LIBRARY_DEEP_2:()=>n}),n={"lib-f2":{thesis:`Naming the stall out loud converts hesitation into deliberation. Confidence is not the absence of a pause — it is ownership of one.`,deep:[`When your mind goes blank, the instinct is to hide it: keep making sound, look busy, hope. That instinct is what produces the ramble. Hidden hesitation is read as incompetence; named hesitation is read as thought. The difference between the two is a single sentence, and the sentence costs you nothing except the admission.`,`The mechanism works because it removes the audience's uncertainty. When someone says "let me give you the honest version, I've got three threads here and I want to pick the right one", the room stops evaluating you and starts waiting. You have converted an unexplained silence into a stated process, and stated processes are trusted even when they are slower.`,`There are three versions worth having. The *threads* version for a complex question. The *correction* version for when you started badly: "let me start again, the real point is this". And the *boundary* version for when you genuinely cannot answer: "I don't know, and I'd rather say that than guess." All three share the property that they name the truth of the moment rather than performing a competence you do not have in that second.`,`One caution: naming the stall is only convincing if it is followed by something. Used repeatedly with nothing after it, it becomes a verbal tic — the person who always has "three threads" and never resolves them. Once or twice, then deliver.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`What's your view on the restructuring?`},{who:`You`,line:`Give me two seconds — I have three reactions to that and only one of them I'd defend in public.`},{who:`Them`,line:`Fair enough.`},{who:`You`,line:`Here's the one I'd defend: the structure is fine, the sequencing is wrong. We're cutting before we've moved the work.`}],failureModes:[`Naming the stall out loud on every question. It becomes a signature of evasion rather than of thought.`,`Naming it and then producing nothing of substance. The room will remember the preamble and the emptiness together.`,`Naming it apologetically — "sorry, this is a hard question". The apology is the problem, not the pause. State the process without the apology.`],drill:[`In the Impromptu Lab, record a hard prompt and deliberately open with a named stall, then answer with a structure.`,`Practise the correction version: start badly on purpose, say "let me start with the actual point", and continue.`,`Use it once in a real meeting this week and notice that nobody reacted badly.`],related:[`lib-f8`,`lib-f1`,`lib-f7`,`lib-g3`]},"lib-f5":{thesis:`Silence is pressure that costs you nothing. Most people concede it because they cannot tolerate four seconds of nothing.`,deep:[`In negotiation and in conflict, whoever speaks first after a demand has usually given something. They have given a reason, a concession, a justification, or a softening. That is not because silence is magic but because silence is uncomfortable and discomfort is resolved by speech — and the person who resolves it first is the person who pays.`,`The tolerance is trainable, and it must be trained in low stakes first. Start by not replying for three seconds after someone finishes a story. Then after they answer a question. Then after they name a price. Each stage builds two seconds of additional capacity, and capacity is what you need — the technique itself is nothing more than not talking.`,`The physical component matters and is the part people get wrong. A silence delivered with a nervous smile, a fidget, or a rush of breath reads as anxiety and hands the pressure straight back. A silence delivered with a relaxed face, still hands and comfortable eye contact reads as confidence. Identical silence, opposite effect — decided entirely by the body.`,`The ethical version is worth stating. Silence as leverage in a negotiation is fair; silence as punishment in a relationship is not. In intimate contexts, the still, wordless gap is experienced as withdrawal and does damage far beyond the value of the point. Use silence where there is something to negotiate — not where there is someone to punish.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`We'd need to see a discount before we can go further. Fifteen percent.`},{who:`You`,line:`[relaxed face, three seconds of silence, no movement]`},{who:`Them`,line:`...Or, look, twelve might work if we extend the term.`},{who:`You`,line:`Extending the term is possible. Twelve isn't. Tell me what the term would need to be.`}],failureModes:[`Nervous silence: smiling, fidgeting, or exhaling audibly. That reads as uncertainty and reverses the pressure.`,`Using it in personal relationships, where it reads as withdrawal rather than strategy.`,`Breaking it yourself with a justification — the three seconds pass and you explain why your number was fair, which is the same as conceding.`],drill:[`Arena: stay silent for one full beat after the persona finishes, three times in a session.`,`In a shop or café, let a silence run after a question you would normally fill.`,`Then use it after one real demand this week and write down what the other person added unprompted.`],related:[`lib-g3`,`lib-g2`,`lib-f1`]},"lib-f7":{thesis:`Three observations, two connections, one ask — a structure that uses the room as material instead of your memory.`,deep:[`The 3-2-1 frame exists for the specific situation where you must speak with no preparation: a toast, a feedback round, an unexpected introduction, a client call where you need to sound useful immediately. It works because it does not require you to have content in your head — it requires only that you have been present, because the first three items come from the room.`,`The three observations should be specific and true: something someone said, something that happened, something you noticed. They do not need to be clever, only concrete. The two connections are where the thinking happens — you are paid for how you link the observations. Then one ask or one claim, which gives the whole thing a destination.`,`The reason this beats memorised material is that it demonstrates an ability the audience can verify: attention. Anyone can sound prepared; almost nobody can sound genuinely present, because it requires having actually listened. The frame simply gives that presence a shape, so that being attentive looks like being articulate.`,`Practical note: the observations should be delivered without preamble. "Three things I noticed" is fine as a signpost, but do not explain the frame before using it — explaining the technique turns a moment of presence into a demonstration of a technique, which is precisely what it was designed to avoid.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Three things and I'll sit down. The first is that every question from the floor was about onboarding, not pricing. The second is that two people used the phrase 'it depends who you ask'. The third is that we ran over by twenty minutes and nobody objected.`},{who:`You`,line:`Those connect for me: the questions people ask tell you where the confusion lives, and 'it depends who you ask' is a documentation problem, not a product one. And we had time to keep asking because nobody could answer definitively.`},{who:`You`,line:`So my one ask: before the next release, one page that says who to ask about what. That's it.`}],failureModes:[`Observations that are opinions in disguise — "the third is that we are working very hard". Opinions do not demonstrate attention; facts noticed do.`,`Explaining the frame before delivering it, which turns presence into a technique demo.`,`Ending without an ask or a claim, which leaves the room with three observations and no destination.`],drill:[`Impromptu Lab: give a three-observation, two-connection, one-ask answer with ten seconds of prep.`,`Take the observations directly from something you actually noticed in the last hour.`,`Then use the frame in a real meeting at the end, when nobody has prepared anything.`],related:[`lib-f1`,`lib-s6`,`lib-g3`]},"lib-f8":{thesis:`Never define yourself cold. Bridge from something they already care about into what you do.`,deep:[`"What do you do?" is the most dangerous question in professional small talk, because the honest long answer is a history and the honest short answer is a job title, and neither engages anyone. The bridge sentence solves it by starting inside the other person's world and moving from there, so that your work arrives already connected to something they have a reason to care about.`,`The structure is: their thing, similarity, your thing, and the one interesting difference. "You mentioned the mess of approvals — I have the same problem in sensor deployments, except mine gets rejected by weather instead of by building management." Three sentences and you have a shared frame, an explanation, and a memorable specificity.`,`This works because relevance is relational. A fact about you is not interesting in itself; it becomes interesting when it explains something in their world. The bridge sentence is not a trick of flattery — it is a genuine claim that your work and theirs share a mechanism, and the quality of the sentence depends on the quality of that claim.`,`The failure is a false bridge: claiming a similarity that does not exist to seem agreeable. People detect a manufactured connection immediately, and it is worse than no bridge at all because it suggests you were not listening, in a sentence designed to prove the opposite.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`So what do you do?`},{who:`You`,line:`You were describing the approval mess — three sign-offs for a decision nobody disputes?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Exactly that.`},{who:`You`,line:`Same problem in a different costume. I get the fun version: a sensor that has to survive a winter, where the approval process is the weather. The interesting bit is that the failures are all about the handover between stages, not the technology.`}],failureModes:[`Inventing a similarity. People hear the false bridge immediately and it is more damaging than a plain job title.`,`Bridging and then reverting to the standard description of your work, which orphans the connection you just built.`,`Bridging from the wrong thing — the trivial detail rather than the thing they were animated by.`],drill:[`Arena: in three conversations, bridge from something the persona disclosed into your own work.`,`Keep the sentence under forty words and include one interesting difference.`,`Then test it on a real stranger this week and notice whether they ask a follow-up about your work.`],related:[`lib-c3`,`lib-f4`,`lib-s5`]},"lib-s1":{thesis:`Six structural beats carry any story, from Pixar to a wedding speech. The two 'because of that' beats are the ones amateurs skip.`,deep:[`The spine is: once upon a time (world), every day (routine), until one day (disruption), because of that (consequence), because of that (consequence), until finally (resolution). Its value is not creativity — it is a container strong enough to survive you losing your place, which is why it works live and under pressure.`,`The most common structural failure is a story that begins at the disruption. Without a routine, there is nothing for the disruption to interrupt, so the audience has no baseline and therefore no feeling of change. Twenty seconds of ordinary life, then the break: that is what makes the break feel like a break rather than a fact.`,`The second most common failure is a consequence that is actually a sequence. "Because of that" must be caused by the previous beat, not merely next in time. If the next event would have happened anyway, it belongs in "and then", not in the spine — and the difference is exactly what separates a story from a chronology.`,`Advanced use: two spines running at once, one external and one internal. The external consequences raise the stakes; the internal ones make it about a person. This is how you write a story with a turn and no plot twist, and it is why the best stories feel both eventful and personal.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`I spent four years writing the same email on Friday afternoons.`},{who:`You`,line:`Every week, the same six lines to the same eleven people, and I was proud of how good it had got.`},{who:`You`,line:`Until one day somebody replied to it, to the whole list, with a single word: 'why?'`},{who:`You`,line:`Because of that I couldn't answer. Because of that I spent a weekend finding out that four of the eleven didn't open it at all.`},{who:`You`,line:`So the email stopped, and the four people who did read it now get a phone call instead. And on Fridays I sit outside for ten minutes, feeling oddly guilty about it.`}],failureModes:[`Starting at the disruption, so there is no routine to be interrupted.`,`Using "and then" where "because of that" is required — sequence dressed as causality.`,`Skipping the "ever since" close, which leaves the audience unsure why they were told the story.`],drill:[`Take two memories through all six beats, out loud, two minutes each.`,`Check that both consequence beats are genuinely causal.`,`Then compress one to sixty seconds keeping the hinge and the closing image.`],related:[`lib-s3`,`lib-s4`,`lib-s5`]},"lib-s2":{thesis:`Meaning lives in the gap between what is and what could be. A pitch that stays in possibility never feels urgent.`,deep:[`Duarte's insight is that persuasion is rhythm rather than argument. You alternate between the current reality and the possible future, and each swing builds tension until the final resolution. A talk that stays in the future is a vision statement; a talk that stays in the present is a status report; only the alternation creates the feeling that something could actually change.`,`Practically, this means structuring a pitch as a series of contrasts rather than a list of benefits. What is: manual, slow, invisible. What could be: one template, ten minutes, everyone knows. Then back to what is: the client email nobody wants to send. Each return to reality is what keeps the future from sounding like fantasy.`,`The craft point is honesty in the 'what is' sections. A current state described in softened language makes the gap look small; described in accurate, specific, uncomfortable language, it makes the future look necessary. Most pitchers under-describe the present because it feels rude, and then cannot work out why nobody is urgent.`,`The close should be a single, small version of the future — not the full transformation. "One week of this template" is more persuasive than "a transformed communication culture", because a listener can imagine themselves inside it and can survive failing at it.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`What is: every Friday, three people write the same update by hand.`},{who:`You`,line:`What could be: one person, three lines, ten minutes.`},{who:`You`,line:`Back to what is: last month that produced two contradictions that reached a client.`},{who:`You`,line:`What could be: one template, one owner, and by Friday nobody writes a status from scratch again. Try it for four weeks and tell me it didn't work.`}],failureModes:[`Staying in the future, which produces agreement without urgency.`,`Softening the current state so the gap looks survivable.`,`Ending on the grand transformation instead of a small, survivable first version.`],drill:[`Impromptu Lab: pitch one change, alternating what-is and what-could-be at least three times.`,`Make the current-state sentences specific and uncomfortable rather than polite.`,`Close with a small, survivable version of the future.`],related:[`lib-s6`,`lib-c6`,`lib-s3`]},"lib-s3":{thesis:`Build expectations for two beats, then subvert the third in a way that reframes everything before it.`,deep:[`Predictability is the enemy of power in narrative, just as it is in negotiation. A story that runs exactly as the audience expects is received as information; the same story with one well-placed subversion is received as meaning, because the listener is forced to reinterpret what they already heard.`,`The mechanism is retroactive reframing. When the third beat breaks the pattern, the first two beats are re-read in the light of the new information — which is why the best twists are not surprises but revelations. The audience does not feel tricked; they feel they have understood something that was there all along.`,`Craft rules: the subversion must be implicit in the earlier material rather than invented at the end. It must arrive late enough to have something to reframe. And it must not be explained — the moment you say "what I mean by that is", you have converted a revelation into a lesson, and lessons are forgotten.`,`The personal-narrative version is the most useful: a story where the shift is in your own understanding rather than in events. "I thought the problem was the team. Two beats later, the problem was me." Nothing external changes; everything is re-read. That is the structure of almost every memorable personal story in this library.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`For two years I was the person who caught everything. Every near-miss, every bad number, every awkward email — I found it first.`},{who:`You`,line:`Everyone said it was a gift, and it felt like one. I was indispensable.`},{who:`You`,line:`Then my manager left and the new one, in her second week, asked me a question nobody had asked: 'What have you caught that you didn't tell anyone about?'`},{who:`You`,line:`Turns out the answer was eleven things. Being the safety net had meant nobody else learned to fall, and I'd been the reason the team was fragile.`}],failureModes:[`A subversion that contradicts rather than reframes — the audience feels cheated rather than illuminated.`,`Explaining the twist afterwards, which converts revelation into instruction.`,`Placing the turn too early, so there is nothing yet to reinterpret.`],drill:[`Tell a two-minute story with one planted expectation and one subversion at the two-thirds mark.`,`Check that the subversion was implicit in the first beat rather than invented for the last.`,`End on the image and say nothing about what it meant.`],related:[`lib-s1`,`lib-w1`,`lib-s5`]},"lib-s4":{thesis:`One sound and one smell will do more than a paragraph of description. Two sensory anchors create a scene; five create a tour.`,deep:[`The brain builds scenes out of concrete sensory detail, and it does so automatically — which means the storyteller's job is not to describe but to trigger. A single well-chosen sound does more work than three sentences of atmosphere, because it arrives pre-loaded with associations the listener already owns.`,`The pairing matters. One auditory and one olfactory anchor is usually the strongest combination, because smell is the most memory-linked sense and sound the most immediately spatial. "The fridge hum that gets louder when nobody is talking" and "the smell of the photocopier" together install a room in about fifteen words.`,`Restraint is the craft. More than two anchors per scene and the listener stops constructing and starts receiving, which is far less memorable. This is why the best storytellers appear to leave things out — they do, deliberately, because the gaps are where the listener's own memory gets to participate.`,`Advanced move: introduce an anchor early and repeat it at the end. The repetition converts a detail into a frame, and a framed story feels composed rather than recounted. It costs one sentence and it is the difference between a story that stops and one that closes.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`The waiting room had a fish tank with a broken filter — this rattling hum, like a phone buzzing in another room.`},{who:`You`,line:`There was also the smell of hot photocopier, which for some reason made the whole thing feel like a school.`},{who:`You`,line:`I was in there forty minutes to be told a date that could have been an email.`},{who:`You`,line:`And the tank was still rattling when I left, which felt like a joke somebody hadn't finished telling.`}],failureModes:[`Too many anchors, which turns a scene into an inventory.`,`Anchors that carry information rather than emotion — accurate details that evoke nothing.`,`Using adjective-laden atmosphere ("a bleak, sterile waiting room") instead of one object that produces the feeling.`],drill:[`Retell any story adding exactly one sound and one smell.`,`Repeat one of them in your final line as a frame.`,`Record it and count how many images appeared in your own head.`],related:[`lib-s1`,`lib-s5`,`lib-c8`]},"lib-s5":{thesis:`One story about yourself, retellable in two sentences by someone else. Their version is your real draft.`,deep:[`Your reputation is composed almost entirely of other people's retellings of you, which means the most valuable narrative asset you own is a story that can survive compression and transmission in someone else's voice. Most people have anecdotes; far fewer have a story someone else can carry.`,`Three tests define it. It explains something you actually value, so it is not merely entertaining. It survives being told by someone else without losing its point. And it ends on an image or consequence rather than a moral, so the reteller gets to supply the meaning — which is why they will bother to retell it at all.`,`The subject should be a change, not an achievement. Achievement stories are impressive but difficult to retell, because the reteller cannot reproduce the impressiveness; change stories compress naturally because the listener can locate themselves in the turning point. "I stopped believing X" travels; "I grew the team to nineteen" does not.`,`The test that actually matters: tell it once, wait a day, and ask the listener to describe it as they would to someone else. Do not correct them. Their version is what will travel — and the distance between their retelling and your intention is a precise map of what needs work.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`So what do you actually care about, professionally?`},{who:`You`,line:`I used to think being needed was the same as being good at my job.`},{who:`You`,line:`Then I took two weeks off and came back to ninety-one messages asking where things were. Nothing had broken — nobody had been trusted to not need me.`},{who:`You`,line:`Most of what I do now is make myself less necessary. It's slow, and I still check things I shouldn't, but it's the best work I do.`}],failureModes:[`Making it an achievement story, which is impressive once and unretellable forever.`,`Ending with a moral, which tells the listener what to think and removes their reason to retell it.`,`Never testing it — most signature stories fail in transmission and the speaker never finds out.`],drill:[`Draft and deliver your signature story at ninety seconds, then at forty-five with the hinge intact.`,`Ask one listener what they think you value based on the story alone.`,`The next day, ask them to retell it and treat their version as your new draft.`],related:[`lib-s1`,`lib-s3`,`lib-c4`]},"lib-s6":{thesis:`Head, heart, hands. One undeniable fact, one human story, one concrete action. Skip any one and the talk fails.`,deep:[`Persuasion requires three different jobs done in one sequence: establishing that the problem is objective, that it matters to a person, and that there is something specific to do. Most pitches do one of the three and then blame the audience for the missing reaction. A number without a story persuades nobody; a story without a number is unfalsifiable; both without an ask are a mood.`,`The head should be one countable fact, not fifteen supporting ones. Multiple facts invite debate about which fact matters; a single undeniable specific makes the argument finite. Keep it to one sentence and make sure it contains something verifiable — a number, a comparison, an observable.`,`The heart is a story with a person in it, ideally small and specific. Not "morale is suffering" but "on Thursday, Rina stayed until eight rewriting a status update nobody read." The name and the detail are doing almost all of the work, because specificity is the difference between a claim and an experience.`,`The hands is one action sized correctly. If the ask needs a second sentence to explain, it is too big — the listener cannot imagine saying yes to something they cannot picture. And in a hostile room, invert the order: story first, because a defensive audience cannot process your numbers until it has decided you are not a threat.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Head: we spend two days a month writing status updates that four people read.`},{who:`You`,line:`Heart: last Thursday Rina stayed until eight finishing one. It was about a meeting we were planning for the following week.`},{who:`You`,line:`Hands: cancel Monday's status meeting for four weeks and replace it with a three-line Friday note. If it fails, we revert with no argument.`}],failureModes:[`Stacking facts until the head becomes a debate rather than a premise.`,`Substituting intensity for the heart — passion delivered as volume instead of a specific human consequence.`,`Asking for a transformation rather than an action, which nobody can picture accepting.`],drill:[`Deliver a two-minute pitch with all three parts, naming them out loud as you go.`,`Then cut it to forty-five seconds keeping all three.`,`Test it in a hostile setting by leading with the heart and putting the number second.`],related:[`lib-s2`,`lib-c6`,`lib-f7`]},"lib-g3":{thesis:`The more you say, the more ordinary you look. Short, dense statements create the impression of depth because the listener supplies it.`,deep:[`Greene's fourth law is usually read as a style preference and is actually a mechanism. Talk is abundant, and abundance is cheap. When you speak at length you are spending the most plentiful resource in the room, and the listeners' estimate of your value adjusts to what you are spending. Silence and brevity are scarce, and scarcity reads as substance.`,`The psychological engine is completion. A short, dense statement leaves a gap that the listener fills with their own interpretation — and interpretations are always more flattering than explanations, because the listener supplies meaning in their own vocabulary. A long explanation forecloses that; you have done the thinking they would have done for you, and they remember none of it.`,`Practically: one point per turn, no preamble, no justification unless asked, and stop before you feel finished. The feeling of being unfinished is the signal that you are at the right length, because the extra material you want to add is almost always defensive — it exists to pre-empt a challenge nobody made.`,`The limit is important. Saying less is not the same as saying nothing; the law is about density, not silence. A person who is genuinely withholding reads as evasive rather than powerful, and in close relationships it reads as cold. Use it in rooms where attention is contested, not with people who need to know you.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`What did you make of the proposal?`},{who:`You`,line:`It solves the wrong problem.`},{who:`Them`,line:`...Which part of it?`},{who:`You`,line:`The onboarding. The proposal assumes people leave because of price. They leave because of the second week.`}],failureModes:[`Using brevity to seem mysterious, which reads as evasive and makes people work to include you.`,`Being terse with people who need information — brevity in a briefing is a failure of care, not a display of power.`,`Density without substance: short statements that say nothing are merely quiet.`],drill:[`In the Arena, make three replies of under fifteen words that each contain one claim.`,`Practise stopping before you feel finished, and note the urge to add a justification.`,`In one meeting today, make your point once and then stay quiet for three full minutes.`],related:[`lib-f5`,`lib-g1`,`lib-g2`]},"lib-g4":{thesis:`Arguments entrench; demonstrations change minds. You win the point by making the point unnecessary.`,deep:[`Direct contradiction triggers a defensive response: the other person's identity becomes attached to their position and they will defend it even after the evidence has moved. This is why two intelligent people can argue for an hour and both leave more certain. Argument entrenches, and entrenchment is the opposite of what you wanted.`,`Demonstration bypasses the mechanism entirely because it does not ask anyone to admit anything. You run the test, show the number, build the small version, produce the example. Nobody has to concede in public, so nobody needs to defend — the evidence simply exists and can be integrated privately, which is how most real belief change actually happens.`,`The practical discipline is asking, before any disagreement, whether this can be demonstrated rather than debated. Often it can: a prototype, a five-minute experiment, one call to a customer, a quick measurement. The cost of demonstrating is usually a day; the cost of arguing is a relationship plus a position that never moves.`,`Where demonstration is impossible — a matter of taste, values, or an untestable prediction — the correct move is not argument but narrowing: 'what would change your mind?' That question converts a dispute into a definition and frequently reveals that the disagreement is smaller than it appeared.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`The build isn't the problem. It's fine.`},{who:`You`,line:`Maybe. Can I check?`},{who:`You`,line:`[runs the build, times it, shows the screen] Forty-one minutes.`},{who:`Them`,line:`Huh. That's... yeah. That's not fine.`}],failureModes:[`Demonstrating in a way designed to humiliate — the evidence lands, the relationship does not.`,`Arguing about things that could have been measured, which converts a solvable question into a status contest.`,`Demonstrating something irrelevant because it was easy to prove, rather than the thing actually in dispute.`],drill:[`Pick one live disagreement and design a five-minute demonstration instead of an argument.`,`Run it and report the result without commentary.`,`For anything undemonstrable, ask "what would change your mind?" and note the answer.`],related:[`lib-c9`,`lib-g6`,`lib-c7`]},"lib-g5":{thesis:`Anything with a fixed shape can be grasped and attacked. Predictability is the most exploitable trait a person has.`,deep:[`Formlessness is not inconsistency — it is refusing to offer your opponents a stable target. People build models of you within weeks: what you always object to, what you always want, which arguments you always make. Once that model exists, it can be used to steer you, and you will feel steered without being able to point to the moment it happened.`,`The mechanism applies far beyond strategy. In meetings, the person who always raises costs is managed by being given costs to raise. In negotiation, the party with a predictable first offer is always met with a prepared counter. The defence is not deception but variation: sometimes ask about timing instead of price, sometimes let someone else speak first, sometimes concede early on something small to break the pattern.`,`The safest form of formlessness is genuine curiosity, because it is unpredictable by nature. A person who asks a different question each time cannot be modelled, and the questions are honest rather than tactical. This is why the most strategically effective people in most organisations are also the most inquisitive — curiosity is both a virtue and an unreadable strategy.`,`One boundary: formlessness does not license unreliability. Being unpredictable about your tactics is a strength; being unpredictable about your commitments destroys trust. Keep your word exactly and vary your moves freely.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`I assume you're going to push for more budget again.`},{who:`You`,line:`Actually I want to talk about scope. If we cut the second phase, the budget question disappears.`},{who:`Them`,line:`That's not what I expected.`},{who:`You`,line:`It's what the numbers say. What would you cut first?`}],failureModes:[`Confusing formlessness with flakiness — varying your commitments destroys trust and is not strategy.`,`Being unpredictable for its own sake, which reads as erratic rather than unreadable.`,`Announcing that you are being unpredictable, which restores the model you were trying to break.`],drill:[`List three things people can predict about you in meetings. Then break one of them deliberately this week.`,`Replace your habitual first question with genuine curiosity and note how the conversation changes.`,`Keep every commitment exactly while varying your tactics — write both lists down.`],related:[`lib-g2`,`lib-g3`,`lib-c7`]},"lib-g7":{thesis:`Mastery comes from depth, not breadth. Every field has a hidden core that only appears after hours of immersion.`,deep:[`The early phase of any skill is supposed to feel slow, and most people quit precisely because it does. They interpret the plateau as a verdict on their talent rather than as the entry cost of a hidden structure — and the structure only becomes visible after the hours, which means the hours are the method, not the obstacle.`,`Greene's account of mastery is essentially about attention rather than time. Two people can spend a thousand hours and one becomes excellent because they watched closely, tolerated boredom, and kept asking why the exceptions happen. The distinguishing behaviour is not discipline in the abstract but a willingness to stay with a detail after it has stopped being interesting.`,`For speaking specifically, this means the plateau is real and productive. Weeks three to six feel like nothing is improving; the recordings say otherwise. The people who emerge are the ones who kept the daily rep through the flat stretch, because the flat stretch is where the automaticity is being installed.`,`The practical advice is to choose one thing and go embarrassingly deep. Not public speaking — one narrow skill, like the pause, or the story ending, or the clean refusal. Depth in one place creates transfer, because the structure you learn at depth turns out to be the same structure in adjacent skills.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`How long before I'm good at this?`},{who:`You`,line:`Two years of ten minutes a day. But you'll be noticeably different in six weeks.`},{who:`Them`,line:`That's a long time.`},{who:`You`,line:`It is. And you'll feel like nothing is happening for about three of those weeks — that's the part almost everybody quits in.`}],failureModes:[`Mistaking breadth for progress: consuming many frameworks without practising one to depth.`,`Quitting at the plateau, which arrives on a predictable schedule and feels like a verdict.`,`Going deep on something too broad (public speaking) rather than something narrow enough to actually change.`],drill:[`Choose one narrow skill from this programme and commit to thirty days on it alone.`,`Track the daily rep in writing so the plateau has evidence against it.`,`Expect weeks three to six to feel flat, and pre-decide what you will do in that stretch.`],related:[`lib-g1`,`lib-v1`,`lib-c8`]},"lib-c1":{thesis:`Criticism wounds pride and breeds resentment. Create the conditions for change instead of arguing for it.`,deep:[`Criticism is a status event before it is an informational one. The moment you tell someone they are wrong, their attention moves from the content to their standing, and from that point no information is processed — only defended against. This is why the most accurate feedback often produces the least change.`,`The alternative starts with a genuinely different question: what were they trying to achieve? Most errors are solutions to a problem you cannot see, and the person usually knows more about their constraints than you do. Asking converts criticism into diagnosis, and diagnosis is collaborative by nature.`,`When criticism is genuinely necessary — and sometimes it is — the delivery determines whether it is heard. Private rather than public. Behaviour rather than character. Specific rather than general. And framed as a shared problem: "this is going wrong and I think we both want it to work, so let's look at the mechanism" rather than "you did this wrong".`,`The deepest form of this rule is understanding that people do not change because they were told they were wrong. They change when a different behaviour becomes available, cheap, and dignified. Your job in feedback is not to establish fault but to make the better behaviour easy to choose.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Can I ask about the report? I want to understand how it got built before I say anything stupid.`},{who:`Them`,line:`I used last quarter's template.`},{who:`You`,line:`That makes sense — is that template still the one with the old margin assumptions?`},{who:`Them`,line:`...It is, actually. That's the problem, isn't it.`},{who:`You`,line:`That's the problem. Want to fix the template together so this can't happen again?`}],failureModes:[`Criticising in public, which guarantees defence rather than change.`,`Criticising character rather than behaviour — "you're careless" cannot be acted on; "this number is wrong" can.`,`Asking the diagnostic question and then criticising anyway, which teaches people that your questions are traps.`],drill:[`Recall one piece of feedback you gave that did not land. Rewrite it as a diagnostic question.`,`Deliver one piece of behavioural, private, specific feedback this week.`,`Notice whether the person offered a fix unprompted — that is the signal it was received.`],related:[`lib-c7`,`lib-c9`,`lib-g6`]},"lib-c2":{thesis:`Praise the specific choice, not the trait. Specific appreciation names a behaviour, which means it can be repeated.`,deep:[`"You're brilliant" is a compliment about a fixed quality, and fixed qualities are both unactionable and fragile — they tell the person their success is a property of them rather than a set of decisions they can repeat. "The way you handled that question kept the client in the room" names a behaviour, which means the person can do it again and knows you were watching.`,`The formula is observation plus effect: what they did, and what it caused. That structure makes the compliment credible, because it could only have been produced by someone present. Generic praise can be given without attention; specific praise cannot, and the recipient can tell the difference instantly.`,`The most valuable compliments are for things nobody notices: restraint, preparation, a difficult silence held, a question asked at the right moment, a bad idea dropped gracefully. These are rarely praised, so they land disproportionately — and praising them signals that you understand what the work actually involves, which is a second compliment in itself.`,`The counterpart rule is about receiving. Take the compliment in one breath and redistribute the credit to someone specific. Deflecting in front of others spills your credit over the room and makes everyone slightly uncomfortable; accepting and sharing is what high status looks like, and it makes the praiser feel their compliment was well placed.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`That was one of the best talks I've seen here. No notes.`},{who:`You`,line:`Thank you — that means something coming from you.`},{who:`You`,line:`The demo only worked because Rina fixed it twice at midnight. Ask her about the second time.`},{who:`Them`,line:`I will.`}],failureModes:[`Praising traits the person cannot control — appearance, natural talent, accent.`,`Praising so generally that it reads as politeness rather than observation.`,`Deflecting in public, which makes the praise about your modesty instead of their contribution.`],drill:[`Give three specific, causal compliments inside one Arena conversation.`,`Each must name a behaviour and its effect, not a trait.`,`Then give one in real life to someone whose contribution is usually invisible.`],related:[`lib-c3`,`lib-c9`,`lib-c4`]}},r=e({LIBRARY_DEEP_3:()=>i}),i={"lib-c3":{thesis:`You make more friends in two months by being interested than in two years by trying to be interesting. Attention is the rarest gift you can give.`,deep:[`This is the most quoted rule in the library and the most frequently performed rather than practised. The difference is detectable: performed interest asks questions in sequence without reacting; genuine interest reacts to the specific content of each answer. One produces a form, the other produces a conversation.`,`The mechanism is attention scarcity. Everyone in a room is competing to be interesting; almost nobody is offering undivided attention. That asymmetry means curiosity is not merely pleasant, it is rare — and rarity is what makes it memorable. The person who was actually interested in you is remembered as fascinating, which is the great irony of social skill.`,`The practical craft has three parts. Ask forked or open questions rather than closed ones. React briefly to each answer, proving you heard the specific words. And follow up at depth — the second and third questions about the same thing, which is where people feel met rather than surveyed.`,`One caution: interest without reciprocity becomes unnerving. Offer small things — a reaction, an admission, a brief relevant story. The ratio should feel roughly two-thirds them to one-third you in a first conversation, not nine to one. Interest is a gift; scrutiny is not.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`I'm on the sensors side, mostly.`},{who:`You`,line:`Mostly? What's the other part?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Honestly? The mud. I spend more time cleaning equipment than designing it.`},{who:`You`,line:`That's the first honest job description I've heard tonight. Is the mud a design failure, or is that just the job?`}],failureModes:[`Question sequences with no reactions, which read as a survey rather than interest.`,`Redirecting to yourself after each answer, which closes the thread you just opened.`,`Interest aimed at impressive people only, which everyone in the room notices.`],drill:[`Arena: ask three follow-ups on one thread before moving on.`,`React to each answer with something specific rather than "right" or "cool".`,`In one real conversation today, aim for two-thirds their talking to one-third yours.`],related:[`lib-f3`,`lib-c5`,`lib-f4`]},"lib-c4":{thesis:`The sweetest sound to a person is their own name — used early, used twice, then never again.`,deep:[`Names work because they are the highest-value identifier a person owns and because using one proves you were paying attention at a moment when it was easy not to. Used correctly — twice, early, attached to something specific — a name creates the feeling of being registered. Used badly, it becomes the signature move of a salesperson and the listener steps back.`,`The pattern: once in your first reply after learning it ("Good to meet you, Maya — what brought you here?") and once within the first few minutes, attached to something they said ("Maya, the thing about the mud is the most honest thing I've heard tonight"). Then stop. After that, the relationship exists without the label, and further repetition reads as pressure.`,`The deeper version is not names at all, it is detail. A remembered project, a sister's wedding, a broken phone — these outperform the name because they prove attention rather than recall. This is why the rule is really about being registered, with the name as the cheapest available proxy.`,`And the recoverable failure: if you forget a name, do not bluff and do not stack apologies. "I'm not going to pretend — your name has left the building. But I remember the project you were fighting for." One line of honesty plus a specific memory is more charming than a lucky recall, because it shows the relationship matters more than your record.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Maya, right? I'm Sam. What brought you here tonight?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Honestly? Free wine and mild professional guilt.`},{who:`You`,line:`Respectable. Maya — you said you're mostly on the sensor side. What's the part that isn't sensors?`}],failureModes:[`Sprinkling the name into every sentence, which is a pressure tactic everyone recognises.`,`Using a name to create false intimacy at the start of a difficult message — "Look, Maya, here's the thing..."`,`Bluffing when you have forgotten it, which risks getting it wrong and creates a small humiliation for both of you.`],drill:[`Arena: use the persona's name twice in the first three turns, then never again.`,`Replace the third usage with a specific detail they gave you.`,`In real life, practise the honest recovery line once with someone you have forgotten.`],related:[`lib-c3`,`lib-f6`,`lib-c2`]},"lib-c5":{thesis:`Listen with your body and the speaker will describe you as a brilliant conversationalist. Attention is more persuasive than contribution.`,deep:[`Listening is not a passive virtue; it is a set of visible behaviours, and the speaker is reading all of them. Phone face down. Torso angled toward them. Response short and followed by another question rather than a comparable story. These signals decide whether someone experiences you as present, and presence is what people are actually asking for when they say someone is good company.`,`The hardest part is not interrupting, and the reason it is hard is that listening requires holding a thought without speaking it. Most interruptions are not rudeness — they are anxiety that the thought will be lost. The fix is mechanical: keep a note, or hold one keyword, and trust that the thread can be returned to. Almost every thought survives ninety seconds of patience.`,`The second hardest part is not competing. When someone tells a story, the instinct is to offer a comparable one, which converts a shared moment into an exchange of credentials. The alternative is to ask about the thing they just said. It feels less generous and is dramatically more so.`,`Listening well also has a strategic benefit that is rarely named: you learn things. In any room, the person asking and listening accumulates information while everyone else spends it. That asymmetry — knowing more than you have said — is the foundation of almost every form of social influence in this library.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`So we lost the account, and honestly the worst part was telling the team.`},{who:`You`,line:`Telling the team was worse than losing it?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Much. Because they'd worked weekends on it.`},{who:`You`,line:`That's the part people don't see from outside. How did you do it?`}],failureModes:[`Listening while preparing your reply, which produces perfectly timed responses that answer the wrong thing.`,`Topping the story with a comparable one of your own, which turns listening into an exchange of credentials.`,`Performing listening — nodding and "mm-hmm" without a single follow-up on the actual content.`],drill:[`In three conversations today, respond with a question instead of your own story.`,`Keep your phone face down and your torso angled toward the speaker.`,`Note one thing you learned in each conversation that you would not have known otherwise.`],related:[`lib-c3`,`lib-f3`,`lib-c2`]},"lib-c6":{thesis:`People act on their own reasons. Frame every request as something they already want.`,deep:[`Every request can be framed two ways: what you need, and what they get. Only the second moves people reliably, and the translation is usually a single sentence. "I need the data by Thursday" becomes "if the data lands Thursday, the release goes out before the weekend and support doesn't spend Monday firefighting."`,`The craft is identifying the real benefit, which is almost never grand. People act to avoid blame, avoid rework, look good to someone specific, finish early, or avoid a meeting. Naming one of those accurately outperforms any appeal to strategy, because those are the reasons people actually have.`,`There is an ethical line worth holding. Framing is honest when you are surfacing the trade that was always implicit; it becomes manipulation when you invent a benefit they do not hold. "This will be great for your development" said to someone who wants to leave at six is transparent and slightly insulting.`,`And when you cannot find their benefit, ask: "what would make this easier for you?" The answer is usually the exact framing you needed, delivered for free — and asking also signals that you view them as a person with constraints rather than a function.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Can you review this before Friday?`},{who:`Them`,line:`It's tight. I've got three things landing Thursday.`},{who:`You`,line:`What if I take the client call on Monday? Then a Friday review means you're not in it.`},{who:`Them`,line:`Then yes. Send it over.`}],failureModes:[`Explaining your urgency, which transfers a cost rather than offering a benefit.`,`Inventing a benefit they do not want, which is detectable and corrodes trust.`,`Framing generally ("this will help the team") instead of specifically (no Monday firefighting).`],drill:[`Rewrite three real requests in terms of their gain rather than your need.`,`Name one specific thing they care about for each: blame, rework, visibility, time.`,`Then ask one of them today and measure the response speed.`],related:[`lib-g7`,`lib-s6`,`lib-c2`,`lib-c9`]},"lib-c8":{thesis:`Show it, don't describe it. A demonstration beats a claim, and images outlive adjectives.`,deep:[`Comprehension is easier than belief and both are easier with a demonstration. When you describe a problem, the listener has to construct it; when you show it, they simply see it. That reduction in effort is why a single vivid case outperforms a page of statistics in almost every setting where you need someone to feel something rather than merely agree.`,`The craft is scale: demonstrate small and fast. A physical object, a screenshot, a two-sentence re-enactment, one number written on a whiteboard. Demonstrations fail when they become performances — a five-minute skit is a claim wearing a costume. The best examples take under twenty seconds and produce one clear reaction.`,`The second craft point is selection. Demonstrate the thing that makes the problem feel real, not the thing that is easiest to show. A dashboard is impressive and abstract; the actual customer email is small and devastating. Choose the artefact with the emotional payload, which is usually the smaller one.`,`This applies to your own credibility too. Instead of describing yourself as careful, show the checklist. Instead of claiming you are organised, show the three-line plan. The general principle: whenever you find yourself about to use an adjective about yourself or your work, replace it with an artefact.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`You know how the status update takes two days a month?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Roughly, yes.`},{who:`You`,line:`Here's last month's. [shows the document] Four pages. And here's the client email that contradicts it.`},{who:`Them`,line:`...I didn't know that had gone out.`}],failureModes:[`Demonstrating at the wrong scale — a five-minute skit where a twenty-second artefact would do.`,`Showing the easy artefact rather than the emotionally real one.`,`Describing yourself with adjectives when an artefact was available.`],drill:[`Take one claim you make often and find the artefact that proves it.`,`In a real conversation, demonstrate instead of describing — under twenty seconds.`,`Replace one adjective about your own work with a concrete example this week.`],related:[`lib-g4`,`lib-s4`,`lib-s2`]},"lib-v1":{thesis:`Pitch, pace, pause, projection, tone — five dials, one default. Most speakers never move them deliberately.`,deep:[`Delivery is a mixing desk. Before any sentence that matters, you can choose to lower the pitch, slow the pace, add a silence, raise the projection or change the tone — and one deliberate move is enough to make that sentence land. The reason most speakers sound flat is not a lack of ability but a lack of decisions: they speak at whatever setting their nerves happen to produce.`,`The dials are not equal. Pause is the strongest and least used: silence before an important line creates anticipation, and anticipation is attention. Pace is second, because a contrast between fast and slow does more for comprehension than uniformly good clarity. Pitch and projection matter, but they are amplifiers; tone is the emotional colour that decides how the words are received.`,`The discipline is one dial per sentence. Speakers who discover this technique often move all five at once, which reads as theatrical and slightly desperate. Choose the single dial the sentence needs, use it, then return to baseline — variation is only perceptible against a norm.`,`Train it as a habit rather than a performance: before each of your three most important sentences today, pick a dial and move it. Within a week the choice becomes semi-automatic, and you will hear the difference in your own recordings before anyone comments on it.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Flat: "We didn't hit the target this quarter and I want to talk about why."`},{who:`You`,line:`Dialled (pace −30%, pitch down slightly): "We didn't hit the target this quarter. — pause — I want to talk about why."`},{who:`You`,line:`The words are identical. The second version buys the room's attention because the tempo announced that something serious was arriving.`}],failureModes:[`Moving all five dials at once, which reads as performance rather than intention.`,`Using volume as the default dial, which is the least interesting and most tiring option.`,`Never returning to baseline, so the variation stops being perceptible.`],drill:[`Say one sentence five times, each time moving exactly one dial.`,`Record the version that sounds most like you meaning it.`,`Then pick one dial before each important sentence in a real conversation today.`],related:[`lib-v4`,`lib-f5`,`lib-v2`]},"lib-v2":{thesis:`Clarity comes from space in the mouth, not force in the throat. Articulation is geometry.`,deep:[`Mumbling is a space problem. Consonants are made by interrupting airflow with lips, tongue and jaw, and when the jaw is half-closed and the tongue rests low, those interruptions blur into a soup of vowels. The fix is mechanical and immediate: drop the jaw more than feels natural and let air fill the chamber before you speak.`,`Force is the wrong instinct and it is nearly universal. Pushing produces louder mumbling, because tension in the throat does not create space in the mouth. The test is simple: if your throat is tired after ten minutes, you were forcing. If your ribs and back are tired, you were supporting correctly.`,`The training method is deliberate overshoot. Articulate at 130% of normal for two minutes — exaggerated mouth shapes, visible consonants, looking absurd — then return to normal and observe that normal has become noticeably crisper. You have raised the ceiling rather than learning a new setting.`,`Where this pays off most is names, numbers and technical terms, because those are the words a listener cannot reconstruct from context. If your articulation is vague at the edges of your vocabulary, you will be described as unclear even when everything else is excellent.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Teeth almost together: "red leather, yellow leather" — unrecognisable.`},{who:`You`,line:`Jaw dropped two fingers, one breath in: "red leather, yellow leather" — louder without effort, and recognisable.`},{who:`You`,line:`Nothing about the throat changed. Only the geometry of the mouth.`}],failureModes:[`Pushing harder when misunderstood, which increases volume and decreases clarity.`,`Practising at conversational speed, which rehearses the problem rather than the fix.`,`Articulating only when nervous, so the habit never becomes available when relaxed.`],drill:[`Run three twisters with a deliberately exaggerated jaw, twice through each.`,`Record one at normal speed and score it, noting the exact cluster that failed.`,`Do a two-minute exaggerated warm-up before anything that matters today.`],related:[`lib-v1`,`lib-v3`,`lib-v5`]},"lib-v3":{thesis:`Think in five to eight word phrases. Verse gives your voice a floor to stand on, and prevents both rambling and running out of air.`,deep:[`Long sentences create two failures at once: you run out of breath and you lose your own structure. Verse — speaking in short chunks with a breath at each boundary — solves both, because it converts a sentence into a sequence of deliverable units. Nothing about the content changes; only the amount of it that arrives per breath.`,`The mechanism is double. Physically, five to eight words is what a supported breath comfortably carries, so the voice never has to push. Cognitively, each chunk gives the listener a place to agree with you before the next clause arrives, which is why verse sounds considered rather than fragmented — provided each chunk carries a distinct idea.`,`The editing move is to split at every conjunction. Take a sentence with "because", "but", "which" and "so" inside it and give each clause its own full stop and breath. What was a paragraph becomes four lines, and the four lines are dramatically easier to follow and to remember.`,`The trap is monotony: verse delivered at a constant pace with identical pitch is a shopping list. Chunking must be paired with variation — the most important chunk gets the longer pause before it and the downward landing on its final word.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Prose: "I used to think charisma was something you were born with or not, which bothered me because I'm not loud and I'm not quick with jokes, so I assumed the door was closed."`},{who:`You`,line:`Verse: "I used to think charisma was genetic. / I'm not naturally loud. / I'm not quick with jokes. / So I assumed the door was closed. / I was wrong, and the reason is specific."`}],failureModes:[`Chunking without variation, which produces a robotic list rather than considered speech.`,`Chunks that contain two ideas, which defeats the purpose of the boundary.`,`Using verse in writing and reverting to prose when speaking, which learns the wrong skill.`],drill:[`Answer any prompt in five chunks of six words or fewer, breathing at each boundary.`,`Mark which chunk is most important and give it the longest pause before it.`,`Record it and compare the structure and clarity scores with your usual delivery.`],related:[`lib-v1`,`lib-f7`,`lib-v5`]},"lib-v4":{thesis:`End statements downward. Uptalk converts a claim into a request for approval.`,deep:[`A rising final word on a declarative sentence is not a flaw in the voice — it is a social habit, and it does real work. It signals openness, and in casual conversation it reads as friendly. In any context where you are making a claim, quoting a number, or stating a position, it signals that the claim is provisional, and listeners treat provisional claims as negotiable.`,`The fix is narrow and mechanical: the last two words of statements, and only statements. Land the final stressed syllable downward and the same sentence becomes a decision rather than a suggestion. You are not flattening your melody, and you are not touching questions — genuine questions must still rise, or you will sound like a person issuing verdicts.`,`The contexts where uptalk clusters are exactly where credibility matters: talking to people more senior, stating opinions in groups, and saying numbers. This is why the habit is worth targeted work — it costs you the most in the rooms where it is hardest to notice you are doing it.`,`One test that works in practice: record five statements and five questions. If you cannot tell which is which with the sound only, your endings are not yet doing their job.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Rising: "So the timeline is six weeks and we'd need two more reviewers?" — now it is a question nobody asked.`},{who:`You`,line:`Falling: "The timeline is six weeks. We'd need two more reviewers." — now it is a position.`},{who:`You`,line:`The second version is not aggressive. It is merely finished.`}],failureModes:[`Over-correcting into a clipped, permanently downward delivery that sounds military.`,`Applying it to questions, which turns curiosity into interrogation.`,`Ignoring numbers, which are the words most damaged by a rising ending.`],drill:[`Make ten statements out loud, landing each final word downward.`,`Record five statements and five questions and test whether they are distinguishable.`,`Use one firm statement in a meeting and notice whether anyone reopens the decision.`],related:[`lib-v1`,`lib-g3`,`lib-v2`]},"lib-v5":{thesis:`Walk, stop, breathe, speak. Four seconds of deliberate stillness buys four minutes of attention.`,deep:[`Most speakers start talking before they arrive: mid-entrance, mid-adjustment, mid-apology. The room's first impression is therefore of someone slightly out of control, and subsequent polish has to overcome it. A start ritual inverts that — you arrive, stop, breathe visibly, look up, and only then speak.`,`The silence is the active ingredient. Four seconds of stillness tells the room that you are not in a hurry, which is the single most reliable signal of status available in public speaking. It also gives your voice time to settle onto supported air, so the first sentence arrives low and steady rather than high and rushed.`,`The ritual is physical rather than verbal, which matters under nerves: it does not depend on remembering anything clever. Stop moving, exhale, look up. Three actions that work even when your mind is blank, and which together produce the appearance of composure — and, because posture influences state, a good deal of the substance of it.`,`Apply it beyond the stage: before a difficult conversation, before a video call, before speaking first in a meeting. The same four seconds works everywhere, and it is the cheapest available upgrade to how seriously you are taken.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Unritualised: "Okay so, hi, sorry, can everyone hear me, right, so the thing I want to talk about is—"`},{who:`You`,line:`Ritualised: [walk, stop, breathe, look up] "Three weeks ago we lost a client because of one sentence in an email. I'm going to show you the sentence."`}],failureModes:[`Filling the silence with "right, so" — the ritual only works if it is actually silent.`,`Speaking while still moving, which splits the audience's attention.`,`Apologising for the technology or the delay, which spends the attention the ritual just bought.`],drill:[`Rehearse a three-minute talk with the full ritual and record it.`,`Check that the first four seconds contained no words at all.`,`Use the same four seconds before a real conversation today, and notice your own state change.`],related:[`lib-v1`,`lib-f5`,`lib-g3`]},"lib-v6":{thesis:`Around sixty to seventy percent eye contact reads as confident. Under forty reads as evasive; constant locked gaze reads as threat.`,deep:[`Eye contact has an inverted-U relationship with trust, and knowing the shape is more useful than trying to be fearless about it. Too little reads as shame or evasion; too much reads as dominance or challenge. The comfortable band, in most Western professional settings, is roughly sixty to seventy percent while speaking and somewhat more while listening.`,`The technique that makes the band achievable is the eye triangle: move your gaze between the two eyes and the mouth rather than fixing on one point. This keeps contact continuous without the unblinking intensity that raises stress, and it gives you somewhere natural to look — which eliminates the darting that the fear of staring produces.`,`Break gaze for thought, not for escape. Looking away while constructing a sentence is read as consideration; looking away while being asked something difficult is read as evasion. Same act, opposite meaning, decided by whether it happens after the question or during it.`,`In groups, distribute: the speaker gets most of your gaze, the room gets some. Fixing exclusively on the senior person is one of the most common errors in meetings, because everyone else sees exactly what you did and draws conclusions about your motives.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`"What happened with the Henderson account?" [hold contact through the question]`},{who:`You`,line:`[look away for two seconds while assembling the chronology]`},{who:`You`,line:`"It slipped by four days. Here's where it went wrong." [return to contact]`}],failureModes:[`Constant unbroken gaze in the belief that it signals confidence, when it usually raises the other person's stress.`,`Looking away during the question rather than during the thinking, which reads as evasion.`,`Fixing on the most senior person in a group and ignoring everyone else.`],drill:[`Practise the eye triangle in three conversations.`,`Hold contact through questions and break only while thinking.`,`In one meeting, give your gaze to whoever is speaking.`],related:[`lib-v5`,`lib-c3`,`lib-v1`]},"lib-w2":{thesis:`The laugh lives at the end of the sentence. Rearrange until the funniest word arrives last and nothing follows it.`,deep:[`This is the single most mechanical improvement available in humour. The same content, with the funniest word moved to the end, is funnier — because the laugh needs a place to land and the end of the sentence is the only place that has no competition. Most failed jokes are structurally sound and terminally ordered.`,`The second half of the rule is the harder half: nothing follows the punch word. Amateurs add a tag — an explanation, a softening laugh, a "sorry" — and each addition gives the listener permission to downgrade their reaction. Say the funny thing last, then stop, and let the silence be the laugh.`,`The editing process is subtractive and fast. Take a sentence you actually said, find the funniest word, move it to the end, then delete every word that is not load-bearing: the setup clause that explains why you are joking, the qualifier that softens it, the self-conscious aside. The result is usually half the length and reliably sharper.`,`One exception worth knowing: in stories, the punch word is often an image rather than a joke word, and moving it to the end has the same effect — the final image becomes the thing the listener keeps, which is why good endings and good punchlines are the same craft.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Bloated: "I got there late, well not that late, and everyone was already sitting down which was a bit awkward honestly."`},{who:`You`,line:`Compressed: "I arrived late and everyone was already seated — which is the only situation where walking in is a public event."`},{who:`You`,line:`"Public event" is the punch word, and nothing follows it.`}],failureModes:[`Adding a tag after the punchline — a laugh, an explanation, or "sorry" — which refunds the laugh.`,`Burying the funny word mid-sentence because it is grammatically tidier.`,`Explaining the joke when it dies, which is the one recovery that never works.`],drill:[`Take five sentences you said today and move the funniest word to the end of each.`,`Delete every clause that exists to explain or soften.`,`Deliver them with a flat face and keep the two that still work.`],related:[`lib-w1`,`lib-w3`,`lib-w5`]},"lib-w3":{thesis:`Punch yourself, not your listener. Low status on purpose is a high-status move — once, small, and aimed inward.`,deep:[`Self-deprecation is a status instrument rather than a personality trait. By lowering your own position before anyone else does, you remove the need for competition and demonstrate that your self-image is not fragile. The audience conclusion is not "this person is weak" but "this person is safe and secure", which are the same thing in most rooms.`,`The three constraints are what separate the working version from the one that makes people wince. *Inward*: the joke targets your choice or behaviour, never your listener's. *Small*: a real but minor flaw, specific enough to be recognisable. *Once*: repeated self-deprecation stops reading as confidence and starts reading as a request for validation.`,`Placement multiplies the effect. Immediately after praise, a self-jab prevents the compliment becoming an obligation. Immediately after an attack, it removes the status question so the conversation can proceed. In both positions you spend a little status and buy a lot of ease — which is the best exchange rate in social life.`,`The strongest constraint is what never to use. Do not self-deprecate about something you are genuinely insecure about, because you will over-perform it and the room will hear the wound underneath. The material must be a flaw you would happily be teased about by someone who likes you.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`You're late again.`},{who:`You`,line:`Guilty. I was up all night rehearsing a better excuse and this is the best I got.`},{who:`Them`,line:`That's genuinely worse than no excuse.`},{who:`You`,line:`It's a gift.`}],failureModes:[`Humblebragging — "I'm such a perfectionist" — which is a compliment in a costume and listeners detect it instantly.`,`Using self-deprecation before you have any credibility in a hostile room, which invites more criticism.`,`Repetition, which converts confidence into a request for reassurance.`],drill:[`Answer three high-status teases with warmth plus one small self-jab, under thirty words each.`,`Keep the warmth score above 65 and check that no jab points outward.`,`Use one in real life today and notice the absence of a second hit.`],related:[`lib-w2`,`lib-c2`,`lib-w4`]},"lib-w4":{thesis:`Treat the jab as high praise and it has nowhere to land. The room watches you hold the frame.`,deep:[`Misattributing an insult as a compliment is the most elegant defensive move in the repertoire, because it does not fight. Fighting accepts that status is at stake; reframing as praise declares that you did not experience an attack at all. The attacker is left either repeating themselves — which looks petty — or joining the new frame.`,`The structure is three moves: take the sting literally, reinterpret it generously, and hand back something the other person wants. "You're very competitive" becomes "Thank you, I try to be — on your behalf." You have accepted the word, given it a flattering meaning, and implied a service, and there is nothing left to attack.`,`The delivery is the whole trick. It must be warm, unhurried and free of any wince. A flip delivered with tension in the face tells the room the insult landed, which returns the status to the attacker and makes the line look like a recovery rather than a choice. If you cannot deliver it calmly, do not attempt it.`,`The practical preparation is to have three flips ready before you need them: one for a competence jab, one for a status jab, and one for a personal jab. Fluency here is retrieval, not invention — nobody constructs these in real time, which is why two seconds of breath before replying is more valuable than any amount of quick thinking.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`So you're the one who does the little side project? How cute that you have hobbies.`},{who:`You`,line:`It's adorable, I agree. I keep it small so it can't disappoint me publicly. What's your version of that?`},{who:`Them`,line:`[pause] ...I don't have one, actually.`},{who:`You`,line:`Then I'm winning, obviously.`}],failureModes:[`Delivering the flip with a nervous smile, which reveals the insult landed.`,`Flipping into a counter-insult, which converts a frame-hold into a fight.`,`Attempting it after you have already replied defensively — the moment has passed and it now looks like a second attempt.`],drill:[`Wit Simulator: three hostile prompts, each answered with a one-line flip.`,`Prepare one flip each for competence, status and personal jabs and say them out loud.`,`Practise the two-second breath before replying; the flip depends on it.`],related:[`lib-g4`,`lib-w3`,`lib-w1`]},"lib-w5":{thesis:`The first laugh already did the work. Two or three words of reference is enough, and the shared language is worth more than the joke.`,deep:[`A callback is the only form of humour whose setup has been paid for in advance. The room has already reacted to the earlier moment, so a three-word reference can produce a disproportionate laugh — but the laugh is the lesser benefit. The greater one is that callbacks create private language, and private language is the strongest available marker of belonging.`,`Three rules keep it reliable. *Short*: two or three words, never a recap. *Pointed at a moment*: if the original moment was someone's embarrassment, the callback is a reminder rather than a joke. *Delayed*: it must arrive after the conversation has moved on, or it is merely continuation.`,`The advanced form is the chain — the same reference used two or three times across an evening, each instance shorter than the last, until the final one is a single word or a gesture. At that point the group owns something together, which is a social outcome far more valuable than any individual laugh.`,`The defensive use is worth having in conflict: a callback to a moment where you and the other person were aligned resets the emotional baseline without argument. You are not making a point — you are reminding both nervous systems that you have been on the same side before, and that reminder is more persuasive than any case you could build.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`[twenty minutes earlier] ...and that's when the goat got into the car.`},{who:`You`,line:`[later] So Portugal in June — no goats, ideally.`},{who:`You`,line:`[later still, as someone leaves] Watch the gate.`}],failureModes:[`Explaining the reference, which destroys the pleasure of recognition and marks you as someone keeping score.`,`Using a callback to someone else's embarrassment, which is a reminder rather than a joke.`,`Referencing something that got no reaction the first time — there is nothing to borrow.`],drill:[`Wit Simulator: run three Callback scenarios and keep every reference under ten words.`,`In a real conversation, use one detail twice — once early, once after the topic has moved.`,`Attempt the chain: three references to the same moment across one evening.`],related:[`lib-w1`,`lib-w2`,`lib-c4`]}},a=e({LIBRARY_DEEP:()=>o}),o={"lib-f1":{thesis:`A blank mind is not a knowledge problem, it is a sequencing problem. You have plenty to say; you simply cannot find the entrance while four people watch. The buy-time question buys the entrance.`,deep:[`When a question catches you cold, your working memory collapses to roughly one slot. You are simultaneously trying to work out what the asker means, whether you are qualified to answer, and how to begin — and those three tasks compete for the same tiny space. That is why the answer that eventually arrives is usually worse than the one you would have given five seconds later, alone in a corridor. The blankness is not ignorance. It is contention.`,`The fix is not to think faster, it is to stop pretending you have already thought. Ask a question back: "What made you ask that?" or "Is this about the launch or the budget?" You have now done four things at once. You have bought eight to twelve seconds. You have found out what they actually want. You have flattered them by treating the question as worth clarifying. And you have converted a performance into a conversation, which is a far easier state to think in.`,`The subtlety is in the tone. A deflecting question sounds like evasion; a curious question sounds like care. The difference is whether you look interested or cornered when you ask it — so keep your eyes on them, slow down, and let the question sound like it matters to you. If it does not sound like it matters, do not ask it; use the naming version instead: "Let me give you the honest version — I've got three threads here and I want to pick the right one."`,`Then answer with a structure, not with your whole knowledge. Point, reason, example, point. Four beats, ninety seconds, done. The buy-time question has bought you the time to choose a structure; spend it on structure rather than on trying to remember a better fact.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`So where do you see this going in the next two years?`},{who:`You`,line:`Which part — the product, or the team?`},{who:`Them`,line:`The team, mostly. I'm worried we're hiring for the current shape of the company.`},{who:`You`,line:`Then here's my honest read. Point: we're about to hire two people for a company that won't exist in eight months. Reason: everything we've built this year has been a bet that hasn't resolved yet. Example: we hired a generalist in March to run support and she's now effectively our product manager. Point: so I'd rather hire one person who can be re-shaped than two who fit today.`}],failureModes:[`Asking it every time. Used repeatedly, the buy-time question stops being curiosity and becomes a tic. Once or twice in a conversation, at genuine moments of ambiguity.`,`Asking and then not listening. If they answer and you steam ahead with a prepared point, you have exposed the question as a stall. Their answer must visibly shape your reply.`,`Using it on a hostile question. In a hostile setting, a question back can read as evasive. There, name the difficulty first: "That deserves a better answer than my first one."`],drill:[`In the Arena, take one turn where you deliberately reply to the persona with a clarifying question instead of an answer.`,`Notice whether their next disclosure is more useful than anything you would have guessed.`,`Then in an Impromptu recording, deliberately open with two seconds of silence and a single clarifying sentence before your point.`],related:[`lib-f2`,`lib-f7`,`lib-c5`]},"lib-f3":{thesis:`Three follow-ups before one opinion is the cheapest way to be remembered as fascinating while doing almost none of the talking — and it is not a trick, because curiosity genuinely does produce better conversations.`,deep:[`Most conversations are two people taking turns to be interesting. That is why they feel like work. The Triple Follow-Up breaks the turn-taking pattern: instead of responding with your own story, you go one level deeper on theirs. "What happened next?" — "How did that feel at the time?" — "What did you do about it?" Three questions, no story from you, and the other person will leave describing you as easy to talk to.`,`The reason this works is depth rather than breadth. A single question gets you the headline; three questions get you the person. Most people move on to a new topic out of politeness, afraid of prying. In practice the opposite is true: people are usually waiting for someone to ask the second question, because almost nobody does. Being the person who asks the second question is a genuine social advantage and it costs you nothing.`,`The third question does specific work. The first two are about events and feelings; the third is about agency — what did you do? It moves the conversation from report to reflection, and that is the level at which people say things they actually mean. A conversation that never reaches the third question feels pleasant and forgettable, like small talk with better vocabulary.`,`One constraint keeps this from turning into an interrogation: you must offer something small in return. A brief recognition — "that sounds exhausting" — or a one-line admission of your own, placed between questions, keeps the exchange reciprocal. Three questions and no self-disclosure is an interview; three questions with a small offering is intimacy.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`I just moved here from Manchester. Bit of a shock, honestly.`},{who:`You`,line:`What's been the biggest shock?`},{who:`Them`,line:`The noise. And nobody says hello on the street.`},{who:`You`,line:`That would get to me — I'm from a village where everyone's in your business. How have you been handling it?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Badly, at first. Then I started running at six in the morning because the city's empty then.`},{who:`You`,line:`And has that changed how you feel about living here, or just how you cope with it?`},{who:`Them`,line:`...That's a good question. Actually it's changed it. I know the place now.`}],failureModes:[`Rapid-fire questions with no reciprocity. That is an interview, and the other person will start giving shorter answers to make it stop.`,`Asking a follow-up you do not care about. Insincerity is detectable in the follow-up's specificity: "how did that feel?" asked flatly lands as form-filling.`,`Skipping the third question. Without the agency question you never get past reporting, and reporting is not memorable.`],drill:[`In the Arena, run a conversation where you ask three follow-ups before offering any opinion or anecdote of your own.`,`Check the curiosity score in the summary — this drill should push it above 75.`,`In real life, do it once today with someone you normally exchange logistics with.`],related:[`lib-c3`,`lib-c5`,`lib-f4`]},"lib-f4":{thesis:`Everything someone says contains free information they did not know they were offering. Conversations die when you step over the offering to find a more interesting topic.`,deep:[`The first sentence anyone says in a new conversation is dense with usable material, and almost all of it gets discarded. "I just came from the gym" offers: the gym, the time of day, the habit, the body, the reason they are out afterwards. "I work in logistics" offers: the industry, the daily frustration, why they chose it, what they would rather do. The material for forty minutes is already in the room. You do not need a better topic; you need to stop leaving the offered material on the floor.`,`The technique is a simple rule: when someone gives you free information, go three levels deeper before changing subject. Gym → which gym → what do you do there → how long have you gone there. Each level is a question, and each question is answered with more free information for the next level. This is why some people can talk to anyone for an hour without an interesting topic and others die after ninety seconds of real conversation.`,`The key move most people miss is using a *detail* rather than a *category*. "The gym, nice" is a category and it kills the thread. "The gym at eight in the morning — is that a discipline thing or a sleep thing?" is a detail forked into two options, either of which is a genuine answer. Forking also helps because it refuses the yes/no trap: give people a choice between two interests and they will pick one and explain themselves.`,`And then the exit is graceful, because you can leave the thread open rather than changing topic abruptly: "I want to come back to the gym thing, but first — you said you were about to move." Nothing you were given is wasted, and nothing is dropped awkwardly.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`Long day. I just came from the gym, actually.`},{who:`You`,line:`At this hour? Is that discipline or is it the only slot you can get?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Only slot. I've got two kids, so it's the gym or nothing.`},{who:`You`,line:`Two kids and you're here. What does the gym actually give you — is it the body or the hour of silence?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Honestly? The silence. The body's a side effect.`},{who:`You`,line:`That's the most honest reason I've heard tonight. What happens to you on the weeks you miss it?`}],failureModes:[`Answering instead of asking. Every time you respond to free information with a fact about yourself, you have closed the thread.`,`Changing subject too early. Three levels is the minimum depth at which a thread starts producing anything worth remembering.`,`Asking category questions ("what do you do?"). They produce one-word answers and one-word answers are conversation graveyards.`],drill:[`In the Arena, find the free information in the persona's first line and go three levels deeper before anything else.`,`Write down which level produced the most interesting disclosure.`,`Next real conversation: take the first thing someone offers and go three questions deep, offering one small thing of your own between questions.`],related:[`lib-f3`,`lib-c3`,`lib-v6`]},"lib-f6":{thesis:`How you leave is remembered longer than how you arrived. A warm exit makes you a person with options; a slow fade makes you a person who could not find one.`,deep:[`Exits are the most neglected part of social skill, and the most diagnostic. Most people handle them one of two ways. They fade — hovering at the edge of a group, contributing less, drifting until the conversation dies around them. Or they escape — seizing on any excuse and vanishing, leaving the other person mid-sentence. Both produce the same residue: a slight awkwardness that outlasts everything said before it.`,`The warm exit has three components. First, a *reason that protects the other person*: "I want to let you go before I monopolise you." Note the framing — you are not escaping them, you are being considerate. Second, *evidence you were listening*: name one specific thing you want to follow up. Third, a *concrete next step*, even a tiny one: "I'll send you that article." That converts an ending into a continuation, and continuations are what relationships are made of.`,`Why this matters disproportionately: endings are the peak-end. People do not remember the average quality of a conversation, they remember its most intense moment and its finish. A perfectly fine twenty minutes fading into "well, I should probably circulate" becomes a forgettable encounter, while a mediocre conversation with a crisp, specific exit becomes a name they remember later.`,`In groups, exits need a slightly different move: address the exit to the group and the thank-you to the individual. "I'm going to grab some air — Priya, genuinely, the signposting thing is going to live in my head for a week." You have left the group without making them responsible for your leaving, and you have given one person a specific gift on your way out.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Maya, I'm going to let you go before I monopolise you for the whole evening.`},{who:`Them`,line:`You're fine, honestly.`},{who:`You`,line:`I'm going to anyway, because I want to follow up properly — the thing you said about the market being too patient is going to annoy me all week, in a good way. I'll send you the paper on smallholder sensor longevity, since we're clearly the only two people here who'd read it.`},{who:`Them`,line:`Please do. That's genuinely the first useful thing anyone's said tonight.`},{who:`You`,line:`Then I'll quit while I'm ahead. Good to meet you properly.`}],failureModes:[`The apology exit: "Sorry, I should go, I hope that's okay." Nothing to be sorry for; the apology makes your leaving a cost you are imposing.`,`The unresolved exit: naming a follow-up and never doing it. This is worse than no follow-up, because you have proved your specific attention was a technique.`,`The slow fade. Standing at the edge of a group not speaking is interpreted, by everyone, as waiting for a better option.`],drill:[`In the Arena, end a conversation using an explicit warm exit rather than just stopping.`,`In real life, leave one conversation today with a reason that protects them, one specific detail, and one concrete next step.`,`Write yourself the next-step line so you cannot forget it — and then actually send it.`],related:[`lib-c4`,`lib-f5`,`lib-c3`]},"lib-g1":{thesis:`Displaying your talent too brightly makes the people above you feel insecure, and insecure superiors act against you. The solution is not to be worse — it is to make your contribution feel like their idea.`,deep:[`This law is widely misread as flattery, which is both distasteful and ineffective. Its actual claim is structural: the person above you is measured by their judgement, so an idea that arrives from below without their fingerprints on it is a threat rather than an asset. They must either adopt it and look like they were led, or reject it and look stubborn. Most choose a third way — quiet delay — and your excellent idea dies of scheduling.`,`The remedy is to give them ownership before you give them content. Early, informally, and without an audience: "I've been going in circles on the pricing thing — could I borrow your brain for ten minutes?" You now have a conversation in which the idea emerges jointly, and their fingerprints are on it from the first minute. When it is later proposed in a meeting, they will defend it as their own, because it is.`,`The second half of the law is about visibility. Credit in public, substance in private. When something works, let them narrate it to the room and be visibly comfortable with that. What you get in exchange is something more durable than applause: the reputation, with them, of a person whose work arrives without friction. That reputation is the actual currency of advancement.`,`There is a boundary worth naming. This is not advice to be servile or to hide your competence. You are building a track record that must be legible to everyone else — so be excellent in public, just be *strategically unthreatening*. The distinction: never let a superior be surprised by your ability in front of an audience that matters to them. Surprise them in private instead, where it reads as promise rather than competition.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Do you have ten minutes this week? I keep hitting a wall on the pricing structure and you've done this three times.`},{who:`Boss`,line:`What's the wall?`},{who:`You`,line:`I think we're pricing the wrong thing — we're charging for seats when the value is in the integration hours. But I don't trust my own read on it yet.`},{who:`Boss`,line:`Hm. Send me the numbers and let me think about it.`},{who:`You`,line:`Will do. And if you land on something, I'm happy to build the model and take it to the Tuesday group under your name — you'll get the questions you actually want asked.`}],failureModes:[`Copying them in on a finished deck the night before the meeting. This is the classic outshining move: it presents a fait accompli and forces them to either endorse something they did not shape or delay it.`,`Winning the argument in front of their boss. Even if you are right, you have cost them the most expensive thing they own.`,`Taking this to the point of invisibility. If nobody knows what you did, you have not managed status, you have erased yourself. Be excellent in public; be unthreatening in private.`],drill:[`Find one idea you are currently attached to. Rewrite the pitch as a question, and ask it of the person it would most threaten.`,`Notice whether their version of the idea is better than yours. Frequently it is, which is why the technique survives contact with reality.`,`Then rehearse the "take it under your name" line in the Arena — it is a high-status move disguised as deference.`],related:[`lib-g3`,`lib-c7`,`lib-c6`]},"lib-g2":{thesis:`If people cannot read your intentions, they cannot prepare against them. Ambiguity is not dishonesty — it is the refusal to hand people the script of your next move.`,deep:[`Concealing intentions is the most misunderstood of Greene's laws because it sounds like deception. In practice it is mostly about *sequencing*: not announcing what you want before you understand the terrain. The person who declares their ambition in the first meeting has handed everyone a map of how to stop them, and usually they hand it over before they know who in the room has an interest in doing so.`,`The everyday version is simply asking better questions before answering any. "What's the outcome you're hoping for?" before you state your position. "Who else has a view on this?" before you commit. You are not hiding anything. You are declining to negotiate against yourself before you have seen the other side of the table.`,`The decoy is the sharper tool, and it should be used sparingly and never dishonestly. If a negotiation is going to be about price, let the first conversation be about scope — genuinely, because you need to know it. Your real priority remains unstated until you know whether it is achievable. Ambiguity has a legitimate form: "I want this to work for both of us; I'm not sure yet what the shape is." That is usually true, and it keeps your options open without any lies.`,`Where this becomes genuinely powerful is over months rather than minutes. People build models of you and then act on those models. If your behaviour is always legible in advance — always the same objections, the same eagerness, the same tells — you can be managed. Being slightly less predictable, in ways that are honest, is the closest thing to armour available in an organisation.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`So what's your number? We'd like to get this wrapped up today.`},{who:`You`,line:`Happy to get there. Before I put a number down — is the constraint the total, or the timing?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Timing. Board meets Thursday.`},{who:`You`,line:`Then let me give you two shapes rather than one number. One that lands Thursday at full rate, and one that lands in three weeks at a lower rate. Which is more useful to you?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Thursday, probably. But tell me both.`}],failureModes:[`Using ambiguity as evasion. If the other person asks a direct question and you consistently dodge, you are not being strategic, you are being untrustworthy — and it will be remembered.`,`Playing it so close that nobody can help you. Concealment is about timing, not secrecy with allies. Your team needs to know your intentions.`,`Confusing ambiguity with mystery. Being vague about your own competence is not power, it is camouflage for anxiety.`],drill:[`In the Arena, take two turns where you ask about the persona's constraints before stating your own position.`,`In one real negotiation this week — however small — do not give your number until you know their constraint.`,`Write down what you learned by waiting. The list is usually longer than expected.`],related:[`lib-g3`,`lib-f1`,`lib-g5`]},"lib-g6":{thesis:`People absorb your emotional state before they process your words. Reason delivered in an agitated body registers as agitation, however sound the argument.`,deep:[`This is the single most useful piece of practical psychology in the whole library, and it is mechanical rather than mystical. Nervous systems are coupled through breath, pace, volume and posture — the phenomenon usually called attunement. When two people talk, their heart rates and breathing patterns drift toward each other within minutes. Whoever is more regulated sets the tempo; whoever is faster follows.`,`That means the fastest way to change a room is not to argue better but to breathe slower. In a conflict, deliberately taking one longer exhale, lowering your volume slightly and slowing your speech by twenty percent offers the other person's nervous system a slower rhythm to match. It works far more reliably than reassurance, because reassurance is a claim while a slower rhythm is an offer made directly to the body.`,`The corollary is uncomfortable: you cannot fake regulation. If your own heart rate is elevated, your voice will carry it. Which is why the real training is not conversational technique but a physical reset — one long exhale before you speak, shoulders dropped, weight settled. Do that and you are genuinely calm enough to set the tempo; skip it and your careful words will arrive wrapped in your own adrenaline.`,`Note the asymmetry this creates in groups. In any room, the most regulated person quietly becomes the reference point, regardless of seniority or volume. This is why some quiet people dominate meetings they barely speak in. They are not dominating; they are simply the pace the others have synced to.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`[Fast, loud] I've had this account for six years and nobody has ever sent me something I can't read.`},{who:`You`,line:`[Slower, lower, one full breath out first] You're right to be angry, and I'm not going to argue with the fact. Tell me what it looked like from your side and I'll tell you what I can do before Thursday.`},{who:`Them`,line:`[pace drops] ...My CEO is looking at it right now.`},{who:`You`,line:`Then here's what happens next. I'll send you a corrected version in two hours, and you'll hear from me before it breaks next time — not after.`},{who:`Them`,line:`[quieter] Fine. Same heat, less noise. I appreciate that.`}],failureModes:[`Matching their energy when their energy is anger. Attunement cuts both ways — if you speed up to match, you both accelerate and the conversation becomes a contest of volume.`,`Using calm as a weapon. Slow, low, and patronising is heard as superior and will escalate things faster than shouting would.`,`Trying to reason first. Explanations delivered before regulation arrive as noise; the nervous system has no capacity to process content while it believes it is under attack.`],drill:[`In the Arena, use Marco or Priya. Before each reply, take one full exhale and slow your typing pace deliberately.`,`Track whether the persona's responses shorten and soften over the session.`,`In real conflict today: one long exhale before your first sentence. That is the whole intervention.`],related:[`lib-g4`,`lib-c7`,`lib-c9`]},"lib-c7":{thesis:`A person who loses face is no longer cooperating — they are defending. Leave a dignified interpretation available and you keep both the point and the person.`,deep:[`This is Carnegie's most operationally useful rule and the one most often ignored in professional life, where being right is treated as a virtue independent of its cost. The mechanism: face is not vanity, it is the social version of physical balance. When someone is cornered publicly, their attention leaves the question entirely and goes to restoring their standing. From that moment, no argument you make can be heard, because they are no longer evaluating your evidence — they are managing an audience.`,`The practical move is to construct an exit ramp before you need one. When correcting someone, attach the correction to circumstances rather than to their judgement: "the brief we were given was ambiguous about the deadline" does the same work as "you missed the deadline" while leaving them whole. When someone is wrong in a meeting, route the correction through a question about the process, not about the person: "can we look at how that number was calculated?" Everyone knows what is happening; nobody has been cornered.`,`Public and private is the crucial distinction. Correction in private, credit in public. If a correction must happen in front of others, the framing should make the error ordinary: "this one caught three of us out". You lose nothing — the facts are still on the table — and you gain a person who is now quietly on your side rather than quietly against you.`,`The harder version is with people who are behaving badly. Even there, the rule holds: describe the behaviour in terms that allow them to change without humiliation, and most will take the exit. "I think that came out harder than you meant" is a gift. "You were aggressive in there" closes the door and they will spend the rest of the week explaining themselves to other people.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`[in front of the team] So who signed off on this? Because it's wrong.`},{who:`You`,line:`That's on me — I approved it, and the spec we were working from was ambiguous about the second phase. Can we take ten minutes after this and fix both the document and the error?`},{who:`Them`,line:`...Fine. After the meeting.`},{who:`You`,line:`[later, privately] For what it's worth, you were right to stop it in there. And next time I'll bring you the draft before it goes anywhere.`}],failureModes:[`Letting them save face by blaming someone else. The exit ramp must not require a scapegoat — you have merely moved the cornering downstairs.`,`Saving face so thoroughly that the problem disappears. The point still has to land; keep the facts, soften only the attribution.`,`Public correction of a person who is junior to you. This is the most expensive version available and it teaches the whole room to be careful around you.`],drill:[`In the Arena, escape a confrontation with Priya or Marco without either side losing. Note which of your lines worked.`,`Then recall the last time you corrected someone in public. Write the face-saving version of that sentence.`,`Use one this week, in private or public, and watch how quickly cooperation returns.`],related:[`lib-c1`,`lib-c9`,`lib-g6`]},"lib-c9":{thesis:`You can win the argument or you can win the person. Almost never both — and the argument is worth much less than you think.`,deep:[`When you lose an argument, you lose. When you win an argument, you have made the other person feel inferior, hurt their pride, and made them resent your victory — and they will leave holding their original position more tightly than before. This is not a rhetorical flourish; it is the well-documented backfire effect. Direct contradiction produces defensiveness, and defensiveness produces entrenchment.`,`The practical alternative is to make being wrong cheap. "I may be wrong about this, but I think..." is derided as weak, yet it is one of the strongest available moves, because it removes the status threat that makes people defend positions they do not even hold strongly. "Let me see if I've got this right" is the same move in a different costume. Both let the other person walk away from an old position without losing anything.`,`There is a discipline to this: you must be genuinely willing to be the one who is wrong. If your humility is a tactic and they sense it, it becomes the most annoying thing in the room. The way to make it real is to ask a question you do not know the answer to, and to say so: "I don't actually know how the client's budget works. Can you help me understand it?" That is not a technique; it is what curiosity looks like when it is not performing.`,`Where this goes wrong is when people mistake it for passivity. This rule is about arguments — conversations whose real purpose is establishing who is right. If the decision actually matters and the evidence actually exists, do not argue: demonstrate. Show the number, run the test, produce the example. Actions settle what argument only entrenches.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`Remote work has completely destroyed collaboration here. I've seen it.`},{who:`You`,line:`I might be seeing a different slice of it — can I ask what changed for your team specifically?`},{who:`Them`,line:`The handovers. Nobody knows what anyone's doing on a Tuesday.`},{who:`You`,line:`That's a real problem, and it's not one I'd argue with. What would tell you it had been fixed — less than a week of lag, or something else?`},{who:`Them`,line:`Under a week. Honestly, if handovers were clean I wouldn't care where anyone sits.`}],failureModes:[`Correcting the fact while ignoring the feeling. People argue from experience, not from data; being told your experience is unrepresentative is heard as being told you are stupid.`,`Winning small arguments for sport. Every easy victory you take in a low-stakes conversation spends a balance you will need in a high-stakes one.`,`Using "I may be wrong" as a rhetorical device. It works only when you are actually prepared to be.`],drill:[`In the Arena, find one turn where the persona is wrong and do not correct them — ask a question instead.`,`Note whether their position softened or hardened. Write down the sentence that achieved it.`,`In real life, let one small factual error pass this week. Notice that nothing happened.`],related:[`lib-c7`,`lib-g4`,`lib-g6`]},"lib-w1":{thesis:`Never block the reality someone offers you. Accept it, then add one thing. Blocking kills the scene; accepting an absurdity and escalating it is what makes you the funniest person in it.`,deep:[`Yes, and is an improvisation rule that doubles as a social one. Blocking is any move that corrects the premise of what someone just offered: "no, that's not what happened", "that's not funny", "well, actually". Every block ends a scene, because the other person now has to defend their premise instead of building on it. In comedy this is fatal; in a conversation it is merely dull, which is worse.`,`The mechanic works because humour is generative rather than competitive. If someone says the holiday was a hostage negotiation, an agreeing move accepts the metaphor and extends it — "my last one got two people to leave peacefully" — and now there are two jokes where there was one. A blocking move ("it wasn't that bad") produces zero. This is why the funniest person in a group is usually the one who is easiest to be funny near.`,`Yes-and has a limit that improvisers know and most conversationalists do not: you accept the premise, not the *claim*. If someone says something cruel about a third person, yes-anding the cruelty makes you complicit rather than funny. The safe version accepts the absurdity and adds yourself to it, or turns the joke toward the situation. Agree with the frame; redirect the target.`,`Practically, the move is one sentence: accept, then add a specific. "Agreed — and now it also does X." The specificity is what makes it funny; vague extensions ("yeah, and it's even worse") add nothing because they contain no new image. One concrete absurd detail beats three generic agreements.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`Honestly, your playlist sounds like a hostage negotiation.`},{who:`You`,line:`Hostage negotiation is generous. My last one got two people to leave peacefully.`},{who:`Them`,line:`[laughs] What's the next track, a distant apology?`},{who:`You`,line:`Track seven is forty minutes of rain and a man admitting he was wrong. Nobody's made it to track eight.`}],failureModes:[`Blocking to be accurate. "It wasn't a hostage negotiation, it was just quiet" is true, unhelpful, and ends the fun for everyone.`,`Yes-anding something cruel about an absent person. You have accepted the wrong premise; now you are the source of the cruelty rather than the joke.`,`Adding nothing specific. "Yeah, and worse!" is agreement without content, and content is the whole mechanism.`],drill:[`In the Wit Simulator, take three Banter scenarios and respond with an accept-plus-specific for each.`,`Check the humour score — this drill should raise it while keeping warmth above 60.`,`In one real conversation today, accept somebody's exaggeration instead of correcting it, and add one concrete absurd detail.`],related:[`lib-w2`,`lib-w3`,`lib-w4`]}},s=e({LIBRARY_DEPTH_7:()=>c}),c={"lib-st3":[`The rhythm fails in a specific way with people who are anxious: two questions then a disclosure can read as a script being run at them. The repair is to make the disclosure cost you something small. "I moved here two years ago and I still do not know where to get good bread" is a confession, and a confession invites one back. A neutral fact invites nothing, and the conversation returns to the interview it was trying to escape.`],"lib-st4":[`The test of a three-beat exchange is not whether it was interesting, it is whether the other person had to carry it. If you left and cannot remember one thing they said, you were not in a conversation, you were broadcasting with an audience. The next rep: say your three beats, then ask a question that requires them to have an opinion rather than a fact, and count how many words they use.`],"lib-st5":[`Small stories fail when the teller makes them mean something. "And that is why I always check the receipt" turns a story into a lesson, and a lesson is a small status move. End on the image instead — the receipt, the queue, the wrong train — and let the other person decide what it was about. The story that gets retold is always the one that left room.`],"lib-wm2":[`The specific compliment has one failure mode and it is not the obvious one. It is not creepiness; it is accuracy. If the thing you praise is something she did not choose — her voice, her height, the way she looks in a photograph — you have proven attention to something she had no hand in, which reads as surveillance rather than admiration. Praise the choice, the craft, the decision. Those are things a person can be proud of, and pride is a far better place to be complimented from than defensiveness.`],"lib-wm3":[`Reading interest is the place where confidence and self-deception look identical from the inside. The honest test is not whether she laughed at your joke — people laugh to be kind — it is whether she adds something you did not hand her. Volunteering, extending, asking back: those are investments, and investment is the only signal that costs the other person anything. Everything else is manners, and manners are not evidence.`],"lib-wm4":[`A gracious no is not a soft no. The warmth lives in the tone and the exit, never in the content — the content has to stay unambiguous or it becomes a delay rather than an answer. "I am not going to be able to do that" plus a genuine warm note about the person is a no. Add a reason and you have opened a negotiation; add a maybe and you have moved the work of the no onto them, which is the opposite of gracious.`],"lib-dc2":[`The request is where this collapses in practice, because people phrase it as a feeling and expect it to function as an instruction. "I need to feel respected" cannot be accepted or refused, so it cannot be met. "I need you to tell me by nine if you are not coming" can be accepted, refused, or negotiated — all three of which are progress. If you cannot write your request as a sentence someone could say yes or no to, you have not finished the sentence.`],"lib-dc3":[`Repair works because it separates the event from the meaning. "I raised my voice and that was not fair" describes an event; "I am a terrible person" makes the other person responsible for comforting you, which is a second injury dressed as an apology. The test of a repair is whether the other person has to do any work. If they do, you have made them carry the cost of your guilt as well as the cost of your behaviour.`],"lib-dc4":[`One-job nos fail late, not early. The first refusal is easy; the fifth is where people soften the terms and call it flexibility. The thing that holds is a single sentence said identically, in the same tone, with the same words — because variation is the signal that the position moved. If you catch yourself rephrasing to make it land better, you have started negotiating in the name of kindness.`],"lib-ei1":[`The failure mode of one-rung-deeper is not oversharing, it is skipping the question. Going straight to your own equivalent — "my father was ill too" — feels like matching but is actually a takeover, because it puts the conversation back on your side of the table. The question is the part that keeps it theirs. Ask it first, wait, and only then decide whether your thing belongs in the room.`],"lib-ei2":[`Feel, fact, then fix is the order that works, and almost everyone does it backwards under pressure: they offer the fix first because the feeling is uncomfortable to sit in. But a fix delivered before acknowledgement reads as "your reaction is a problem I would like to solve", which tells the person their emotion is an inconvenience. The order is not politeness; it is what makes the fix audible at all.`],"lib-pr1":[`The interjection fails in one specific way: the sentence before it has no destination, so you claim the floor and then assemble the thought out loud. The rehearsal that fixes this happens before the meeting, not during it — pick the one thing you will say, write it as a single sentence, and let the interjection be the wrapper rather than the beginning. People who speak early and well in meetings are almost always people who decided what they would say while walking in.`],"lib-pr4":[`The imbalance is invisible until it is expensive, which is why the trade has to be named at the moment of asking rather than at the moment of failure. "I can do it by Friday if we drop the integration test, or do the whole thing by Tuesday" is a sentence that puts the choice where it belongs — with the person who owns the priority. Silently absorbing the shortfall feels like being helpful for about three weeks, and then it becomes the baseline everyone plans against.`],"lib-pr5":[`The follow-up fails when it is a summary of the meeting rather than a payment for it. Restating what everyone already agreed is not value; it is proof you were there. The middle line has to give the other person something they did not have before they opened the email — a number, a name, a specific risk, or the one thing you said you would check. Recaps are archived; gifts are answered.`],"lib-bz2":[`The axis move only works if you genuinely agree first, and the agreement has to be specific enough to be believable. "I understand" is a transition; "yes, if I were you I would be worried about the switching cost too" is a position. Then the new axis has to be one they already care about, not one that happens to favour you — moving the comparison to lifetime cost works only with a buyer whose problem is churn, and with anyone else it reads as a rehearsed rebuttal.`],"lib-bz3":[`The silence after a number is the most misread tool in negotiation, because the discomfort of it belongs to the person who said the number. Most people fill it with a justification, which tells the other side the number was soft. The discipline is to have your next sentence decided in advance and to be willing not to use it: "take your time" is a complete contribution. If they speak first, you have learned something; if you speak first, you have only repeated yourself.`],"lib-bz4":[`A volunteered limitation only pays if it is real, specific, and adjacent to something you are good at. "We are slower than the big vendors on integrations" from a company whose selling point is craft is credible and makes everything else believable. A fake limitation — one that is secretly a strength — is transparent and costs you the whole frame. The test: if the limitation would lose you a deal with the wrong buyer, it is real.`],"lib-pr3":[`An upward one-liner is not flattery, and the difference is falsifiability. "Great work on the launch" cannot be checked and therefore means nothing. "The launch note on Tuesday did the thing most people skip — it said what we were not doing" can be checked, and being checked is what makes it land. The rule: if you could not defend the sentence to a third person with a specific example, do not say it.`]},l=e({LIBRARY_EXTRA_3_DEEP:()=>u}),u={"lib-fam1":{thesis:`A boundary only holds if the warmth and the rule arrive in the same breath. Separate them and you get either a doormat or a wall.`,deep:[`Most people fail at family boundaries for a structural reason, not a moral one: they choose between being loved and being free, and they choose it every single time the topic comes up. The warm boundary is the refusal of that binary. It says, in one sentence, that the relationship is permanent and the subject is closed. Two clauses, two different kinds of permanence, and the reason it works is that the other person no longer has to guess which one they are getting.`,`Notice what the first clause is doing mechanically. It is not flattery; it is a status gift. Your aunt has been asking the same question for six years, and on some level she knows she is being tolerated. Naming her care as real care gives her something she has been trying to get the whole time, and once she has it, the question loses its function. People press on topics where they feel unseen. Being seen is the thing that finally lets them stop.`,`The second clause must be a topic rule, never a character rule. "I am not discussing it at dinner" is a topic rule: it tells everyone what is not happening in this room. "You always do this" is a character rule: it tells one person what they are, and you will be arguing about their personality at midnight. Topic rules can be obeyed. Character rules can only be defended or avenged.`,`The third element people forget is the substitute. A boundary with no redirect leaves a hole in the conversation, and holes get filled with the same subject again in ten minutes. "I will call you the day there is news" is a substitute: it gives her a future in which she is first, so she does not have to fight for information now. Warmth, rule, substitute. The whole structure is three beats and it takes nine seconds to say.`,`The hardest part is the repeat. Family systems test boundaries not out of malice but out of habit — the conversation has grooves, and everyone rolls back into them. Your second delivery matters more than your first. If the first was warm and the second is exasperated ("I literally just said—"), you have taught the table that the boundary was a mood. Same words, same tone, second time. That consistency, not the content, is what eventually rewrites the groove.`,`Finally: expect the boundary to be tested hardest by the person who loves you most. A mother who watches your face instead of listening to your words is not being hostile; she is running a longer-term project and your boundary is data. Hold it without punishing her for checking. "Same answer, and I am glad you care enough to keep asking" is the sentence that lets a persistent person keep her dignity while learning the rule.`],script:[{line:`"Auntie, you ask because you actually care, and I love that — and I am not discussing it at dinner."`,note:`Warmth and rule in one breath. Do not pause between the clauses.`},{line:`Them: "We are only asking because we worry."`,note:`The move to notice: the question was never about information, it was about being needed.`},{line:`"I know you worry. That is exactly why I will tell you myself the day there is news."`,note:`Substitute: gives her a future where she is first.`},{line:`"Same answer as earlier, and I am glad you care enough to keep asking."`,note:`The repeat, without irritation. This is the sentence that rewrites the groove.`},{line:`"Let us make this a no-question topic at dinner. Everything else is fair game."`,note:`A rule for the table, not a shot at one person.`}],failureModes:[`Warmth without a rule: a lovely conversation in which nothing is decided, and the same question at the next dinner, and the one after.`,`Rule without warmth: "I am not talking about this." Perfectly clear, and now there is a family story about how you have changed.`,`Delivering the second repeat with irritation, which retroactively turns the first delivery into a performance you were not committed to.`,`Letting a relative turn it into a negotiation about their feelings ("so I am not allowed to care?") instead of simply restating the topic rule.`,`Using a sibling or partner as the reason, which relocates the boundary onto someone who did not consent to defend it.`],drill:[`Write your own three beats for a topic your family will not drop: warmth, topic rule, substitute. Keep the whole thing under thirty-five words. Say it out loud until you can deliver it while pouring tea — because you will be pouring tea when it happens.`,`Then rehearse the repeat. Have someone ask you the same question three times in a row. Your only allowed answer is the same sentence, same tone, three times. Most people break on the second.`],related:[`lib-fam2`,`lib-dc4`,`lib-hm3`,`lib-ei4`]},"lib-fam2":{thesis:`Defensive people are not arguing about the facts. They are defending their standing. Change the grammatical subject and the conversation becomes survivable.`,deep:[`With a proud person — and parents are the archetype — every sentence you say is being sorted into one of two piles: information or indictment. Once something lands in the indictment pile, the content stops mattering entirely and the whole mind goes to the defence of character. This is why the most carefully reasoned case you have ever built in your life produces the response "so everything I have done means nothing". That reply is not a non sequitur. It is a perfectly logical response to being put on trial.`,`The lever is the grammatical subject. "You are hiding something about the house" makes them the subject and the verb an accusation. "I have not been sleeping because I do not understand what is happening with the house" makes you the subject and the verb a condition. Both contain the same information. Only one of them can be answered without defending an identity, which is why only one of them gets answered.`,`The second lever is specificity. Patterns are identity claims in disguise: "you always interfere" is a sentence about who someone is. Incidents are events: "when you told Ramesh to ignore the letter, I was scared" is a sentence about one Tuesday. An event can be discussed and even conceded. A pattern must be denied, because to accept it is to accept a permanent demotion. If you have three incidents, name one tonight and leave the other two in your pocket — you do not need them, and their presence in the room turns a conversation into a case file.`,`Third: build the face-saving door before you need it. "It is possible I have this wrong, and if I do, tell me where" is not a rhetorical nicety. It is the only mechanism by which a proud person can change position without losing rank in front of themselves. Without it, agreement costs them something in their own eyes, and people almost never pay that price in front of their child. With it, agreement becomes a correction rather than a defeat.`,`Fourth: ask for an action, never a character change. "You need to start being honest with me" is unanswerable and unperformable. "Will you come with me on Thursday?" is a yes or a no, and yes is available. This is the whole craft of getting change from defensive people: reduce the ask until it is small enough to be accepted without a loss of face, then let the acceptance do the work of rebuilding the relationship around a new behaviour.`,`Finally, close with what has not changed. A hard conversation with a parent leaves a silence at the end, and that silence gets filled with meaning. "None of this changes that I came to you first" fills it correctly. Love stated after a correction is what marks the difference between being told off and being trusted, and proud people listen for exactly that distinction.`],script:[{line:`"You have been on my side my whole life. That is exactly why I am coming to you and not going around you."`,note:`Establishes standing before the content, so the content cannot become a trial.`},{line:`"I have not been sleeping, because I do not understand what is happening with the house."`,note:`State, not charge. You are the subject.`},{line:`"When you told Ramesh to ignore the letter, I was scared."`,note:`One incident, not a pattern. The other two stay in your pocket.`},{line:`"It is possible I have this wrong. If I do, tell me where, and I will drop it."`,note:`Face-saving door, offered in advance.`},{line:`"I am not asking for a promise. I am asking whether you will come with me on Thursday."`,note:`One action, answerable today.`},{line:`"I came to you first because you are still the person I come to. That is not changing."`,note:`Fill the closing silence with the relationship, not with a summary.`}],failureModes:[`Bringing the history, which converts a solvable Thursday into a trial of the last twelve years.`,`Recruiting a sibling as corroboration, which makes it a coalition and therefore a war.`,`Softening so completely that the Thursday appointment never gets asked for, and leaving with a warm feeling and no change.`,`Winning the point and losing the standing, then discovering that every subsequent conversation has a new formality in it.`,`Saying "I am not accusing you" while delivering a charge — the disclaimer does not survive the next sentence.`],drill:[`Take the hardest thing you need to say to a parent and rewrite it three times, each version starting with "I" instead of "you". Keep the version that is still true. Then say it out loud and record it; listen for "always", "never" or "again" and cut them.`,`Then rehearse being interrupted. Someone cuts you off mid-sentence and your only reply is: "Let me finish this one part, then you can tell me where I am wrong." Ten times.`],related:[`lib-fam1`,`lib-dc1`,`lib-dc2`,`lib-ei2`]},"lib-fam3":{thesis:`In family labour disputes the currency is not hours, it is being taken for granted. Fix the assumption and the rota fixes itself.`,deep:[`The stated conflict is usually about who does what. The real conflict is about whether anyone has noticed. This is why the hours argument never resolves: you can win a fact about Thursday and still lose the thing you actually wanted, which was acknowledgement. Anyone trying to settle this by arithmetic alone will be having the same fight next month with a new spreadsheet.`,`Start with the private list, and be strict about its two uses. Use one: protect yourself from gaslighting, because "I do most of it" is easy to dispute and "eleven visits, six pharmacy runs, four insurance calls in October" is not. Use two: keep your own account honest so you are not litigating a feeling. What the list must never be is evidence in the room. Once the ledger is on the table, the conversation becomes an audit of the past, and nobody has ever repaired a family by winning an audit.`,`Impact beats unfairness, every time. "I have cancelled work twice this month and I am running on empty" is a fact about your capacity, and it is impossible to dispute without disputing your own experience. "You never help" is a verdict, and verdicts get appealed. If you want a sibling to move, you have to give them something they can accept without admitting they were the villain — which means describing the cost, not the fault.`,`Ask for tasks, not effort. "Can you help more?" is a mood; it produces a warm yes on Sunday and nothing on Thursday. "Will you take the hospital visits on Thursdays and the pharmacy every other week?" is a decision that can be scheduled. The more specific the ask, the more real the answer — including when the answer is no, which is information you need.`,`Let them choose. People resist assignments and over-deliver on selections. Offer two or three real slots and let your sibling pick, then take the rest without commentary. The deeper move is that choosing makes them a participant rather than a defendant, and participants show up.`,`Build the ritual before you need it. Ten minutes every Sunday, before resentment has a case to make, is what prevents the annual explosion. The purpose is not scheduling — it is maintaining the relationship at a frequency where small problems are still small. Families that talk weekly fight yearly; families that talk yearly fight permanently.`],script:[{line:`"We are both trying to do right by her. I do not think either of us needs to be the villain here."`,note:`Naming the fear of being the villain removes the need to defend against it.`},{line:`"I have cancelled work twice this month and I am running on empty."`,note:`Impact, not unfairness. Undisputable.`},{line:`"What I need is not more effort. I need the Thursday visits and the pharmacy every other week."`,note:`A task, not a mood.`},{line:`"Pick whichever you can actually do — visits, pharmacy, or the insurance calls. I will take the other two."`,note:`Choice converts a defendant into a participant.`},{line:`"Ten minutes every Sunday, just us. If we stop talking, we end up fighting."`,note:`Maintenance beats reconciliation.`}],failureModes:[`Leading with the ledger, which turns a shared problem into a court case about the past.`,`Accepting "I will try" as a commitment — try is the sound a task makes as it disappears.`,`Escalating to the parent, who is both the subject of the care and the least able to arbitrate it.`,`Keeping a score you never intend to mention, which curdles into contempt and eventually leaks out at the worst possible moment.`,`Doing everything yourself "for now", which trains the whole family that the rota needs no discussion.`],drill:[`Write your October task list with counts. Then write one specific ask in a single sentence with a day of the week and a frequency in it. Say the ask out loud with no explanation attached — every clause of justification you add is an invitation to negotiate the reasons instead of the task.`,`Then practise the no. Have someone refuse your ask, and answer only: "That is fair. Which of the other two can you take?"`],related:[`lib-fam5`,`lib-dc4`,`lib-pr4`,`lib-mny2`]},"lib-fam4":{thesis:`In irreversible situations, the offer to fix is about your discomfort. Presence is the only help that does not require the situation to be manageable.`,deep:[`Watch yourself in the first minute of bad news. Almost everyone produces a solution within fifteen seconds, and almost everyone reports later that the solution was not the point. The solution exists to end the silence, and the silence is what is unbearable to you. This is not a character flaw, it is a nervous system, but it is the reason so much comfort lands as abandonment: the person with the diagnosis has just been told, by implication, that their situation would be fine if they were handling it better.`,`The alternative is to be unsolved with them. "That sounds unbearable, and I am not going to pretend I can fix it" does something no advice can: it confirms their reality. Most of what a person in crisis is doing in conversation is checking whether what is happening is as bad as it feels. Everyone else is minimising, because minimising is easier to be around. The person who agrees it is bad, and stays anyway, is the one they will remember.`,`The second half of the sentence matters as much as the first. "And I am not going anywhere" is what makes the honesty safe. Naming the unbearable without staying is just accurate cruelty. Presence is a package: the truth plus the continued body in the room.`,`Then get practical in a way that costs them nothing to accept. "Let me know if you need anything" is a task handed to someone with no capacity. "I am bringing food Thursday at six — what can you not eat?" is a decision they can answer in three words, and it removes the exhausting work of asking. Specific help is the only help that gets taken.`,`Follow their lead on register. Some days the person wants to plan the funeral; most days they want to talk about cricket, because cricket does not remind them. Being good company in the ordinary register is not avoidance, it is a service — you are lending them a few hours in which they are a person rather than a patient. The failure here is insisting on the serious version in order to prove your depth.`,`And then the long tail. Support arrives in a flood and leaves in a drought. The fourth month is emptier than the first. The single most valuable thing you can do is put a reminder in your phone for a date everyone else has forgotten, and call on an ordinary Tuesday with nothing to offer but company.`],script:[{line:`"That sounds unbearable, and I am not going to pretend I can fix it."`,note:`Confirms their reality instead of minimising it.`},{line:`"And I am not going anywhere."`,note:`Makes the honesty safe. The two sentences are one unit.`},{line:`"How was today? You can answer with one word."`,note:`"How are you?" is unanswerable; a day can be answered.`},{line:`"I am bringing food Thursday at six. What can you not eat?"`,note:`Specific, answerable, costs them nothing.`},{line:`"We can talk about it, or we can talk about the match. Both are fine with me."`,note:`Follow their lead on register.`},{line:`"It has been three months and everyone has gone quiet. I have not."`,note:`The fourth-month call, which almost nobody makes.`}],failureModes:[`The fifteen-second solution, which tells them the situation is manageable and their feelings are excessive.`,`The bright side — "everything happens for a reason" — which is comfort for the speaker, delivered as though it were for the listener.`,`Comparisons to someone who survived, which turns a lived situation into a probability problem.`,`Crying until they comfort you, which reverses the transaction and guarantees they will not call again.`,`Disappearing after the funeral, which is when the silence becomes loudest.`],drill:[`Write the two-sentence unit — the honesty and the staying — for the hardest situation in your life right now. Say it out loud. If it makes you reach for a fix in the following breath, you have not finished; rehearse until you can sit in ten seconds of silence after it without rescuing anyone.`,`Then put one reminder in your phone for ninety days from now, for someone who is currently in crisis.`],related:[`lib-ei5`,`lib-ei1`,`lib-fam1`,`lib-ei3`]},"lib-fam5":{thesis:`Adults sharing a home inherit whatever arrangement is not discussed in the first fortnight. Terms written early are warmer than resentment written late.`,deep:[`The pattern is so reliable it is almost a law. Week one is a holiday: everyone is generous, everything is temporary, and nobody wants to be the person who raises money. Week three is a tribunal: the mother is tidying a room she was told was private, or the sibling is commenting on the hours someone keeps, and both sides are now arguing from facts that were never agreed. The blow-up is not a failure of goodwill. It is the predictable result of running an adult household on inherited teenager rules.`,`The first move is timing, and it is non-negotiable. The conversation must happen while there is nothing to defend — in week one, in daylight, with tea, before anyone has been annoyed. Every day you wait, the old arrangement hardens into "how we are", and then any proposal sounds like a criticism of the last twenty years.`,`The second move is asking before offering. You are almost certainly wrong about what they want. Many parents do not want rent; they want presence, or to feel useful, or to know you are not going to vanish the moment your finances recover. "What would make this feel fair to you while I am here?" is a question that produces the actual currency of the household, which is rarely money.`,`Then propose terms, specifically. Contribution, chores, guests, quiet hours, the one space that is genuinely yours, and a review date. Written on the fridge is not cold; it is what saves you from renegotiating in the middle of an argument, when the terms proposed are always the harshest ones. Concrete terms are also a form of respect: they say you take the arrangement seriously enough to be bound by it.`,`Protect one domain and be generous everywhere else. If the kitchen runs by her rules, that is cheap — say yes, mean it, and it buys enormous credit. But your schedule and your room are not negotiable, and it is far easier to hold one clear line than to fight eleven small skirmishes. The one line should be stated in the terms conversation, not discovered later at a door.`,`Finally, the review clause. "Let us look at this again in two months" quietly converts a permanent regression into a temporary arrangement, which is what both of you actually need to hear. It also gives you a scheduled, unemotional moment to fix what is not working — before it becomes a story about the time you moved back and everything went wrong.`],script:[{line:`"This is a big thing you are doing for me. I do not want to be casual about it."`,note:`Gratitude first, and mean it. It is not leverage, it is truth.`},{line:`"Before I say what I think — what would make this feel fair to you while I am here?"`,note:`Asking first usually reveals the real currency is presence, not rent.`},{line:`"I want to be clear about money, chores and evenings, so none of it becomes a dinner-table fight."`,note:`Terms framed as a service to the relationship.`},{line:`"The kitchen is completely yours. What I need is my room stays mine and I do not have to explain my evenings."`,note:`One line, held properly, beats eleven small skirmishes.`},{line:`"Let us look at this again in two months and change what is not working."`,note:`The review clause that turns permanence into a lease.`}],failureModes:[`Silent gratitude, which they read as ingratitude, and which then becomes the real subject of every disagreement.`,`Treating the house as a hotel — coming and going with no notice in a shared home is not independence.`,`Letting them subsidise you for the sake of peace, which creates standing, and standing creates commentary.`,`Having the terms conversation for the first time during a fight, when both of you will propose the worst version of everything.`,`Agreeing to terms you cannot keep in order to seem reasonable, which teaches everyone that the agreement is decoration.`],drill:[`Write your terms on one page: contribution, chores, guests, quiet hours, your one non-negotiable space, review date. Then rehearse the gratitude sentence until it sounds like you and not like a press release.`,`Then rehearse the one line. Have someone ask you where you were last night and answer with the line — once, warmly, without explanation.`],related:[`lib-fam1`,`lib-fam3`,`lib-dc3`,`lib-st4`]},"lib-mny1":{thesis:`People avoid debts because of shame, not greed. Take the shame out of the room and the arithmetic becomes discussable.`,deep:[`The eight-month silence is not laziness. It is a person who feels worse every month and therefore contacts you less, which makes the debt feel bigger, which makes them contact you even less. This is a doom loop with a very specific engine: the moment they see your name on their phone, they feel the amount plus the failure. Your job in the first ten seconds of the conversation is to disable that engine, because a person in shame cannot make a plan — they can only endure the conversation and then avoid you again.`,`"I never followed up because it felt awkward, and that is on me" is a strange-sounding sentence that does more work than any demand. It admits a fault of your own that is real (you did avoid it too), it does not reduce the debt by a rupee, and it gives your friend their dignity back. Once they are not being prosecuted, the negotiation can start.`,`Then give structure immediately, because vagueness is the medium shame grows in. "I am not asking for it all at once. Does 5,000 a month from next month work?" is a proposal with a number, a date and a frequency, and it is answerable. Notice the wording: you are not asking whether they remember the debt, or whether they intend to pay; you are asking about a payment plan. Precisely framed questions get precise answers.`,`Let them adjust the structure. If 5,000 is impossible, the goal is not to win the number, it is to have a number. A plan they helped shape is a plan with a psychological owner, and people pay things they own. What you must not do is accept "I will sort it out soon" — that is not a structure, it is a deferral with good manners.`,`Name the stake once, plainly. "I would rather have this awkward ten minutes than have this change the way we are with each other." This sentence is usually true, and it is the one that reframes the whole conversation from debt collection to friendship maintenance. It also tells them what will happen if the plan fails, without a threat: the thing at risk is not your money, it is the ease between you.`,`And confirm in writing the same day, lightly. Not a contract — a kindness that protects the next conversation. "Confirming: 5,000 by the 5th, and I will nudge you if I have not heard by the 7th." You are also giving them a way to be reminded without either of you having to feel like an adversary.`,`One more decision to make before you open your mouth: is the outcome money, a plan, or the friendship without the debt? All three are legitimate. Choosing one makes the conversation coherent; asking for all three at once is how it becomes a fight. If you decide to forgive it, forgive it out loud, because a silent forgiveness leaves them feeling the debt and you remembering it.`],script:[{line:`"I want to sort out the money from March properly, so it stops being a thing between us."`,note:`Names it in the first sentence. Ambiguity is what kept it alive for eight months.`},{line:`"I never followed up because it felt awkward. That is on me."`,note:`Takes the shame out of the room without touching the debt.`},{line:`"I am not asking for it all at once. Does 5,000 a month from next month work?"`,note:`Structure, not a demand.`},{line:`"I would rather have this awkward ten minutes than have this change the way we are with each other."`,note:`The stake, named once.`},{line:`"Confirming: 5,000 by the 5th, and I will nudge you by the 7th — that is me keeping my own word."`,note:`Written confirmation framed as your discipline, not their unreliability.`}],failureModes:[`Hinting — "so, things better now?" — which teaches them the topic is a trap.`,`Asking in front of others, which produces public defensiveness and a private grudge.`,`Forgiving silently, which leaves the debt live for both of you.`,`Adding interest, history or insults to the first ask; one topic per conversation.`,`Accepting "soon" as a plan, which is the same deferral that produced the eight months.`],drill:[`Write the amount, the original date, and the monthly plan you are asking for. Say the opening two sentences out loud five times until "awkward" costs you nothing to say. Then practise the answer to "I will sort it out soon": "I know you will. Let us put a number and a date on it so neither of us has to think about it again."`],related:[`lib-mny2`,`lib-fam3`,`lib-dc4`,`lib-mny3`]},"lib-mny2":{thesis:`Money becomes corrosive when it is private and vague. A number in a group is arithmetic; a number in private is an accusation.`,deep:[`The shared bill is a social problem disguised as a maths problem. Everyone at the dinner knows roughly what they owe, precisely none of them want to be the person who asks, and the one person who does ask is volunteering to be the villain of the memory. That is the trap: the cost of raising it feels personal, so nobody raises it, so the person who is out of pocket carries a resentment that is far more expensive than the amount.`,`The design that works is public, specific and self-directed. Public, because a number stated to the group cannot be reinterpreted as a personal insinuation. Specific, because vagueness is where avoidance lives — "I think it was around six hundred each?" invites three weeks of non-answers. Self-directed, because paying your own share in the same message removes any suggestion that you are collecting on behalf of your own needs.`,`"I am going to be that person: dinner was 4,800 total, so 600 each. Sending mine to Ramesh now." Six seconds of read, four pieces of information, zero accusation. The self-mocking opening is not decoration: it acknowledges the social rule that naming money is gauche, and steps over it deliberately. Everyone exhales, because you have done the thing nobody wanted to do and taken the small social cost for the group.`,`Then respect the volume rules. One clear ask in the group is the entire social contract. A second nudge after about a week, still light, still specific, is entirely reasonable. A third converts the money into a bigger object than the friendship, and from there the group remembers the year someone would not let go of six hundred rupees.`,`Handle the individual privately and once. If one person has not paid, the group message must not single them out — a public naming is a humiliation that will outlive the debt. A private note is fine: "no rush, just keeping the spreadsheet honest." Then, if they cannot pay, let them say it and move on with "cover it and get the next one", said once, warmly, and never referenced again.`,`Finally, install the norm before it recurs. "Shall we split as we order from now on?" is a structural fix, and structures beat willpower. The awkwardness was never about the amount; it was about the absence of a rule. Give the group a rule and the topic disappears, which is the only durable outcome worth having.`],script:[{line:`"I am going to be that person: dinner was 4,800 total, so 600 each. Sending mine to Ramesh now."`,note:`Public, specific, self-directed. Six seconds.`},{line:`"Two of us still in on Friday — 600 each, no rush, just keeping the spreadsheet honest."`,note:`The second nudge, a week later. There is no third.`},{line:`"Shall we split as we order from now on? I keep forgetting and then it gets weird."`,note:`A norm, not a complaint. Structures beat willpower.`},{line:`"Cover it and get the next one. I will not remember, and I would rather not."`,note:`Grace that ends the topic permanently.`}],failureModes:[`Silent resentment, which is a real cost paid on a fake debt.`,`Publicly naming the one person who has not paid, which is a humiliation with a long half-life.`,`Rounding up to make a point, which is not a joke to everyone.`,`Never mentioning it and then declining the next dinner, so everyone reads a mood and nobody learns the rule.`,`Chasing four times, which converts a small debt into a large story about you.`],drill:[`Write the group message exactly as you would send it, then read it aloud and delete every word that explains yourself. The final version should be two sentences: the number and what you are doing about your own share.`,`Then practise saying "shall we split as we order?" in a way that sounds like a suggestion rather than a correction.`],related:[`lib-mny1`,`lib-st4`,`lib-fam3`,`lib-f6`]},"lib-mny3":{thesis:`Speed reads as doubt. Say the number flatly, attach it to scope in one clause, and let the silence do the negotiating.`,deep:[`The price conversation is decided before the objection, in the three seconds after you say the number. Most people spend those three seconds worried about the other person’s reaction, and the body answers before the mouth does: the sentence accelerates, the pitch rises, a small disclaimer gets appended. All three are heard as uncertainty. The client is not evaluating whether your price is fair; they are evaluating whether you believe it is. If you do not, they are obliged to help you lower it.`,`Remove the runway. "So, given everything, we were thinking maybe somewhere around, if that works for you…" is not a price, it is a request for permission. Permission is expensive. "It is X, and that includes the strategy, the build and two rounds of revisions" is a statement about the world. One clause of scope does more work than a minute of justification, because it converts a number into a package and a package into a comparison that is in your favour.`,`Then stop. The five seconds of silence after your own number are the most profitable discomfort in business. Whatever you say in that gap will be a discount: a joke, a nervous laugh, a softening clause, "but we can be flexible". Say nothing. Let them be the first to speak, even if you have to count in your head.`,`When "that is too expensive" arrives — and it usually will — do not defend the price. Defending a price is arguing about your worth, and it validates the frame that the number is negotiable on rate. Ask about their budget instead: "What number were you working with?" You move from fear to information in one sentence, and you keep the conversation about fit rather than about your margin.`,`If you must move, move scope, never rate. "I can do it for Y if we drop the revisions and you handle the copy. Same rate, smaller job." Discounting tells them your first number was fiction, and clients who discover fiction keep looking for it. De-scoping keeps the number true and gives the client a real choice about what they want to buy.`,`And be willing to lose it well, out loud. "That may be more than this is worth to you, and that is a fair answer. If it changes, I would like to do it." Walking away cleanly is the only mechanism that over time raises your average price, and it takes about eleven words to do it with grace.`],script:[{line:`"It is X. That covers the strategy, the build and two rounds of revisions."`,note:`No runway, one clause of scope, then stop.`},{line:`(Silence. Five full seconds.)`,note:`Everything you say here is a discount. Say nothing.`},{line:`"What number were you working with?"`,note:`Replaces defending the price with collecting information.`},{line:`"I can do it for Y if we drop the revisions and you handle the copy. Same rate, smaller job."`,note:`De-scope, never discount.`},{line:`"That may be more than this is worth to you, and that is a fair answer. If it changes, I would like to do it."`,note:`Losing well is a pricing strategy, not a consolation.`}],failureModes:[`A range — "between A and B" means B, and everyone in the room knows it.`,`The apologising sentence: "sorry, I know it is a lot, but…" which agrees with the objection before it is made.`,`Explaining your costs, which invites the client to audit your life instead of evaluating your work.`,`Discounting to close, which teaches the client that your numbers are opening bids.`,`Filling the silence with a joke, which converts a firm number into a nervous one.`],drill:[`Say your real number out loud, flat, then sit in silence for five seconds on a timer. Ten reps. You are training the pause, not the sentence.`,`Then practise the de-scope: for your last quote, write down two things you could remove so the price can fall without the rate falling.`],related:[`lib-bz3`,`lib-bz2`,`lib-f5`,`lib-mny1`]},"lib-hm1":{thesis:`Humour fails when the audience has to work out whether they were the joke. Make yourself the target and the calculation disappears.`,deep:[`Funny is a social judgement, not a comedic one. A line that would kill in a club can die at a dinner table because the room is busy running a threat assessment: is the person holding the floor about to make one of us the punchline? Until that question is answered, nothing is funny. A self-targeted joke answers it instantly and answers it in your favour, which is why the safest humour in an unknown group is always at your own expense.`,`The status arithmetic is counterintuitive and worth spelling out. Making yourself the target raises your status, because you chose the target and you are not bruised by it — that is what composure looks like. Being made the target by someone else lowers it. The same content, aimed inward, reads as security; aimed outward, it reads as aggression. Confidence is the ability to be the butt of your own joke without flinching.`,`The rule that keeps this charming rather than pitiful is specificity plus confidence. "I once tried to be mysterious at a party and someone asked if I was feeling alright" is precise, small, and told as a finished fact. "I am just useless with people, honestly" is a bid for reassurance, and everyone can tell the difference within one sentence. Self-deprecation without specificity becomes self-pity; self-pity asks the room to look after you, and the room stops finding you funny.`,`There is a limit and it is lower than most people think. One or two self-targeted jokes per conversation is warmth; five is a persona, and a persona is a mask that stops people getting close. The same line that makes a group relax on first meeting becomes a way of never being known by the twentieth. Use it to open, then let it go so the conversation can move to something with more risk in it.`,`The best version of this is not jokes at all — it is honest failure, told without an apology. Someone asks how the new job is going. "I have learned that I am the kind of person who needs three reminders to attach a file" gets a real laugh because it is true, because it is ordinary, and because you delivered it as information rather than as a confession. Aim for that register: things that actually happened to you, described with enough precision to be funny.`,`And never undercut the joke with an explanation or a hedge. "Sorry, that was stupid" after a self-deprecating line undoes the composure that made it work, which is the only thing making it funny. If it dies, let it die. A dead joke handled well is more charming than a live one handled nervously.`],script:[{line:`"I tried to be mysterious at a party once. Someone asked if I was feeling alright."`,note:`Specific, small, delivered as a finished fact.`},{line:`"I am the kind of person who needs three reminders to attach a file."`,note:`Honest failure told as information, not as a confession.`},{line:`"That joke usually works, so I am going to blame the room."`,note:`Handling a dead joke with composure — the recovery is the charm.`},{line:`"I have peaked. Everything after this is admin."`,note:`Small, self-directed, does not ask the room for reassurance.`}],failureModes:[`"I am just useless with people, honestly" — self-pity in a joke costume, which asks the room to look after you.`,`Five self-deprecating lines in a row, which builds a persona and blocks intimacy.`,`Apologising after the line, which removes the composure that made it funny.`,`Using a self-deprecating joke as a way to pre-empt criticism you actually fear, which reads as anxious rather than warm.`,`Targeting a nearby person "gently" in a group you have just joined — gentle is a judgement the audience makes, not the speaker.`],drill:[`Write three true things that happened to you this week that are mildly embarrassing and not serious. Cut each to one sentence with a specific detail. Say all three out loud to one person and watch which one actually lands — the winner is usually the most specific, not the most dramatic.`],related:[`lib-hm2`,`lib-hm4`,`lib-w1`,`lib-st5`]},"lib-hm2":{thesis:`New jokes demonstrate wit; returning jokes demonstrate attention. A callback is the cheapest intimacy available.`,deep:[`Attention is the scarce good in conversation, not cleverness. Everyone can produce a decent joke; almost nobody remembers what the other person said forty minutes ago. This is why the callback is such an efficient social instrument: it is evidence. The joke is only the vehicle — what the other person hears underneath it is "I was listening, I remembered, and we have a world that is ours".`,`The raw material is ordinary and easy to collect once you know to look. Oddly specific things: the coffee that tasted like a decision, the chair that collapsed, the colleague who says "circle back", the dog with no respect for anyone. You are not looking for the funniest moment in the evening, you are looking for the most particular one, because particularity is what makes a callback recognisable. A generic memory cannot be called back, because nobody is sure it happened.`,`Timing has three usable windows. Same conversation, after a lull — a callback is far better than a topic change because it restarts the energy without any work. Same evening, in a bigger group — this is the strongest use, because it makes the earlier person feel like your co-author in front of others. And days later, in a message — "still thinking about the coffee that tasted like a decision" is a whole relationship in eleven words and requires no reason to text at all.`,`The frequency rule is two. Two callbacks in one conversation is wit and warmth. The third turns the shared joke into a bit, and bits are entertainments rather than conversations — the person becomes an audience for your running gag and the intimacy stops growing. Retire the reference early and keep it available for a later date; a callback works best when it has had time to become a memory.`,`The advanced move is the callback with an upgrade: returning the joke in a form that adds something. "The chair that collapsed has been promoted — it is now in the hallway with a new role." You are demonstrating memory and invention in the same breath, and you are co-writing a private canon with that person. This is how groups become groups: not through shared interests, but through shared references nobody else in the room understands.`,`The failure mode is the callback that requires explanation, which is a joke about your own memory. If you have to say "remember when I said…", the recall has failed and you are now performing a footnote. Keep the reference short enough to be self-evident and you never have to explain it.`],script:[{line:`"Still thinking about the coffee that tasted like a decision."`,note:`The next-day callback. Requires no reason to text.`},{line:`"The chair has been promoted. It now has a role in the hallway."`,note:`Callback with an upgrade: memory plus invention.`},{line:`"I see we are back on the topic of the dog with no respect."`,note:`Same-evening callback in a bigger group — makes them your co-author.`},{line:`"We are going to be the people who never let that go, are we not?"`,note:`Names the shared world out loud.`}],failureModes:[`The callback that requires "remember when I said…", which turns a joke into a footnote.`,`Three callbacks in one conversation, which converts a friendship into a fanbase.`,`Calling back something that was embarrassing to the other person rather than jointly absurd.`,`Collecting material so deliberately that you stop being present in the conversation.`,`Calling back a reference nobody else remembers, which is a private joke with an audience of one.`],drill:[`For one evening, collect exactly three specific references — not funny things, specific things. Then use one in the same conversation, one the following day, and hold the third for a week. Notice that the week-old one lands hardest.`],related:[`lib-hm1`,`lib-hm3`,`lib-w5`,`lib-st5`]},"lib-hm3":{thesis:`A joke can carry a truth a sentence cannot, because it offers everyone an exit. The craft is the truth ratio.`,deep:[`In families and workplaces there is a category of true thing that cannot be said directly. Saying it plainly produces a war; not saying it produces a slow rot. The joke is the only vehicle that can move cargo across this border, because it is deniable. If it lands badly, everyone can agree it was a joke and the subject retreats safely. If it lands, the subject has been opened and nobody has been publicly accused.`,`The mechanism is the exit, and it must be real rather than rhetorical. A joke that has no way back is just an accusation with a laugh track, and everyone in the room knows it. "So nobody noticed the deadline until it was two days past, which is a bold strategy" leaves the whole group an exit; "So Ramesh decided the deadline was optional, interesting" leaves one person with none, and that person will remember it precisely and for years.`,`Truth ratio is the dial. At 100% joke you have probably bought a laugh and changed nothing — pleasant, forgettable, and the true thing remains unsaid. At 100% truth you have made a speech and dressed it up, which is heard as passive-aggression and produces exactly the defensiveness you were trying to avoid. Aim for one true observation wrapped in something light, delivered once: a hypothesis about the situation, offered at an angle, with warmth in the delivery.`,`Delivery details decide whether it survives. Keep it short — a joke that needs a sentence and a half of setup loses its exit before the punchline. Keep your body relaxed and your eyes on the group rather than on the person it is aimed at; a joke aimed with the eyes becomes a dart. And keep your tone affectionate: the same words delivered cold become a public correction, and the exit closes.`,`Then stop, and never explain. Explanation is where the exit closes — once you say "obviously I mean that we should…", you have withdrawn the joke and claimed the accusation. If someone asks whether you were serious, the correct answer is warm and true: "half." Half is honest, keeps the exit, and invites the conversation to continue if the other person wants it.`,`Two more guards. First, only use this where you have accumulated goodwill; pressure-valve humour aimed at someone who does not yet trust you reads as an attack with plausible deniability, which is the worst thing a joke can be. Second, never use it twice on the same subject in one sitting. Once is a signal, the second is a campaign, and the third is how a family gets a new tradition of not speaking.`],script:[{line:`"Nobody noticed the deadline until two days after, which is certainly a bold strategy."`,note:`One true observation, whole-group target, exit intact.`},{line:`"I am told the meeting could have been an email. It was in fact an email. It was also an hour."`,note:`Pressure valve aimed at a situation, not a person.`},{line:`Them: "Wait, are you being serious?"`,note:`The moment the exit is tested.`},{line:`"Half."`,note:`Warm, true, keeps the exit open, and invites the real conversation if they want it.`},{line:`Them: "That is a fair point, actually."`,note:`The subject is now open, and nobody lost face getting there.`}],failureModes:[`Aiming the pressure valve at one person in front of everyone — that is a public correction with a laugh track.`,`Explaining the joke, which withdraws the exit and confirms the accusation.`,`Using it twice on the same subject, which converts a signal into a campaign.`,`Delivering it cold, which removes the warmth that makes it deniable.`,`Using it before any goodwill exists, which reads as an attack by someone clever enough to deny it.`],drill:[`Write the unsayable thing in your family or team as a plain sentence. Then wrap it into one light observation of twelve words or fewer, aimed at the situation rather than the person. Say it to a mirror and check your own tone is warm, not clipped. If you hear a prosecutor, cut the sentence in half.`,`Then rehearse "half" as an answer to "were you serious?" — and stop there.`],related:[`lib-hm1`,`lib-fam1`,`lib-ei4`,`lib-dc1`]},"lib-hm4":{thesis:`Teasing is only charming when the care is on the record. Until then, tease the situation and keep the person out of it.`,deep:[`Teasing is a status manoeuvre. One person holds the floor and makes the other person the subject of attention, and whether that feels delightful or humiliating depends entirely on two variables: is there warmth underneath, and who else is watching. In a new interaction, neither variable is in your favour yet, which is why early teasing so often produces a polite laugh and a small step back rather than chemistry.`,`The safe target in early conversation is the third thing: the queue, the institution, the weather, the absurdity of the venue, the coincidence that you are both there. Teasing the situation makes you allies against a shared object. Teasing the person makes you the audience and them the act. The direction is everything, and it is invisible to the person doing it — which is why so many people are baffled when their banter does not land.`,`Once warmth is established, the arithmetic changes. Teasing a person with whom you have a record of care reads as affection, because the underlying claim is "you are too secure with me to take this seriously". That is a gift. Without the record, the same sentence is a claim about their standing, made in public, and they have to decide instantly whether to laugh or to fight back — a decision that costs them something either way.`,`Playfulness done well is mutual. The tell is whether they can tease you back comfortably, and whether you receive it well. A tease you cannot receive is not play, it is one-directional commentary. So offer a target, allow a return, and take the return with visible pleasure — "that was good, that was better than mine" — which is what converts a jab into a game. People relax the moment they discover the game is not rigged.`,`Keep the subject small and the truth real. Teasing something that is true and acknowledged is warm; teasing something they are actually insecure about, even lightly, is a wound dressed as a joke. If their face changes, stop immediately and name it: "That one was too close, I am sorry." Recovery is a skill and it costs three words. People forgive a clumsy tease far more readily than a missing apology.`,`One final distinction worth carrying everywhere: flirtation is not insults with a nice voice. Warmth, attention, specificity and a little risk of your own are the ingredients. Teasing is a garnish. Used as the main ingredient, it produces a person who is laughing and slightly on guard, which is the opposite of the effect everyone claims to want.`],script:[{line:`"This queue has clearly been designed by someone who has never had to stand in it."`,note:`Tease the situation: you are allies against the third thing.`},{line:`"Right, I am going to say something slightly risky and then pretend I did not."`,note:`Names the risk, so it becomes play rather than a test.`},{line:`Them: "That was rude."`,note:`The moment warmth has not been established yet.`},{line:`"That was too close, I am sorry — genuinely."`,note:`Recovery in three words. People forgive clumsiness far more than a missing apology.`},{line:`Them: (teases you back)`,note:`The tell that it is mutual.`},{line:`"That was better than mine. I am going to let that sit for a second."`,note:`Taking a return well is what converts a jab into a game.`}],failureModes:[`Teasing a person you met an hour ago, which asks them to pay for warmth they have not yet received.`,`Teasing in front of an audience before you have any private warmth, which is a public status move.`,`Aiming at a real insecurity — what a joke costs you is measured by their face, not your intention.`,`Building the whole interaction out of teasing, which makes the other person laugh and stay slightly guarded.`,`Not apologising when a tease cuts, and instead doubling down with "it was just a joke", which converts clumsiness into untrustworthiness.`],drill:[`For one week, practise situational teasing with strangers: one observation about the queue, the venue, the weather. Notice how easy the laugh is compared to person-directed humour. Then find one person you already trust and practise being teased back and receiving it with visible pleasure.`],related:[`lib-hm1`,`lib-hm2`,`lib-wm1`,`lib-w2`]}},d=e({LIBRARY_EXTRA_5_DEEP:()=>f}),f={"lib-cr1":{thesis:`A first impression is not a verdict on your personality. It is a compression your brain performs because it cannot afford to evaluate every person properly. The useful news is that the compression follows rules — and the rules are learnable in an afternoon.`,deep:[`Within about a tenth of a second, people form a judgement about your trustworthiness, competence and aggression, and that judgement colours everything you say afterwards. The mechanism is unglamorous: your face at rest, your posture, how long you hold eye contact, whether you look like you are waiting for permission. None of it is character. All of it is legible. What makes first impressions feel unfair is that they are made from data you are not consciously transmitting.`,`Warmth is read before competence, and it is read mostly through the face at rest and through the first thing you do with your attention. A person whose face is neutral while listening looks severe; the same person with the faintest upturn at the eyes looks calm. That is not a smile — a smile at nothing is unnerving. It is the relaxation of a face that is not currently defending itself, and it is the single highest-leverage thing you can change.`,`Competence is read second and largely through stillness. Fidgeting, over-explaining, filling silence and laughing at your own sentences all read as a request for reassurance. It is not that confident people are calm; it is that calm people get read as confident, whether or not they are. Slow hands, one thing at a time, a pause before answering — these read as authority because they suggest nothing is being hidden.`,`The mistake almost everyone makes is trying to be impressive in the first thirty seconds. Impressive is a status claim, and a status claim from a stranger invites scrutiny. Warmth invites company. So the sequence that works is unglamorous: be readable, then be specific, then be slightly more interesting than expected. Nobody has ever been remembered for a great opening line; people are remembered for the reason they wanted to keep talking.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`(Walks into the room. You have four seconds before they decide how to treat you.)`},{who:`You`,line:`Hi — Alex. I was told you would be the person who actually knows how this works.`},{who:`Them`,line:`Depends what "this" is. What are you trying to do?`},{who:`You`,line:`Make sure I do not spend the next month building the wrong thing. Can I ask you one specific question?`}],failureModes:[`Performing warmth with a fixed grin. Held too long, a smile reads as a mask and makes people slightly uneasy — the goal is a settled face, not a happy one.`,`Trying to demonstrate competence before they have decided you are safe. Everything you say in those first minutes is heard through suspicion, however true it is.`,`Talking about yourself to fill a silence. The silence was fine; filling it told them something true and unflattering about your relationship with it.`,`Assuming the first impression is final. It is not — it is a hypothesis people revise constantly, which means the recovery is easy and most people never attempt it.`],drill:[`For one day, at every introduction, give your name and one specific reason you are talking to this person. No "how are you", no "nice to meet you". Watch how much faster the conversation gets real.`,`Record yourself answering "what do you do?" on your phone. Watch it back with the sound off. Whatever your body is doing in the first four seconds is what strangers are reading.`],related:[`lib-f8`,`lib-st1`,`lib-wm2`]},"lib-cr2":{thesis:`Repair is not what you say after you get it wrong. It is what you do in the four minutes afterwards, and those four minutes have a shape that most people instinctively get backwards.`,deep:[`When trust is damaged, the other person is not primarily looking for an explanation. They are looking for evidence that you understand the shape of what happened — because being understood is the experience of safety. That is why the apology that opens with "I am sorry, but" fails even when the "but" is true: the sentence tells them you are still in the position of explaining rather than the position of seeing.`,`The order that works is unhelpfully counter-intuitive. Name the thing, in specific behavioural terms, before any reason. Then name the effect, and name it as theirs, not yours — "you had to redo the whole thing on Friday" lands; "I felt awful about it" is about you. Then say what changes, in a way they can hold you to. Then stop. The stop is the part nobody does, because silence feels like being judged, and the urge to fill it produces the second apology that undoes the first.`,`There is a version of this that fails silently and is worth naming: the repair that is too big. A long, emotional apology turns the conversation into a performance of remorse and implicitly asks the hurt person to comfort you, or to reassure you that you are a good person. It is a kind of emotional taxation, and people remember it more than the original offence.`,`Finally, repair has a timestamp. What is small and immediate stays small. The same sentence three weeks later has to carry three weeks of ambiguity, and it will not. If you know you got it wrong, the trade is not between saying something awkward now and saying it gracefully later. It is between saying it now and it becoming a story about your character.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`It is fine. Do not worry about it.`},{who:`You`,line:`It is not really fine, and I would rather say it properly. I gave your work back at the last minute on Monday, which meant you rebuilt it over a weekend.`},{who:`Them`,line:`(Silence.)`},{who:`You`,line:`From now on you get it Thursday morning, and if I cannot make Thursday I will tell you on Wednesday. And I am sorry — that was on me, not the process.`}],failureModes:[`The "but". Any explanation appended to an apology converts the apology into an argument about whether the hurt was proportionate.`,`Asking for absolution: "are we okay?" turns the other person into a judge and hands them a job they did not apply for.`,`Repairing in public. A public apology is often a performance for the audience; the actual repair happens in a smaller room.`,`Changing the behaviour but never saying the sentence. It repairs the relationship but leaves them with the private thought that you never noticed, which is the thing they will bring up in three months.`],drill:[`Take one thing you got wrong this week and write the four beats in order: behaviour, their effect, what changes, stop. Say it out loud. If the version out loud is longer, it is not the same apology.`,`In your next disagreement, at the first moment you are wrong, say "that is on me" in four words or fewer and then stop. Count the seconds you can hold the silence.`],related:[`lib-dc3`,`lib-dc1`,`lib-ei2`]},"lib-cr3":{thesis:`A warm boundary is not a softer boundary. It is the same line, said in a way that leaves the other person with their dignity — which is the only reason they can follow it without needing revenge.`,deep:[`People hear the word "boundary" and picture a wall. The research on what actually holds is the opposite: boundaries that stick are the ones where the relationship feels safer after the sentence than before it. A line that costs someone face produces compliance and resentment, in that order, and the resentment usually outlives the compliance.`,`The mechanism is simple and almost embarrassingly polite. Warmth first: something true and specific that shows you have understood their position. Then the line, short and unhedged. Then a door: what you are offering instead, so they know the connection is not the thing being refused. Three beats, and the second one is the only one people practise.`,`The line itself must be a position, not a feeling, and not a negotiation. "I am not able to do that" is a position. "I would really rather not, if that is okay with you" is a negotiation you have already invited them to win. Most people who struggle with boundaries are not bad at saying no; they are bad at saying no without turning it into a question.`,`The hardest part is repetition. A warm boundary said once is often treated as a position to be argued with, not a fact — particularly with family, where the pattern of your life so far is that persistence works. The second saying is where the warmth has to stay identical and the line has to stay identical. Change either one and you have told them the line is negotiable.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`You can just do this one thing for me. It is not a big deal.`},{who:`You`,line:`I know it is not a big deal to you, and I can see why you would think that.`},{who:`Them`,line:`So? It would take you ten minutes.`},{who:`You`,line:`I am still not going to be able to do it. But I can send you the two names who would do it properly — do you want them now or tomorrow?`}],failureModes:[`Explaining. Every reason is a handle. The first reason invites a rebuttal; the fifth invites a debate about your character.`,`Apologising more than once. One "sorry" is warmth; three tells them you think the boundary is rude and invites them to agree.`,`The iced-over version. A cold, correct boundary is a very expensive way to win one exchange.`,`Changing the terms on the second ask. The second ask is the test, and everyone knows it. Say the same words again, in the same tone.`],drill:[`Write the boundary you have never said out loud, then write it in the three-beat form. Practise the line until you can say it without a question mark in your voice.`,`Say no once today, warm and short, with no reason attached. Notice how much less explaining is needed than you expected.`],related:[`lib-dc4`,`lib-fam1`,`lib-wm4`]},"lib-cr4":{thesis:`Interviewing is not a test of your career. It is a matching problem run twice — once by them, once by you — and almost everyone prepares only for their half.`,deep:[`The interviewer is not collecting your life story. They are collecting what the industry calls evidence: a small number of concrete, checkable instances that let them defend a hiring decision to someone who was not in the room. This is why an honest story with a number in it outperforms a polished story without one. You are not being assessed on how good your career sounds; you are being assessed on how easy you are to advocate for.`,`That reframes the hardest questions. "What is your biggest weakness?" is not an invitation to a humble-brag, it is a calibration check: can you see yourself accurately? "Tell me about a failure" is the same check with a story attached. "Why are you leaving?" is a test of whether you will badmouth your current employer, which is a proxy for what you will say about them. In every case the answer that works is shorter, more specific and more honest than the one most people rehearse.`,`The second half of the matching problem is yours, and it is the half people skip. One question about the actual constraint of the team — what has to be true in six months for this hire to have worked, and what usually stops it from being true — tells you more about the job than the entire job description, and tells them more about you than any of your answers. It also converts the interview from a test into a conversation between two people trying to decide something, which is the room you want to be in.`,`Two practical consequences. First, silence is legal: after a hard question, two seconds of thinking reads as substance, while an immediate answer reads as a reflex. Second, do not take the job on the strength of the interview alone. The interview is a sample of one person’s behaviour in a formal room. Ask to speak to someone who will be your peer, and ask them what they would change.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`Tell me about a time you failed.`},{who:`You`,line:`I shipped a migration two weeks late because I told the team it would take a week and never re-checked my own estimate. The cost was a frozen release and one very bad Friday.`},{who:`Them`,line:`What did you change?`},{who:`You`,line:`I now write the estimate down and check it with the person doing the work, not the person who wants it done. Which is why the second migration landed early.`},{who:`Them`,line:`Any questions for us?`},{who:`You`,line:`Two. What has to be true in six months for this hire to have worked, and what usually stops that from being true?`}],failureModes:[`Preparing answers and not questions. The questions are where you find out whether you want the job, and they are also the part interviewers remember.`,`Rehearsing until the answer sounds written. Rehearsed perfection reads as a lack of judgement about what mattered.`,`Naming a weakness that is secretly a strength. Everyone in the room has heard "I care too much" and it costs you more than an honest one would.`,`Treating the interview as the whole relationship. The first ninety days are the actual interview, and how you leave the room matters more than how you enter it.`],drill:[`Take your three strongest stories and compress each to 45 seconds with one number and one honest caveat. Time them out loud.`,`Write one question about their constraint. Ask it in your next interview and say nothing for five seconds after they answer.`],related:[`lib-pr2`,`lib-pr1`,`lib-pr5`]},"lib-cr5":{thesis:`The conversation after the conversation is where most of the damage happens. A single awkward exchange survives; an awkward exchange that nobody mentions grows into a theory about the other person.`,deep:[`When something goes slightly wrong between two people — a joke that missed, a decision made without somebody, a compliment that sounded like a slight — both parties run the same private process. They replay the moment, decide what it means about the other person, and then wait to see whether the other person brings it up. The waiting is the problem. Silence confirms the theory, because silence is compatible with both interpretations.`,`The repair after a small thing is therefore not an apology. It is a reference. "I have been thinking about what I said on Tuesday and I think it landed badly" costs one sentence and closes the loop. The point is not the awkwardness itself, which has already faded; the point is that the other person no longer has an unfinished sentence in their head with your name on it.`,`What makes this hard is a false economy. Bringing up something small feels like making it bigger, so the instinct is to let it go. But the other person has already made it bigger. You are not choosing between a small thing and a big thing; you are choosing between a small thing now and an unspoken thing that will shape how they read your next three messages.`,`There is one exception worth knowing: do not perform repair for an audience. The public "I am sorry if anyone was offended" is a demonstration for the room, not a repair for the person. If somebody was actually hurt, they need a version of the conversation with nobody else in it.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Can I say one thing about Tuesday? I made that joke about the deadline and I heard how it landed a second after I said it.`},{who:`Them`,line:`It is fine, honestly.`},{who:`You`,line:`Good — but I wanted you to know I noticed it, rather than leaving you to wonder whether I did.`}],failureModes:[`The pre-emptive apology. Apologising constantly for things nobody noticed trains people to see you as fragile.`,`Repairing by text when it deserves a voice. Text makes the small thing feel formal and gives them time to draft a reply they do not mean.`,`Asking "are we okay?" — it puts the burden of reassurance on the person who was on the receiving end.`,`Deciding the other person is oversensitive. It is a convenient conclusion that makes further conversation unnecessary, which is exactly why it is tempting.`],drill:[`Pick one moment from the last fortnight you have been quietly replaying and say the one-sentence reference out loud. Then actually send it, today.`,`In your next three conversations, when you notice the other person pause oddly, name it: "that landed differently than I meant — what did you hear?"`],related:[`lib-cr2`,`lib-dc1`,`lib-hm3`]},"lib-cr6":{thesis:`Delegation fails at the sentence, not at the trust. Most people delegate a task and accidentally keep the decisions, then wonder why the work comes back as a draft of their own thinking.`,deep:[`A delegation has three parts — the outcome, the constraints, and the decisions the other person is allowed to make without asking — and almost everyone says only the first. "Can you put together the vendor comparison?" sounds complete and is missing both the standard of done and the boundary of authority. The person receiving it has to guess, and the safe guess is to produce something that can be corrected, which is why you get a draft instead of a decision.`,`The second failure is invisible and more expensive: delegating the work while retaining the anxiety. You hand over the task and then check in daily, ask for early drafts, and rewrite the parts you would have done differently. The person learns two things — that the task is theirs and that the outcome is yours — and the second lesson is the one that sticks. Next time they wait to be told.`,`What good delegation sounds like is deliberately unpolished: "The outcome is a one-page recommendation with a number on it. You can decide who you talk to and which three vendors you shortlist. The only thing I need to approve is the recommendation itself, and I do not want to see a draft before Thursday." Four sentences, and each one removes a decision they would otherwise have had to guess at.`,`The last piece is the follow-up, which should be about the work and not about reassurance. "What do you need from me?" is a better question than "how is it going?" — the first presumes the task is theirs and offers support; the second invites a status report and quietly reinstates you as the person who has to be satisfied.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`I want you to own the vendor decision. The outcome is one page, three options, a recommendation and a number.`},{who:`Them`,line:`Who do I need to check with?`},{who:`You`,line:`Nobody. You choose who you speak to and you pick the shortlist. I approve the recommendation on Thursday and I will not ask for a draft before then.`},{who:`Them`,line:`What if I get it wrong?`},{who:`You`,line:`Then we will have found out on Thursday instead of in June. What do you need from me to start?`}],failureModes:[`Delegating the task and keeping the credit. It is the fastest way to make a capable person stop volunteering.`,`The "just a quick update" check-in every morning. It is a leash with a friendly name.`,`Rewriting the output silently. Half-delegation is worse than no delegation: they lose the learning and you lose the hours.`,`Delegating to the person who is already overloaded because they are the one you trust. Reliability is not infinite, and this is how your best person becomes your former best person.`],drill:[`Take one thing you are currently doing yourself and write the four sentences: outcome, constraints, decisions they own, when you will look. Send them today.`,`For one week, ask "what do you need from me?" instead of "how is it going?" in every handover conversation.`],related:[`lib-pr4`,`lib-pr2`,`lib-dc2`]},"lib-cr7":{thesis:`An honest no is not the opposite of a good relationship. It is the maintenance schedule for one — because every yes you did not mean becomes a debt that eventually gets paid with interest.`,deep:[`Most people say yes for one of three reasons: the request arrived in a warm moment, the alternative is a two-second silence that feels like disappointment, or they are running a private rule that a good person is available. All three are ways of spending your future self's time to buy a small amount of comfort now, and all three are invisible to the person asking.`,`The mechanics of a no that survives contact with real people are consistent. Acknowledge the ask specifically, so they know you actually heard it rather than reflexively declining. Give the shortest true reason, or none at all — a reason is not required, and a long one is an invitation to negotiate. Offer what you can genuinely do, if anything, and make it smaller than what was asked. Then stop. The stop matters because the negotiation is rarely in the request; it is in the pause afterwards.`,`There is a version of honesty that is worse than a vague yes, and it is worth being precise about. "I would love to but I am completely swamped and I feel terrible" is a no surrounded by so much feeling that the other person ends up managing you. Honesty about your capacity is a gift. Honesty about your guilt is a request for care dressed as transparency.`,`The long-run effect is the counter-intuitive part. People trust a clear no more than a soft yes, because a clear no is information they can plan around. The person who says yes to everything becomes slightly unreliable in a way nobody says out loud — you stop being the person they ask when the answer matters, and start being the person they ask when it does not.`],script:[{who:`Them`,line:`Could you take on the client review this quarter? I know you are busy.`},{who:`You`,line:`I cannot take the review this quarter — I am already carrying two that I would have to drop, and dropping them would be worse for everyone.`},{who:`Them`,line:`It would really help me out.`},{who:`You`,line:`I know, and I am sorry. What I can do is read your summary on Thursday morning and tell you if it is ready. That is genuinely all I have.`}],failureModes:[`The no with a runway: three sentences of preamble before the actual word, so the request has time to gather support.`,`The false yes you plan to wriggle out of later. It is still a no; it just costs the other person a week and your reputation a little more.`,`Offering a reason that can be solved. "I am swamped this week" invites "then do it next week" — say the constraint that is actually fixed.`,`Saying no to the person and yes to the guilt, then doing the task badly at midnight. The worst of every option.`],drill:[`Write your default no in three sentences and keep it somewhere you can find it under pressure. The reason you need it in advance is that the moment itself is not a thinking moment.`,`Say no to one small request today with no reason attached, and stay in the conversation afterwards. The staying is the skill.`],related:[`lib-cr3`,`lib-dc4`,`lib-mny1`]},"lib-cr8":{thesis:`You cannot control how a message lands. You can control whether the person receiving it has enough context to build a fair version of it — and most conversational damage is a context problem wearing the costume of a personality problem.`,deep:[`Every message gets read through three things: your history with the sender, your current state, and whatever you assume about their intent. None of these are in the message. That is why the same sentence is affectionate from one person and barbed from another, and why "it was obviously a joke" is such a useless defence — obviousness is exactly the thing that varies.`,`The practical implication is to send context along with content. "Short reply, not annoyed — I am typing this on a train" takes eight words and removes an entire interpretive branch. Naming your state is not oversharing; it is the equivalent of street lighting. The people who are easiest to work with are usually not the warmest, they are the most legible.`,`The other half of this is reading generously without becoming naive. If a message is ambiguous and the relationship is good, assume the better reading and check it lightly: "I read that as a bit sharp — was that the intention?" This is not weakness; it is refusing to let an ambiguity quietly become a fact. The naive version is assuming the better reading and never checking; the cynical version is assuming the worse one and never checking. Both leave the same unfinished sentence in the room.`,`And when you are the one being misread, the correction is one sentence, not a defence. "That came out sharper than I meant" repairs it. Explaining what you actually meant, at length, usually confirms the impression that you are more interested in being understood than in the effect you had.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`Quick one before you read the rest: I am on a train and typing with one thumb, so if this reads blunt, it is the thumb.`},{who:`Them`,line:`Got it. The deadline thing — is that a real deadline or a wish?`},{who:`You`,line:`Real, and it is not mine. Which is why I am asking rather than telling.`}],failureModes:[`Assuming a text has a tone. It does not — you supplied one, and then argued for it.`,`Long explanations of intent when the other person has already told you the effect. The effect is the data; your intent is the defence.`,`Reading everything generously and checking nothing. Context is not the same as conflict-avoidance.`,`Naming your state as an excuse: "I am stressed, so…" explains the message and excuses nothing. Say what happened, not what you were feeling when it happened badly.`],drill:[`In your next three messages that could be misread, add one clause of context: your state, your channel, or your confidence level. Notice which one people respond to.`,`Take one ambiguous message you received this week and write the two readings — the generous one and the cynical one. Then ask the light question instead of choosing.`],related:[`lib-cr5`,`lib-dc1`,`lib-pr5`]}},p=e({LIBRARY_EXTRA_DEEP:()=>m}),m={"lib-st1":{thesis:`Small talk is not a test of wit. It is a joint activity of finding something to look at together.`,deep:[`Most people treat an opening line as a performance they must pass. That is why they freeze: the imagined audience is judging content. The truth is that openers are judged on safety, not cleverness. The other person is running one question underneath your words — is this going to cost me something? An observation about the shared situation answers that question with a no.`,`Shared reality is powerful because it cannot be wrong. The queue, the weather, the venue, the dog, the awful coffee, the announcement nobody can hear — you both already agree. Agreement, even trivial agreement, is the first beat of rapport, and you get it for free.`,`The mistake is turning the observation into a question immediately. "That queue is slow, is it not?" is a closed door with a question mark. Instead, offer the observation and let it sit for a second: "This queue is moving like it is being paid by the hour." Most people will pick it up. If they do not, you have lost nothing and confirmed only that they are not available — which is information, not rejection.`,`Read the response for investment, not warmth. Politened smiling costs nothing; adding a detail ("I have been here twenty minutes, I am starting to take it personally") is a small investment. That is your permission to continue, and it arrives within about ten seconds.`],script:[{line:`"This queue is moving like it is being paid by the hour."`,note:`Observation, shared, low risk.`},{line:`Them: "I have been here twenty minutes. I am starting to take it personally."`,note:`They invested. Continue.`},{line:`"Twenty minutes. What are you here for that is worth that?"`,note:`Fork: work door or person door.`},{line:`"If I am still here in ten, I am going to start reviewing the coffee."`,note:`Light, no demand, keeps the exit open.`}],failureModes:[`Opening with a compliment on appearance — it demands a response about her body before you have any standing.`,`Asking a closed question and then leaving a four-second gap, which reads as an interrogation with no answer key.`,`Opening with something that requires the other person to be witty back (a joke, a riddle, a debate prompt). You have made them work.`],drill:[`Three shared-reality openers in one day, to anyone: a barista, a colleague, someone in a queue. You are not trying to start a friendship, only to hear yourself say the sentence out loud.`,`Record which opener got a detail added back. That is the only metric that matters.`,`Say the observation and then deliberately say nothing for one beat. Notice how strong the urge is to rescue it.`],related:[`lib-st2`,`lib-st6`,`lib-wm1`]},"lib-st2":{thesis:`Every answer contains two doors. Most people thank the person for the answer and walk away from the building.`,deep:[`Conversation dies at the point where a question can be fully answered in four words — "I am a nurse", "not much", "it was fine". That is not because the other person is boring. It is because you asked a question with a single exit.`,`A fork is a question with two honest paths, and the second one is usually the personal one. "What made you go into nursing — or did it choose you?" Now they can answer with the job or with the story. The word "or" is the mechanism: it signals that you are interested in the person, not the fact.`,`The second door is almost always about choice, change, or origin. Why this, how did you get here, what was it before, what would you do instead. Work is the door everyone opens; the person behind the work is the door worth opening.`,`The discipline is to stop treating an answer as the end of a transaction and start treating it as a map. You are not looking for the next clever question; you are looking for which word in their answer has heat in it. Heat sounds like a longer sentence, a change of pace, or a small complaint.`],script:[{line:`Them: "I am in accounting."`,note:`Flat answer, single exit.`},{line:`"What kind — the forensic side, or the keeping-the-lights-on side?"`,note:`Fork one: work door.`},{line:`Them: "Honestly? Keeping the lights on. It is boring in a way I have started to like."`,note:`Heat: they volunteered an opinion.`},{line:`"That is the most interesting thing anyone has said about accounting. What does boring do for you?"`,note:`Go one level deeper, not sideways.`}],failureModes:[`Firing fork after fork without ever giving anything back — that is an interview with better questions.`,`Forks that are really opinions: "What made you pick accounting — or did you just fall into it?" said with a smirk is a judgement wearing a question mark.`,`Chasing a new topic the moment the current one gets quiet, instead of letting one topic deepen.`],drill:[`Take one ordinary answer today and find both doors out loud: "what made you choose it — or did it choose you?"`,`In your next three conversations, count how many times you changed topic instead of going deeper. Changing topic is the tell.`,`Write down the last three things someone told you. For each, write the deeper question you did not ask. That is your map for next time.`],related:[`lib-st1`,`lib-f4`,`lib-ei3`]},"lib-st3":{thesis:`Two questions then a disclosure. That is the difference between an interview and a conversation.`,deep:[`People who find small talk hard usually have one of two patterns: they ask and ask until it feels like an interrogation, or they talk and talk until it feels like a monologue. Both are the same failure — an imbalance in the trading of self.`,`Conversation runs on reciprocity of disclosure. Two of their answers, then one of yours. Not a competing story, not advice, not a summary of what they said — a small, honest thing about you that sits on the same shelf as theirs.`,`The disclosure should be slightly less impressive than it could be. "I only moved here two years ago and I still do not know where to get good bread" is better than a story about your promotion, because it makes you approachable and gives them something to help with. Status that nobody asked for is a wall.`,`The rhythm also protects you. If you have given something and they have given nothing back after two rounds, the honest reading is that they are not available — and you can leave warmly without having performed for twenty minutes.`],script:[{line:`"How long have you been in the area?"`,note:`Question one.`},{line:`Them: "Three years. I came for work."`,note:`Short but with a hook.`},{line:`"What was the move like — did it get easier or harder after the first year?"`,note:`Question two, deeper.`},{line:`"I moved two years ago and I still do not know where to get good bread, so you are ahead of me."`,note:`Disclosure: small, honest, no status play.`}],failureModes:[`Turning their disclosure into a competition ("you think that is bad, I once…"). It ends the exchange and marks you as someone who cannot be confided in.`,`Disclosing something enormous too early, which hands them a weight they did not agree to carry.`,`Offering advice where a disclosure was invited. "You should try…" is the fastest way to make someone stop talking.`],drill:[`In one conversation today, hold to the rule: two questions, one disclosure. Count them.`,`Make your disclosure about ten percent less impressive than the truth. Notice what happens to the other person's body language.`,`Write three one-line disclosures you are comfortable offering to a stranger: where you live and how long, what you do badly, what you are currently trying to learn.`],related:[`lib-st2`,`lib-ei1`,`lib-f3`]},"lib-st4":{thesis:`Exits are remembered longer than openings. Leave while the conversation is still good.`,deep:[`Most people do not struggle to start conversations nearly as much as they struggle to end them. So they wait for the conversation to die — and the last thing that happens between you is an awkward fade, which is what gets remembered.`,`A warm exit has three parts: you name the departure, you give a generous reason, and you leave one thread hanging. "I am going to let you go before I talk your ear off. But I want to hear how Thursday goes — find me later." Nothing about that is a rejection of the other person.`,`The generous reason is the load-bearing part. "I should go" reads as escape. "I want to let you have some of the evening" reads as care, even though both are true at once. You are not lying; you are choosing which true thing to say.`,`The hanging thread is what turns an exit into a future. It gives the other person a reason to come back to you and a script for doing it, which is exactly what someone who finds people difficult needs — one more point of contact that is not cold.`],script:[{line:`"I am going to let you go before I talk your ear off."`,note:`Name it, generously.`},{line:`"But I want to hear how Thursday goes — come and find me when you know."`,note:`Leave the thread hanging.`},{line:`"It was genuinely good to meet you, Sanjay."`,note:`Name them. Names are remembered.`},{line:`[Leave. Do not re-open, do not hover at the edge of the group.]`,note:`The exit only works if it is an exit.`}],failureModes:[`The slow fade — drifting backwards while still talking, which is the most awkward possible ending.`,`Over-explaining: three reasons why you must go, which signals that you believe you need permission.`,`Leaving with a promise you will not keep ("we must get coffee") which converts a warm exit into a small lie.`],drill:[`Practise the exit twice today at low stakes: end a phone call, end a chat at the water cooler. Say the sentence out loud.`,`After one conversation, leave at the high point rather than the natural death. Notice your discomfort — that discomfort is the habit breaking.`,`Write your own version of the three-part exit and use the identical words for a week, until the shape is automatic.`],related:[`lib-f6`,`lib-pr5`,`lib-st5`]},"lib-st5":{thesis:`A small story is three sentences and a stop. Most people add eleven sentences and a moral.`,deep:[`In conversation, you are not competing with silence — you are competing with everyone else who wants the floor. Brevity is not modesty, it is the reason you get asked for another one.`,`The shape that works in speech is smaller than the shape that works on a page: setup in one sentence (who, where), complication in one (what went wrong or strange), turn in one (what happened instead). Three sentences, then stop, before the moral.`,`The stop is the technique. When you stop without explaining what it meant, the listener leans in, and someone will usually ask the question the story was designed to provoke. The story becomes a conversation instead of a performance. If you have ever felt people drift during your stories, the problem is almost always the last two sentences.`,`Choose small over dramatic. A story about a bus driver who waited, shouted, then bought you a coffee is more useful socially than a story about the worst year of your life, because it costs nothing to receive and reveals a way of seeing the world.`],script:[{line:`"The driver waited for me at the lights, which never happens."`,note:`Setup, one sentence.`},{line:`"Then he shouted at me for holding up the whole route."`,note:`Complication.`},{line:`"Then he bought me a coffee at the end of the line. I have been loyal to that route ever since."`,note:`Turn and stop. No moral.`},{line:`[Silence. Let them react or ask.]`,note:`The pause is where the conversation happens.`}],failureModes:[`The moral. "The lesson is that people are complicated." Now it is a lecture with characters.`,`Front-loading context — four sentences of who everyone is before anything happens.`,`The escalation habit: adding details to make it bigger, which reads as exaggeration and costs you belief in everything else.`],drill:[`Write three true three-sentence stories from the last month. Rehearse them out loud until the stop feels natural.`,`In one conversation, tell a story and then physically stop — hands still, no filler. Count how many seconds before someone asked a question.`,`Cut a story you already tell by half. Notice which half was carrying the weight.`],related:[`lib-st4`,`lib-c3`,`lib-w3`]},"lib-st6":{thesis:`Silence is not a verdict on you. It is a room the other person may be about to walk into.`,deep:[`People who find conversation hard tend to experience a two-second pause as an accusation. So they fill it — with a new topic, an apology, a nervous joke. Each rescue tells the other person that silence is dangerous, and so the conversation speeds up until it has nowhere to go.`,`The physiological truth: your discomfort peaks around second two and falls by second five. If you can stay, you teach your own nervous system that a pause is survivable, and you teach the other person that they do not have to perform either.`,`Silence also does work that words cannot. It gives the other person space to reconsider, to add the thing they had decided not to say, or to answer more honestly than their first answer. Most of the best things people tell you arrive in the half-second after you would have interrupted.`,`The trick is to make the pause active rather than absent: stay looking at them, keep your face open and undemanding, breathe out. A pause with attention is confidence. A pause with a phone, a glance away, or an anxious laugh is a withdrawal — same silence, opposite meaning.`],script:[{line:`"What was the hardest part of that year?"`,note:`The question.`},{line:`[Two full seconds of nothing. Eyes level, breathe out.]`,note:`The pause, held.`},{line:`Them: "Honestly? The part nobody saw. I was the one holding it together for everyone."`,note:`The sentence the pause bought.`},{line:`"Say more about that."`,note:`Four words. Do not rescue.`}],failureModes:[`Filling the pause with a summary of what they just said, which signals impatience in the friendliest possible wrapper.`,`Using silence as a weapon — holding it with a hard face to make someone uncomfortable. That is interrogation, not conversation.`,`Apologising for the pause ("sorry, I am not very good at this"), which converts a moment of presence into a request for reassurance.`],drill:[`In three conversations today, wait two seconds after they finish before you answer. Count them out mentally.`,`Practise "say more about that" once. Notice that it is always enough.`,`Sit in a cafe for five minutes and listen to two people talk. Count how rarely either of them pauses. That is the norm you are correcting.`],related:[`lib-st1`,`lib-f5`,`lib-ei3`]},"lib-wm1":{thesis:`Approach as someone calm, not someone brave. The situation is ordinary; treat it that way.`,deep:[`Men who find this hard tend to have built an enormous frame around it: an approach is a test they might fail, a performance that must be earned, a risk of humiliation. That frame is why it feels terrifying and why the behaviour it produces is strange — rehearsed lines, over-loud energy, a manic kind of confidence that everyone can hear.`,`The reframe that actually helps: you are not asking her to like you, you are starting a conversation to find out whether you like each other's company. That flips the direction of evaluation, and it also happens to be the only honest version of what is going on.`,`That is why the boring approach wins. A remark about the shared situation, a sincere question, an offer of help — each of these requires her to do nothing except respond naturally. Exciting approaches ask her to manage your ego as well as the conversation, which is precisely the cost most women are trying to avoid.`,`Finally: it is a numbers game, and not in the cynical sense. Most conversations you start will lead nowhere, because most people do not match, and that is true regardless of how good you are. Expecting a 30% success rate removes the belief that every no is a verdict on your worth.`],script:[{line:`Approach at an angle, not head-on, and not from behind. Stop at a comfortable distance.`,note:`Body first, words second.`},{line:`"Excuse me — I have been trying to work out what that book is. Is it any good?"`,note:`Specific, situational, answerable.`},{line:`Them: "It is terrible, honestly."`,note:`Any answer is a good answer.`},{line:`"Then I will leave it. I am Prabesh, by the way."`,note:`Name and a light close. No outcome demanded.`}],failureModes:[`Approaching with a "technique" voice — a register you do not use with anyone else, which she will hear instantly.`,`Standing in a way that blocks her exit or leaning into her space. She should never have to manoeuvre to leave.`,`Continuing after two flat, uninvested answers. That is not persistence, it is pressure.`],drill:[`Start three conversations with strangers today where the goal is exactly one exchange, with no attempt to continue. Get used to the sound of your own opening.`,`Go somewhere you have a genuine reason to be. A reason makes the approach ordinary.`,`Write down what you are actually afraid of. Most answers are "that she will think I am creepy" — which is why consent-first behaviour is a strategy, not just an ethic.`],related:[`lib-wm4`,`lib-wm5`,`lib-st1`]},"lib-wm2":{thesis:`Specific praise proves attention. General praise proves only that you were looking.`,deep:[`Women receive a great deal of commentary on their appearance and rather little observation of their choices. "You are beautiful" lands in a category that has been used on her by strangers, salesmen and men who wanted something — even when you mean it sincerely, it arrives pre-used.`,`A specific compliment does something structurally different: it can only be said by someone who was paying attention. "You were the only person who laughed at that joke" is evidence. Evidence is what credibility is made of, in every domain of communication.`,`Praise the choice, the mind, the craft, the taste, the courage — and make it concrete enough that it cannot be recycled for anyone else. "You are so easy to talk to" is pleasant but generic. "You asked me the one question nobody asks" is specific and therefore worth believing.`,`One per conversation, sincere, and never a prelude to a request. The moment a compliment becomes a down-payment, it stops being a compliment and becomes a tactic — and people are remarkably good at detecting tactics.`],script:[{line:`"You laughed at the joke nobody else caught. I noticed."`,note:`Choice, not appearance.`},{line:`Them: "It was the only funny thing anyone said."`,note:`She is engaging — good.`},{line:`"That is the nicest thing anyone has said to me about my terrible joke."`,note:`Play, not pressure.`},{line:`[Resist the urge to add a second compliment. One is credible; two is a sales pitch.]`,note:`The stop is part of it.`}],failureModes:[`Complimenting appearance as the first thing out of your mouth — it defines her by her body before you know her name.`,`Compliments that compare her to other women ("you are not like the others"), which is a slight wearing a compliment's coat.`,`Complimenting then immediately asking for something (a number, a date), which retroactively converts the compliment into currency.`],drill:[`Give three specific compliments this week — to women, men, anyone. Specific enough that the person could not recycle the sentence for someone else.`,`After each one, say nothing more and observe. Practise the stop.`,`Write one sentence about a person you know that is true, specific, and about a choice rather than a feature. Say it to them.`],related:[`lib-c3`,`lib-wm3`,`lib-st3`]},"lib-wm3":{thesis:`Move one rung at a time, and check for the nod before the next rung.`,deep:[`Flirtation is not a speech; it is a conversation with a direction. What most men lack is not courage but calibration — the sense of how fast to move, and the willingness to stop when the answer is not a yes.`,`The ladder: warmth (an ordinary friendly exchange), curiosity (real questions about her), play (a light tease or a callback, given gently), personal (a question that costs something to answer), invitation (a clear, low-cost ask). One rung at a time, with a beat in between.`,`The check is reciprocation. If she matches the rung — teases back, asks you something real, adds detail she did not need to add — you may climb. If she does not, hold at the current rung and enjoy what you actually have. Two unmatched rungs in a row means the ladder ends here, and that is not failure; it means you found out early.`,`This is also the ethical core of flirting, not a technique for avoiding consequences. Making the temperature something you keep checking rather than something you announce is what consent actually looks like in a conversation: continuous, mutual, and revocable at any rung.`],script:[{line:`Warmth: "You have been here longer than me — is the coffee worth the queue?"`,note:`Rung zero.`},{line:`Curiosity: "What is the thing you are trying to do this year that nobody asks about?"`,note:`Rung one. Real question.`},{line:`Play: "So you are one of those people who reads the manual. I am one of the ones who complains about the manual."`,note:`Rung two — gentle, about you both, no insult.`},{line:`Them: "I have been complaining about the manual for two years." → Invitation: "I am going to be here again on Thursday. You should tell me whether you made it work."`,note:`Invitation, low cost, easy to decline.`}],failureModes:[`Skipping to rung four (a date request) with no curiosity and no play — it lands as a cold transaction.`,`Teasing as a way to test whether she likes you. A joke that stings is not flirting, it is a power move with a smile.`,`Reframing a clear no as a challenge. "Her loss" or "she was playing hard to get" is how people talk themselves into being unsafe.`],drill:[`Practise the ladder with warmth and curiosity only, on three people you have no romantic interest in. The skill is the calibration, not the outcome.`,`After every conversation, write which rung you reached and where the reciprocation stopped. Do it five times; your calibration improves faster than any other practice.`,`Write down, honestly, what you do when someone does not reciprocate. That behaviour is your whole character.`],related:[`lib-wm2`,`lib-wm5`,`lib-wm4`]},"lib-wm4":{thesis:`How you receive a no is what the next woman hears about you.`,deep:[`A refusal is over in three seconds, but how you handle it is what gets remembered — by her, and by the people who saw. "No problem at all, enjoy your evening" costs you nothing and marks you as safe. Anything else costs you your standing in a room you may return to.`,`The reflex to explain yourself, to negotiate, or to withdraw visibly all come from treating the no as a verdict about your worth. It is not. It is one person, in one moment, with information you do not have. Someone has to be the person who accepted it graciously — that person gets to try again tomorrow.`,`Practise the physical recovery too: shoulder down, face easy, step back and away rather than turning sharply. The body delivers the message before the sentence lands.`,`And do not narrate it afterwards. Telling a group "she shot me down" converts your private disappointment into her public trial. Men who do that wonder why women avoid their group.`],script:[{line:`Them: "No, I am fine, thank you."`,note:`The no.`},{line:`"No problem at all. Enjoy your evening."`,note:`Five seconds, warm, complete.`},{line:`[Step back, turn at an angle, return to what you were doing.]`,note:`Body confirms the words.`},{line:`[Say nothing further. Not a long look, not a sigh, not "your loss".]`,note:`The silence is the test.`}],failureModes:[`Negotiating: "No pressure, just a coffee" is the same request with an argument attached.`,`Punishing her socially — commenting to your friends, joking at her expense, going cold and pointed. Everyone sees it.`,`Over-apologising, which makes your self-worth her responsibility and turns a short moment into a long one.`],drill:[`Ask for something small with a high chance of refusal this week — a discount, a spare seat, a favour. Practise the warm no.`,`Rehearse the exact sentence "no problem at all, enjoy your evening" until it needs no thought.`,`Notice any urge to narrate the refusal to someone later. That narration is the habit to break.`],related:[`lib-wm1`,`lib-wm5`,`lib-dc5`]},"lib-wm5":{thesis:`Friendliness is a default. Interest costs something. Look for what she spent.`,deep:[`The most common failure in this arena is not boldness or shyness — it is misreading politeness as attraction. Professional warmth, customer service, and social habit all produce smiling, nodding and answering. None of it is a signal.`,`Investment is different because it costs something. She asks a question back. She adds detail you did not request ("my sister is a nurse too, she said the same"). Her answers get longer as the conversation continues. She keeps the thread alive when you pause. She returns your tease. She stays when she could leave, or she asks where you are going next.`,`Look for the pattern, not the moment. One long answer could be a chatty day; three increasing messages, plus a question, plus a re-initiation, is a pattern, and patterns are what you act on.`,`Then verify with one low-cost invitation — one specific, refusable, low-stakes ask. A warm yes is a yes. "Maybe, I am not sure, I will let you know" is a no, and there is no next move; the kindest and strongest thing available is to accept it and behave the same way as before. Assuming a warm yes is the whole skill; everything else is noise.`],script:[{line:`[They have asked you two questions and added unrequested detail.]`,note:`Investment is present.`},{line:`"There is a place on that same street that does a decent version of this. Do you want to escape here for an hour on Saturday?"`,note:`One specific ask, easy to refuse.`},{line:`Them: "I would actually, yes."`,note:`Warm yes. That is the answer you needed.`},{line:`Them: "Maybe — I have a lot on, I will let you know." → "No worries at all." Then behave exactly as before.`,note:`Not a warm yes. Treat as a no, with grace.`}],failureModes:[`Reading a service-industry smile as interest — she is at work, and that is her job.`,`Escalating the invitation because the first one was soft: "just a quick coffee" after a no, then "a drink", then "why not".`,`Testing her interest by touching or crowding. There is no verdict at the end of that road that makes it acceptable.`],drill:[`Over the next week, note three conversations where someone invested. Write exactly what they did. Build a personal list of what investment looks like.`,`Practise one low-cost invitation in a non-romantic setting (an offer to a colleague, a friend) to get comfortable with a refusable ask.`,`Keep a list of the times something was not a warm yes and you treated it as one. This is the single fastest way to spare yourself and others.`],related:[`lib-wm3`,`lib-wm4`,`lib-ei3`]},"lib-dc1":{thesis:`Start from the third story — the version a neutral observer could tell — and neither party has to be the problem.`,deep:[`In a difficult conversation each person arrives with a story in which they are reasonable and the other person is the obstacle. Opening with that story forces the other person to defend before they can listen. That is why "I feel that you have been ignoring me" produces the argument you were trying to avoid.`,`The third story is the version an outsider would describe: "I think we are trying to achieve your deadline and my quality bar, and we are each getting frustrated with the other." It is factually acceptable to both sides, and it puts the disagreement in the middle of the table rather than in the chest of one person.`,`Next, name your contribution without waiting to be accused. "I have been avoiding the conversation, and the silence has probably read as sulking." Owning your part first is not weakness; it removes the need for the other person to establish your guilt, which is the thing that makes them loud.`,`Then be clear about what you actually want. "I am not asking for an apology, I am asking for a different way of doing the next one." Vague feelings need a concrete request, or the conversation ends with everyone emotional and nothing changed.`],script:[{line:`"I think we want different things from this project and we are each getting frustrated. Can we look at it together?"`,note:`The third story.`},{line:`"I have been avoiding this conversation, so the silence has been my part."`,note:`Own the contribution first.`},{line:`"What does a good outcome look like for you from here?"`,note:`Their interest, out loud, without your rebuttal.`},{line:`"So what I am asking for is X for the next two weeks, and X for me is Y. Does that work for you?"`,note:`A concrete request from a concrete offer.`}],failureModes:[`Leading with the accusation and then suggesting "we talk like adults" — the third story cannot survive an opening attack.`,`Using the frame as a trick while still needing to win, which people detect within two sentences.`,`Skipping the concrete request, so the conversation feels resolved and the behaviour does not change.`],drill:[`Take a current irritation and write it as a third story in one sentence. If it contains blame, rewrite it.`,`Name your own contribution to a mess this week — at work or at home — before anyone names it for you.`,`Ask "what does a good outcome look like for you?" in one difficult conversation and then say nothing until they finish.`],related:[`lib-dc2`,`lib-dc3`,`lib-ei2`]},"lib-dc2":{thesis:`Observation, impact, request — a boundary stated without a verdict on anyone's character.`,deep:[`Most boundaries fail at the wording. "You are always late" is a character claim with "always" in it, so the argument becomes about whether you are right to say it — and about all the times they were not late. The actual request never gets heard.`,`The three-part form fixes this. Observation: what a camera would record, with no "always" and no interpretation. Impact: what it cost you, concretely and personally. Request: what you want instead, phrased so it can be accepted or refused.`,`Impact is the part people skip, and it is the part that makes the request legitimate. Without it the request sounds arbitrary; with it, you are not demanding a preference, you are reporting an effect and proposing a fix.`,`The request must be doable and re-answerable. "I need a heads-up by Thursday" can be agreed to and checked. "I need you to respect my time" sounds stronger and means nothing in practice, so nothing changes and you will be having this conversation again in a month.`],script:[{line:`"When the report came in on Monday rather than Thursday,"`,note:`Observation. No always, no verdict.`},{line:`"I had to apologise to the client and rebuild the schedule over the weekend."`,note:`Impact — concrete, personal, non-accusatory.`},{line:`"Going forward I need a heads-up by Thursday. Can you do that?"`,note:`Request, doable and refusable.`},{line:`If they refuse: "Then I need to change how the client work is scheduled. I wanted to tell you before I did that."`,note:`A boundary without the request is only a preference.`}],failureModes:[`Burying the request in a paragraph of feelings, so the listener cannot find the action item.`,`Using "I feel" as a shield for an accusation: "I feel that you do not respect me" is still a character claim.`,`Threatening consequences you will not enforce, which teaches people exactly how seriously to take your boundaries.`],drill:[`Take one recurring irritation and write it in three sentences: observation, impact, request. Read it aloud. Cut any word that is a verdict.`,`Deliver one small boundary this week where the stakes are low, so the form becomes familiar before you need it.`,`Notice the word "always" in your speech today and replace it with a date.`],related:[`lib-dc1`,`lib-dc4`,`lib-pr4`]},"lib-dc3":{thesis:`An apology that asks for forgiveness is not finished. Name it, own it, repair it, change it.`,deep:[`Most apologies are actually explanations: "I am sorry, but I was exhausted and you know how I get". The "but" moves the cause from your choice to your circumstances, and the other person hears that they are being asked to accept a reason instead of an apology.`,`The complete version has four parts and no "but". Name what you did specifically, without softening it. Own the effect on them. Repair anything repairable, which usually means a public correction if the harm was public. And state what will be different, not as a promise about your character but as a change in behaviour.`,`Intent is the trap. "I did not mean it that way" is true almost always and irrelevant almost always — the harm happened at the receiving end. If you want to include intent, do it after ownership, as information rather than as a defence.`,`And then stop. Do not ask whether they are okay with you, do not seek reassurance, do not require them to accept it on your timeline. The apology is complete when you have said it; their acceptance is theirs to schedule.`],script:[{line:`"I said your work was sloppy in front of the team. That was out of order."`,note:`Specific, unsoftened, no but.`},{line:`"It embarrassed you in front of people whose opinion matters to you. That is on me."`,note:`Own the effect.`},{line:`"I have already told them that was mine to have raised privately."`,note:`Repair what is repairable.`},{line:`"The next one I bring to you first, before anyone else hears it."`,note:`Changed behaviour, checkable.`}],failureModes:[`The conditional apology ("I am sorry if you felt…") which apologises for their feelings rather than your action.`,`The fast apology designed to end your own discomfort — audible to everyone as relief, not repair.`,`Apologising and then re-litigating the disagreement in the same breath, which un-apologises what you just said.`],drill:[`Write one outstanding apology in four sentences. Cut every "but", "if", and "you know how I am".`,`Deliver the smallest real one this week — the longer it waits, the more it costs.`,`Next time you catch yourself explaining, stop mid-sentence and say the ownership line instead.`],related:[`lib-dc1`,`lib-ei4`,`lib-f2`]},"lib-dc4":{thesis:`A no with an alternative and a preference is a higher-status answer than a reluctant yes.`,deep:[`Most people say yes because refusing feels like conflict. Then they deliver badly, resent the asker, and the relationship quietly degrades — which is a worse outcome than the two minutes of discomfort a clear no would have cost.`,`The one-job no refuses the request while protecting the relationship: you name your constraint honestly, you offer what you can genuinely do, and you ask them to choose. That structure tells the other person you take them seriously enough to negotiate with rather than manage.`,`The critical ingredient is the preference. "I can do either, whatever works" hands the decision back and lets them optimise at your expense. "I would rather hold Friday" is a position, and positions are what make you someone worth negotiating with.`,`You will be tested on this by people who are used to your yes. Expect one round of pressure; answer it with the same sentence, calmly, rather than a new justification. A no that changes shape under pressure was never a boundary, only a preference with better marketing.`],script:[{line:`"I want to be straight with you: if I take this on this week, the thing I promised by Friday slips."`,note:`Name the constraint, not an excuse.`},{line:`"I can hold Friday, or hand this to Priya. Which would you rather?"`,note:`Offer what is genuinely possible.`},{line:`"I would rather hold Friday, honestly."`,note:`A preference is the load-bearing part.`},{line:`Pressure: "It is just a small thing, though." → "It is small, and it is still the thing that makes Friday slip. So: hold Friday, or Priya?"`,note:`Same sentence, not a new argument.`}],failureModes:[`The apologetic no that offers five reasons, each of which can be argued with.`,`Saying yes and then not delivering — a slow no paid for by your credibility.`,`The no that turns into a referendum on the relationship: "you always dump things on me at the last minute."`],drill:[`Refuse one small request this week with the three-part shape: constraint, alternative, preference.`,`Write your default sentence for the most common ask in your life — extra work, a favour, a last-minute plan — and rehearse it.`,`When you feel the urge to add a second reason, notice it and stop. One reason is a boundary; three reasons is a negotiation you have already lost.`],related:[`lib-dc2`,`lib-pr4`,`lib-pr3`]},"lib-dc5":{thesis:`A clean ending is kinder than a fade. Ambiguity is the same rejection, paid in instalments with hope as interest.`,deep:[`People avoid endings because they want to avoid causing pain. So they go quiet, become busy, answer more slowly — and the other person lives in a state of hope that costs them weeks. The pain is not avoided; it is redistributed, with interest, and it lands on someone who cannot ask what happened.`,`The clean version tells the truth without a case for the prosecution: what is ending, that it is a decision rather than a punishment, and — importantly — that there is nothing they can fix. "You have not done something wrong that you can fix" is often the most useful sentence in the conversation, because otherwise they will spend a month looking for the mistake.`,`Choosing is the other half. Endings that linger because of a final meeting, a last try, "one more weekend" prolong the ambiguity. Pick a date, say it clearly, and keep it. A decision that can be revisited is not a decision.`,`And expect it to feel worse, not better, in the moment. Clean endings produce a sharp, short pain; fades produce a long, quiet one and a person who is convinced they are unloveable. That trade is worth choosing deliberately.`],script:[{line:`"I have been thinking about this for a while, and I do not think we should carry on."`,note:`The decision, stated plainly, no ambiguity.`},{line:`"You have not done something wrong that you can fix. I want to be honest rather than gradual."`,note:`Removes the false task of fixing it.`},{line:`"I am not going to leave this open. I am sorry, and I did want to tell you in person."`,note:`Close the ambiguity, address the pain.`},{line:`"Is there anything you want to say? I have got time."`,note:`Give them their say, without reopening the decision.`}],failureModes:[`Leaving it open ("I just need some space") when you have already decided, which buys you comfort with their hope.`,`A list of character faults delivered as an exit — that is not honesty, it is the last word.`,`Disappearing instead of ending it, and then being surprised that you are considered cruel.`],drill:[`Write the four sentences you would say. Read them out loud once, so the first time you say them is not the moment it matters.`,`Notice where you want to leave a door open. Ask whether you are leaving it open for them or for you.`,`If you are on the receiving end of a fade, practise the direct question: "I would rather know — is this over?"`],related:[`lib-dc1`,`lib-wm4`,`lib-ei5`]},"lib-ei1":{thesis:`Match their depth, then go one small step further. Not five.`,deep:[`Depth in conversation is reciprocal, and it is governed by the same logic as any exchange: neither person wants to be the only one exposed. When one person is much more revealing than the other, the more exposed person feels unsafe and the less exposed person feels burdened.`,`The calibrated move is match plus one. If they say "work has been heavy", you do not respond with your father's illness; you respond with a question one step past where they stopped — "what is the heaviest part of it?" — and you offer your own equivalent when the moment opens.`,`Over-disclosure feels like intimacy to the person doing it and like an obligation to the person receiving it. Under-disclosure feels like coldness. One rung deeper is the whole craft, and it applies in friendships, dating, family, and the strange middle-distance of colleagues who have become something like friends.`,`The tell that you have gone too deep is a response that gets loud and formal at once — a summary sentence, a subject change, or the sudden involvement of your own situation. Step back down one rung, warmly, without apologising. Nothing has been broken.`],script:[{line:`Them: "Work has been heavy."`,note:`Rung two.`},{line:`"What is the heaviest part of it at the moment?"`,note:`One rung past. Question, not confession.`},{line:`Them: "I think it is the part where I cannot complain about it."`,note:`They moved first. Now you match.`},{line:`"I recognise that — last year I was the one telling everyone it was fine. It was lonely in a way I did not expect."`,note:`Match plus one, in your own experience.`}],failureModes:[`Flooding — disclosing three personal things and giving the other person no opening to respond at their pace.`,`Interviewing deep immediately with a stranger; depth questions land as intimacy only after ordinary ground has been walked.`,`Making yourself the subject of their disclosure ("that happened to me too, and…") which turns empathy into airtime.`],drill:[`In one conversation today, respond to a disclosure with a question one step past it. Note what happens.`,`Write a "match plus one" reply to a common disclosure so the pattern is available to you in the moment.`,`Notice one time you over-disclosed. What were you hoping for? Most often it is reassurance, and there is a better way to ask for it.`],related:[`lib-ei2`,`lib-ei3`,`lib-st3`]},"lib-ei2":{thesis:`Feel, fact, then fix. Never repair before they are heard.`,deep:[`When someone is upset, the instinct to help is a reflex that arrives before the information. So you offer solutions — and solutions are read as: what you are feeling is a problem I would like you to stop having.`,`The three-beat order works because it mirrors what a person actually needs. First the feeling is named, which is what "being understood" literally means. Then the fact is established, which shows you were listening rather than playing a role. Only then, and only with permission, does the fix appear.`,`Asking permission is the part almost nobody does. "Do you want ideas, or do you just want to be heard?" is one sentence and it prevents the most common scene in every relationship: the person in pain explaining why the advice is not right.`,`The same order works at work. A colleague whose project collapsed does not need your root-cause analysis in the first eight seconds; they need "that is a horrible week" and enough quiet to believe you said it.`],script:[{line:`"That sounds exhausting."`,note:`Name the feeling. Nothing else yet.`},{line:`"So you were doing both jobs for a month and nobody noticed."`,note:`Show the fact is registered.`},{line:`"Do you want ideas, or do you just want to be heard?"`,note:`Ask permission before repairing.`},{line:`Them: "Just heard." → "Then I am not going to solve it."`,note:`The reframe that earns trust.`}],failureModes:[`The silver lining ("at least you learned something"), which is a demand that they feel differently.`,`Competing stories: "the same thing happened to me, only worse."`,`Fixing without being asked, then feeling rejected when the fix is not appreciated.`],drill:[`In three conversations where someone reports a difficulty, respond with only a feeling word and no advice. Sit with the discomfort of not helping.`,`Ask the permission question once this week. Note the answer.`,`Record your own ratio for one conversation: how many sentences of listening versus sentences of solution.`],related:[`lib-ei5`,`lib-ei1`,`lib-dc1`]},"lib-ei3":{thesis:`Surface questions have correct answers. Real questions cost something to answer.`,deep:[`Most conversational questions are requests for data: where do you work, how long, do you like it. They are useful for finding the surface but they cannot take you below it, because they can be answered without the person being in any way present.`,`A question that costs something cannot be answered by a stranger's reflex. "What were you afraid would happen?" "What part of that is still with you?" "What would you do if it could not fail?" "When did you last feel really good about something you did?" These ask the person to hand over something real.`,`Cost is the mechanism, not a side effect. A question that costs the answerer something proves that the asker is not afraid of the answer — which is the definition of a room you can be honest in. This is why good interviewers and good friends feel structurally similar.`,`The safety requirement is real: cost only works where there is already enough trust to pay it. With a stranger at a party, the first cost question should be small — about something they care about, not something they carry. Read the answer: a short, deflecting reply means step back down to surface for a while.`],script:[{line:`"What is the thing you care about that nobody asks you about?"`,note:`A small cost. Good opener into depth.`},{line:`Them: "That is a big question."`,note:`A signal, not a refusal. Let them find it.`},{line:`"Take your time. I am not going anywhere."`,note:`The line that makes depth safe.`},{line:`"What would make the next year feel like it went well?"`,note:`Big, forward-facing, answerable.`}],failureModes:[`Cost questions as an opening move with someone you just met, which asks for intimacy before you have earned it.`,`Asking a real question and then responding with your own comparable story instead of following up.`,`Using depth questions instrumentally — as a technique to make people feel close to you, with no intention of holding what they say.`],drill:[`Choose one question from the list above and ask it of someone you already know. Then say nothing for three seconds.`,`Write the answer you would give to each one. Depth questions you cannot answer are hard to ask sincerely.`,`After one deep conversation, write down what the other person would say you now know about them. If it is only facts, the questions stayed on the surface.`],related:[`lib-ei1`,`lib-st6`,`lib-st2`]},"lib-ei4":{thesis:`Name the unspoken thing. It gives both people permission to stop performing.`,deep:[`Awkwardness in a room is usually not caused by an unknown thing; it is caused by a known thing nobody will say. Everyone can feel it, so everyone performs normality, and the performance is what makes the room unbearable.`,`Naming it is a one-sentence act with a disproportionate effect: "I notice we have both been very polite for ten minutes." "I have been avoiding this because I do not want to get it wrong." "This is the first time we have been in a room alone since the argument." You have said the thing out loud, without blame and without a demand.`,`The naming must be low-temperature and about the room rather than the person. "You are being weird" is a verdict; "I think we are both being a bit careful" includes you and turns it into a shared observation, which is the version people can agree with out loud.`,`It also fails gracefully. If the other person dismisses it, you have still shown that you are willing to be the one who speaks plainly, which is a reputation worth having. If they accept it, the relief in the room is immediate and often surprising.`],script:[{line:`"I think we are both being a bit careful here."`,note:`The room, not the person. Includes you.`},{line:`Them: "…yes, probably."`,note:`Permission accepted. Now say the true thing.`},{line:`"So: I was wrong about the flat, and I have been avoiding the conversation."`,note:`The real content, delivered plainly.`},{line:`"I would rather we said the awkward version than had an easy evening and got nowhere."`,note:`Names the trade explicitly.`}],failureModes:[`Diagnosing the other person: "you are being passive-aggressive" is a label, not a naming.`,`Naming and then immediately backing away from the content, so the room is opened and left open.`,`Using it as a way to force a conversation they are not ready for — naming the room is an invitation, not a lever.`],drill:[`In one awkward moment this week, say the sentence "I think we are both being a bit careful". Notice the temperature change.`,`Write the unspoken thing in your own family or team. Say it — once — with no blame attached.`,`Practise in a low-stakes setting: a group chat gone quiet, a meeting nobody is enjoying.`],related:[`lib-ei2`,`lib-dc1`,`lib-f2`]},"lib-ei5":{thesis:`Someone in grief needs a witness, not a solution. Presence is the support.`,deep:[`When someone is in real pain, we reach for words that will make the room tolerable for us. "Everything happens for a reason", "at least they are not suffering", "you will meet someone else" — every one of these is an instruction to feel differently, and every one lands as a small abandonment.`,`What actually helps is unambitious: showing up, staying, and being honest about your own inability to fix anything. "I do not know what to say. I am here and I am not going anywhere" is more useful than any sentence invented to ease the silence.`,`The other discipline is memory. Grief lasts far longer than sympathy, so the message in month three matters more than the flowers in week one. "I remember today is a year. I have not forgotten" is the kind of line people carry for decades.`,`And keep whatever you offer concrete. "Let me know if you need anything" transfers the work back to someone who has none to spare. "I am bringing food on Thursday at six; I will leave it at the door" is help that does not require them to coordinate.`],script:[{line:`"I do not know what to say."`,note:`Honesty is the only correct opening.`},{line:`"I am here, and I am not going anywhere."`,note:`Presence, not commentary.`},{line:`"Do you want to talk about him, or do you want me to just sit here?"`,note:`Give them the choice they rarely get.`},{line:`"I am bringing food on Thursday at six and leaving it at the door."`,note:`Concrete help, no coordination needed.`}],failureModes:[`The repair reflex: offering explanations, silver linings, or lessons.`,`Venerating the departed rather than listening to the living. They may need to say something unsentimental.`,`Going quiet after the funeral, when the second and third months are harder than the first.`],drill:[`Write down five things never to say to someone grieving, and one thing to say instead: "I am here."`,`Set a reminder for a month from now to message anyone you know who is going through something.`,`Practise sitting in silence for two minutes after someone says something sad. Do not fill it.`],related:[`lib-ei2`,`lib-ei1`,`lib-st6`]},"lib-pr1":{thesis:`Claim the floor with one sentence, make one point, hand it back.`,deep:[`Many people with good content say nothing in meetings, because they are waiting for a gap and for the perfect contribution. The gap never arrives and the perfect contribution is never available in real time, so they leave having been silent and are remembered as quiet rather than as thoughtful.`,`The reliable way in is the interjection with a purpose: "One thing before we move on —" is a sentence that requests the floor without aggression and buys you the two seconds to assemble a thought. Signalling that your contribution is short is what makes people happy to give you the floor.`,`One point, not a survey. "I think we are deciding this before we know who owns it" is a contribution. Five related observations is a monologue, and monologues are punished socially even when they are right.`,`Then hand the floor back deliberately: "That is all from me." Small, structured contributions repeated weekly build a reputation for judgement far faster than one long impressive speech, which mostly builds a reputation for length.`],script:[{line:`"One thing before we move on —"`,note:`The floor request. Brief and accepted.`},{line:`"I think we are deciding this before we know who owns it, and that will bite us in a fortnight."`,note:`One point, one consequence.`},{line:`"That is all from me."`,note:`Deliberate hand-back. The most underrated sentence in meetings.`},{line:`"Happy to take that offline if it is a longer conversation."`,note:`Protects the meeting from your own idea.`}],failureModes:[`Speaking only when the stakes are high, so your first contribution in months is a fully-formed opinion on the biggest issue.`,`Adding "does that make sense?" or "sorry, just a quick thought" — the apology removes the status the contribution earned.`,`Contributing an idea and then defending it for ten minutes, which converts a contribution into a fight.`],drill:[`Make one intervention in your next meeting, using "one thing before we move on" and "that is all from me".`,`Prepare exactly one sentence per meeting in advance. One is achievable; a lecture is not.`,`Count your contributions in each meeting. The goal is frequency with brevity, not volume.`],related:[`lib-pr3`,`lib-pr2`,`lib-dc4`]},"lib-pr2":{thesis:`Interview answers are judged on sequence, not brilliance: situation, obstacle, action, result.`,deep:[`Questions like "tell me about a time you handled conflict" are not invitations to be interesting. They are tests of whether you can structure an account of your own behaviour, which is a proxy for whether you can do it in the job.`,`SOAR gives the sequence: one line of situation, one line of obstacle (the part most people omit, which is also the part that reveals whether there was anything to overcome), two lines of action with your reasoning, one line of measured result. Six sentences. Almost nobody does this, which is why it stands out.`,`The result needs a number, or at least a comparison. "It went well" is not information. "We shipped eleven days late but under budget, and the client renewed" is. Numbers are also a memory aid under pressure: if you can remember the number, the rest of the story tends to follow.`,`Rehearse five of these against the job description you are actually applying for, not against your life history. A great story that does not map to what they are hiring for is a story about you, not an answer about the role.`],script:[{line:`"We were eight weeks from a launch and the vendor pulled out."`,note:`Situation and obstacle in one line.`},{line:`"I re-sequenced the roadmap so the client-facing work kept its date and the internal tooling slipped."`,note:`Action one, with reasoning.`},{line:`"I told the client the honest numbers before they asked, which is why they stayed."`,note:`Action two — the behaviour they are hiring for.`},{line:`"We shipped eleven days late and 4% under budget, and the contract renewed."`,note:`Measured result.`}],failureModes:[`Ninety seconds of context before anything happens, so the interviewer has to work out what the obstacle was.`,`Answering with a team story in which you did nothing identifiable ("we decided…").`,`No result, or a result that is a feeling ("everyone was relieved").`],drill:[`Write five SOAR answers for the five competencies in a job you would actually apply for. Read each out loud in under 70 seconds.`,`Record yourself once. Count the sentences before the obstacle appears.`,`Add a number to every result. If there is no number, produce a comparison.`],related:[`lib-pr1`,`lib-pr5`,`lib-bz4`]},"lib-pr3":{thesis:`Bring a decision, not a problem: two options, one recommendation, one request.`,deep:[`Managers are saturated with problems. Almost every message they receive is a person describing difficulty and waiting for direction, which means the scarce commodity — and therefore the visible one — is somebody who arrives with the situation already structured.`,`The upward one-liner has four parts: two options, a recommendation, a question about ownership. "A is faster and risky, B is slower and safe. I recommend A. Do you want me to run it?" It is under fifteen seconds and it demonstrates judgement, honesty about trade-offs, and respect for their authority at once.`,`The "do you want me to run it" is the part that distinguishes this from a unilateral decision. You are not asking permission to think; you are offering a recommendation and a clear point at which they can intervene. That combination is what promotion conversations are quietly about.`,`It also protects you. A recommendation that was accepted is a shared record that you were thinking; a problem you dumped is remembered as a problem you owned.`],script:[{line:`"Two options on the client thing."`,note:`Signal the structure. They can stop you if it is a bad moment.`},{line:`"A: we absorb the cost and keep the date. B: we renegotiate the date and keep the margin."`,note:`Two real options, honest about the trade.`},{line:`"I recommend B, because the date was our error and margin was not."`,note:`One recommendation with a reason.`},{line:`"Do you want me to run it, or do you want to be in the room when I tell them?"`,note:`An ownership question, not a permission request.`}],failureModes:[`Presenting two options that are obviously unequal — the recommendation becomes theatre.`,`Raising the problem with no options because you wanted thinking-partner time. That is legitimate; say so ("I need twenty minutes of thinking out loud").`,`Asking "what should I do?" after the recommendation, which hands the decision back and erases the point of the exercise.`],drill:[`Convert your next problem into the four-part shape before you message anyone. Send it that way.`,`Write down which one you would pick and why, before you speak. The recommendation is a skill, so practise deciding.`,`Ask for a decision explicitly at the end of three interactions this week. Notice how often silence replaced a clear answer.`],related:[`lib-pr1`,`lib-dc4`,`lib-bz1`]},"lib-pr4":{thesis:`Trade scope, quality or time — never quietly absorb the shortfall in your own health.`,deep:[`Deadline pressure usually arrives as a statement rather than a negotiation: "this needs to be done by Thursday". The person who says "I will manage" has agreed to absorb the impossible part personally, and the organisation learns nothing about the cost.`,`The alternative is to make the constraint explicit and put the choice where it belongs: "I can have it Thursday if I drop the QA pass, or Monday with it. Which matters more to you?" You have not refused; you have surfaced the actual trade. Managers make that choice competently all the time when they are shown it.`,`The same move handles scope creep. "I can add that, and something else moves. Which one do you want to move?" Overwork is rarely the result of one big demand; it is the result of hundreds of small unexamined ones, each of which seemed too trivial to negotiate.`,`Never trade sleep silently. Sacrifice that nobody records is not repaid — it becomes the new baseline, and the next request assumes it.`],script:[{line:`"I can do Thursday, but only if the QA pass goes."`,note:`State the real constraint first.`},{line:`"Or Monday with QA. Which matters more to you?"`,note:`The choice belongs to them.`},{line:`"That is fine — and it means the reporting piece moves to the following week."`,note:`Scope creep handled the same way.`},{line:`If both are demanded: "Then I can do neither well, and I would rather tell you that today than on Friday."`,note:`The honest stop, early, not late.`}],failureModes:[`"I will manage", which is a promise you cannot keep and which teaches them that the capacity exists.`,`Raising the constraint on Thursday morning, when the only remaining variable is your own suffering.`,`Absorbing it once "just this time" — the exception becomes the expectation by the second month.`],drill:[`Next time you are given a deadline, name one thing that would have to move. Say it in the same conversation.`,`Write down the last three times you absorbed an impossible deadline silently. What did it cost, and what did the other person learn?`,`Ask one manager or client: "If these two things both have to happen this week, which one wins?"`],related:[`lib-dc4`,`lib-pr3`,`lib-dc2`]},"lib-pr5":{thesis:`Two lines within 24 hours: reference, value, one ask.`,deep:[`Almost nobody follows up, which is why the people who do seem unusually effective. The follow-up is also the only part of networking that is actually in your control: you cannot make someone interesting, you can reliably send a good message the next day.`,`Two lines is the form. Line one references something specific they said, which proves you were listening and not collecting contacts. Line two offers something small or asks one clear question. No paragraph about your background, no request to pick their brain, no calendar link.`,`The offer matters more than the ask. A link to the thing they said they were looking for, a one-line introduction, a note about the book they mentioned — a follow-up that gives first is remembered; one that takes first is filed under sales.`,`Within 24 hours, without exception. A follow-up sent four days later arrives after the memory of you has cooled, and the specificity that made it good reads as slightly strange. Send it the same evening if you can; latency is the whole game.`],script:[{line:`Subject: the sensor question. Line one: "You said you were stuck on finding a supplier who would do small batches."`,note:`Reference, specific, one line.`},{line:`Line two: "I know one — Maya at Kestrel, she did 200 units for my last team. Want an intro?"`,note:`One offer, one ask, no paragraph.`},{line:`[Send within 24 hours. Nothing else in the message.]`,note:`The brevity is the point.`},{line:`If no reply in a week: one short nudge, then stop. Two follow-ups is the maximum; three is pressure.`,note:`Know when the sequence ends.`}],failureModes:[`The generic "great to meet you, let us connect" — it costs nothing, proves nothing, and gets nothing.`,`The long message about everything you do, sent as a follow-up, which invites a response to your CV rather than to the conversation.`,`Sending it two weeks later, when the specific reference now sounds like research.`],drill:[`Write a two-line follow-up template with blanks for the reference and the ask. Fill it in twice this week.`,`Practise one offer-first message to someone you have no request of.`,`Track follow-ups you sent versus replies. The ratio is usually better than people expect, and the data kills the fear.`],related:[`lib-pr2`,`lib-st4`,`lib-bz4`]},"lib-bz1":{thesis:`Three questions before one sentence about yourself. The buyer diagnoses; you prescribe.`,deep:[`The instinct on a sales call is to explain what you do, because you are worried it is not yet clear. But explanation before diagnosis is a claim about relevance made by someone with no evidence, and buyers are trained to discount it.`,`The sequence that works is situation, problem, implication. Where are you now, what is annoying about it, what does that annoyance cost you in time, money or morale. The implication question is where the sale is actually made: "how many hours a week does that eat? and what does that time cost you?" You are not persuading anyone; you are helping them count something they have been avoiding counting.`,`Only once the cost has been said out loud by the buyer does your solution stop being a pitch and start being an obvious next step. People do not buy products; they buy the removal of a cost they have just acknowledged in their own voice.`,`This also protects you from doing free work for the wrong buyer. If someone will not engage with the implication conversation, they will not buy, and you have saved yourself two proposals, three calls and a follow-up.`],script:[{line:`"How are you handling the scheduling today?"`,note:`Situation. Boring, necessary.`},{line:`"What is the most annoying part of doing it that way?"`,note:`Problem. Let them complain properly.`},{line:`"How many hours a week does that eat, and what does that time cost you?"`,note:`Implication. The sale happens here.`},{line:`"So the tooling question is really a question about a day a week. That is what I would want to fix first."`,note:`Only now do you talk about the fix.`}],failureModes:[`Pitching first and then asking diagnostic questions that are really just invitations to admire the pitch.`,`Asking implication questions that lead to a number you already have, which the buyer can feel.`,`Skipping situation and going straight to implication with a stranger — it reads as a trap, because it is one.`],drill:[`On your next call, commit to three questions before any description of what you do.`,`Write the implication question you would ask for your product in one sentence. Get a number for it.`,`Practise letting silence sit after the implication question. The buyer fills it with the cost.`],related:[`lib-bz2`,`lib-pr3`,`lib-bz4`]},"lib-bz2":{thesis:`Never argue with an objection. Agree with the concern, then move the axis of comparison.`,deep:[`An objection is a fear stated as a fact. "You are too expensive" means "I am afraid of spending this and looking foolish". Arguing with it — "actually, we are competitively priced" — confirms the fear by treating it as a claim to be defeated.`,`Agreement first defuses the defence: "You are right that we are the most expensive option." Nobody expects it, and the concession buys you the credibility to say the next sentence.`,`Then move the axis. The objection was framed on price-in-month-one; you reframe to total-cost-in-year-two, or risk, or the cost of a failed implementation. "The question is whether the cheaper one costs you more in the second year — the last client moved at month twelve." You are not disputing their frame; you are offering a different, more useful one.`,`Close by handing the choice back rather than pressing: "which risk is easier for you to own?" That sentence treats the buyer as an adult and makes the decision theirs, which is the only version that survives to signature.`],script:[{line:`Them: "That is a lot more than the other quote."`,note:`The objection.`},{line:`"You are right, we are the most expensive option on the table."`,note:`Agree. No defence.`},{line:`"The question I would want answered is what the cheaper option costs you in year two — the last client moved at month twelve."`,note:`Move the axis.`},{line:`"Which risk is easier to own: the price, or the second year?"`,note:`Their decision, explicitly.`}],failureModes:[`Reeling off features in response to a price objection, which signals that you heard "insufficient value" and are now listing.`,`Discounting immediately to end the discomfort, which teaches the buyer that the price was invented.`,`Agreeing so enthusiastically that you talk yourself out of the sale ("yes, we are very expensive").`],drill:[`List your three most common objections. Write the agreement sentence for each, out loud, until it is automatic.`,`Write the second axis for each: what is the true comparison your buyer should be making?`,`In your next negotiation, say the agreement sentence and then stop talking for three seconds.`],related:[`lib-bz1`,`lib-bz3`,`lib-bz4`]},"lib-bz3":{thesis:`Name the number once, then let the silence do the work.`,deep:[`Price is delivered, not discussed. The most common error is to state a figure and then keep talking — justifying, qualifying, offering options — because the silence feels unbearable. Every additional sentence after the number reduces it.`,`Say the figure in the middle of a sentence, flatly, at normal volume. "Based on that scope, the number is 12,400." No apology, no "around", no "we could probably". Then nothing at all.`,`The principle underneath: the first person to speak after the price either defends it or concedes it. If you fill the silence, you are defending. If they fill it, they are usually conceding something — a budget range, a decision date, an internal champion.`,`Expect discomfort. The silence after a figure is only about four seconds long in reality, and it is the most profitable four seconds in the conversation. Practise it with small numbers first — a quote for a favour, a rate for a small job — so the reflex is trained before the big one.`],script:[{line:`"Based on that scope, the number is 12,400."`,note:`Flat, mid-sentence, no hedge.`},{line:`[Silence. Do not add a single word. Breathe out.]`,note:`The four seconds that decide it.`},{line:`Them: "That is more than I had in mind."`,note:`They moved first. Now you can negotiate.`},{line:`"What did you have in mind, and what would have to be true for it to work?"`,note:`You are still not discounting. You are diagnosing.`}],failureModes:[`Qualifying immediately ("but we could look at scope") — you have negotiated against yourself.`,`Rounding down pre-emptively before anyone has objected.`,`Delivering the number apologetically, quieter and faster than the rest of the sentence, which signals you do not believe it.`],drill:[`Practise saying a number out loud in a flat voice, then waiting, in an empty room. Ten times. The reflex is physical.`,`Next quote you send, remove every hedge word: around, approximately, flexible, happy to discuss.`,`In one conversation about money this week, be the second person to speak.`],related:[`lib-bz2`,`lib-bz4`,`lib-f5`]},"lib-bz4":{thesis:`Volunteer one real limitation and everything else you say becomes believable.`,deep:[`Buyers assume that pitches are edited. So the more uniformly excellent your claims, the more discount they apply — and the discount is invisible, which is what makes it so damaging.`,`A volunteered weakness inverts the dynamic. "The thing this is weakest at is reporting — it is slow, and if you need instant dashboards I am not your best option" does three things at once: it proves you know your product, it demonstrates you are not editing, and it hands the buyer a test they can run, which makes every other claim verifiable.`,`The weakness must be real and must matter to someone. Cosmetic flaws ("we are almost too responsive") are transparent and make the whole pitch smell worse than no concession at all.`,`Use it early, before the objection arrives. Volunteered, it is credibility. Extracted, it is a confession. Same fact, opposite effect — the timing is the entire trick.`],script:[{line:`"Before you ask: the weakest part is reporting."`,note:`Early, volunteered, unprompted.`},{line:`"It is slow, and if you need instant dashboards, I am not your best option."`,note:`Real, specific, matters to someone.`},{line:`Them: "We do need dashboards, actually."`,note:`Good — that is the conversation you want now, not in month three.`},{line:`"Then let us look at whether the export covers it. It probably covers 80% of what you described."`,note:`Honest again. The credibility compounds.`}],failureModes:[`Fake weaknesses designed to be praised ("I work too hard").`,`Confessing something deal-breaking in a way that wastes their time — the goal is credibility, not self-sabotage.`,`Volunteering the weakness after they have discovered it, which retroactively converts honesty into concealment.`],drill:[`Write your three genuine limitations in one sentence each, in buyer language.`,`Say one out loud at the start of your next pitch and watch the room's response.`,`After each pitch, ask yourself: what did they learn that I did not want them to learn? Answer that first next time.`],related:[`lib-bz1`,`lib-bz2`,`lib-pr3`]}},h=e({WRITTEN_DEEP:()=>g}),g={"lib-wr1":{thesis:`A message does not contain a tone. The reader supplies one, usually from their own mood and their history with you — which is why the same line can land warm on Monday and cold on Friday.`,deep:[`This is not a failure of writing skill. Speech carries prosody: pitch, pace, pauses, the half-smile. Remove all of it and a sentence becomes a bare proposition that the reader must score emotionally themselves. They use three inputs to do it: what they know about your intentions, how the last exchange ended, and their own current state. You control exactly none of those at read time. You control only the words and the timing.`,`The two things readers use to infer tone are ambiguity of intent ("what does he actually want from me?") and ambiguity of regard ("is he annoyed at me?"). Both get resolved in the reader's head with the most negative plausible answer, because a negative answer is safer to act on than a positive one. This is why brevity reads as coldness and why "ok" is the most dangerous word in the language.`,`The unreliable fix is decoration: exclamation marks, emoji, "haha", deliberate misspellings. It gets read as effort, and effort in a message about nothing reads as anxiety. The reliable fix is to remove the guesswork. Name the ask. Name the regard. "Can you send the file by Thursday? No rush if it slips to Friday — I just need to know which." That message has no tone at all, and therefore cannot be misread.`,`There is one more move that is underused: the pre-emptive disclaimer. "Nothing is wrong, I just want to ask you something" costs six words and disarms an entire category of misreading before it starts.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`"Can you send the file by Thursday? No rush if it slips to Friday — I just need to know which."`},{who:`You`,line:`"I am not annoyed, I am just being brief — that is a text problem, not a you problem."`},{who:`You`,line:`"Nothing is wrong. I have a boring logistics question."`}],failureModes:[`Adding warmth by decoration — more exclamation marks, more emoji, more "haha" — which reads as anxiety rather than friendliness.`,`Assuming the reader has your context. They have your last three messages and a bad mood.`,`Apologising for the message before sending it ("sorry to bother you") — you have now made the message an imposition and told them so.`,`Using brevity as efficiency with people who read brevity as coldness. Know your reader.`],drill:[`Take the last message that got a confusing reply. Rewrite it as one ask, one deadline, one clause of regard. Send the rewrite as a follow-up without explaining why.`],related:[`lib-wr3`,`lib-wr4`]},"lib-wr2":{thesis:`Response time is a signal you cannot switch off. You can only decide what it says.`,deep:[`Human beings treat delay as information. In every study of reciprocity, longer gaps between turns correlate with lower engagement — and readers are exquisitely sensitive to changes in a person's usual rhythm. If you normally reply in ten minutes and one day take eight hours, that gap will be read as something, even if you were on a train with no signal.`,`The three readings are warmth, status, and punishment. Fast replies read as warm or available. Slow replies read as busy, important, or cold. And after any friction, a sudden slowdown reads as a verdict: "he has decided something about me". This is why silence after an argument is more painful than any reply — the absence is interpreted, and it is interpreted as the worst available meaning.`,`You cannot opt out. Claiming you "do not play games with reply times" is honest but does not change what the other person reads. What you can do is choose a rhythm deliberately and keep it stable, because stability is what removes the signal. A person who reliably answers logistics within a day and feelings within a few hours is not sending any message at all, and that is the goal.`,`When you genuinely need time, say so. One line — "I want to answer this properly, give me until tonight" — converts a silence that reads as avoidance into a wait that reads as care. It is the single highest-return message in written communication, and almost nobody sends it.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`"I want to answer this properly — give me until tonight."`},{who:`You`,line:`"I went quiet because I did not know what to say, not because I did not care. Here is the honest answer."`},{who:`You`,line:`"I am bad at same-day replies and it is never about you. If something is urgent, call me and I will pick up."`}],failureModes:[`Deliberately delaying a reply to seem busy — it reads as busy to you and as rejection to them, and only one of those is happening.`,`Leaving an emotionally loaded message unanswered for days because you do not have the right words. Silence is a message with worse words.`,`Matching someone else's slow replies as a form of protest. You are both now in a silent contest nobody will win.`,`Apologising repeatedly for reply speed. Once is courtesy; five times is a request for reassurance.`],drill:[`Find the message you have been avoiding. Send one line saying when you will answer it. Notice that the dread drops before you have answered anything.`],related:[`lib-wr6`,`lib-wr3`]},"lib-wr3":{thesis:`Written conflict has none of the three things that de-escalate conflict: tone, timing, and the sight of the other person softening.`,deep:[`A live argument carries continuous repair. You hear the wobble in their voice and soften. You see their face change and stop. You can say "hang on, that came out wrong" and it lands in the same second. Written conflict removes all of it and replaces it with three accelerants: a permanent record you can re-read, an audience who may see it later, and latency, which lets both parties construct their case instead of resolving it.`,`That is why the same disagreement that would take four minutes on a call consumes three days by text and ends with both people quoting each other's worst sentence. Neither party is being unreasonable. The medium is doing it.`,`The move is not to write more carefully. Careful writing in a conflict reads as legal drafting, which escalates. The move is to change channels the moment you notice the temperature rising — and to notice early, at the point where you are constructing an argument rather than answering a question.`,`If a call is impossible, change the register instead: fewer words, one question, no case-building. A three-sentence message ending in a time to talk does more repair than a beautifully written paragraph, because the paragraph is still an argument and the three sentences are still an offer.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`"This is getting long and I do not want to get it wrong. Can we talk at 6?"`},{who:`You`,line:`"I think we read that differently. I am not trying to win this. Call tonight?"`},{who:`You`,line:`"I am not going to do this by text — it will come out wrong. I am free tonight and tomorrow morning."`}],failureModes:[`Writing the paragraph. You will send it, and it will be received as a lawyer's letter regardless of how fair it is.`,`Quoting their worst line back at them, which guarantees they defend the sentence instead of the point.`,`Going silent with no return ticket — that is read as a verdict, and it escalates in a different direction.`,`Using "I am just being honest" as cover for scoring points. Honesty in writing is mostly indistinguishable from precision cruelty.`],drill:[`In your next written disagreement, send three sentences maximum, ending with a time to talk. Do not send a fourth.`],related:[`lib-dc1`,`lib-wr2`]},"lib-wr4":{thesis:`Multiple asks in one message produce one reply, and it is never the important one.`,deep:[`A reader triaging a message looks for the cheapest way to discharge it. Three asks present three exits, and the easiest exit is the one that gets taken: the quick yes, the one they can answer from memory, or worse, the thumbs-up that acknowledges everything and commits to nothing. The important ask dies quietly, and you find out three days later.`,`This is not laziness. It is how attention works under load. A message with one request has one obvious action; a message with four has a small project, and projects get scheduled. Splitting the message converts a project into four actions, each of which can be done in the thirty seconds between meetings.`,`The structure that survives busy readers: the ask in the first line, the deadline and the consequence in the same line, the context underneath and clearly optional. "Quick ask: approve the £2k budget by Thursday? Context below, no need to read it if the answer is yes." A reader can now answer without reading the rest, which is exactly what you want.`,`Say what happens if you hear nothing. Silence should be a decision rather than a limbo, for them and for you: "If I do not have it by Thursday I will assume no and re-plan." That sentence alone converts most vague threads into answers, because it makes the cost of not replying explicit without being a threat.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`"Quick ask: can you approve the £2k budget by Thursday? Context below, no need to read it if the answer is yes."`},{who:`You`,line:`"If I do not have it by Thursday I will assume no and re-plan — that is fine, I just need to know."`},{who:`You`,line:`"A no is genuinely useful here — it lets me move on today."`}],failureModes:[`Three asks in one message. You will get one answer, and statistically not the one that mattered.`,`Burying the ask under two paragraphs of background. The background is for the people who say yes.`,`Following up with "just bumping this" and no new information — it adds pressure without adding a reason to reply.`,`Asking two people the same question in one thread, which guarantees neither feels responsible.`],drill:[`Open your drafts and find a message with more than one request. Split it, put each ask in its own first line, and send them separately.`],related:[`lib-cr1`,`lib-wr1`]},"lib-wr5":{thesis:`Every channel carries a default weight. Sending heavy content in a light channel insults the content.`,deep:[`Channels are not neutral pipes. A meme thread, a voice note, an email, a letter, a call — each arrives with a built-in register, and the reader applies that register before they parse the content. Heavy news arriving in the light channel reads as a joke or as an ambush, depending on which sentence they reach first.`,`The reverse failure is more common at work: light content in a formal channel creates dread. A subject line of "Quick question" above three paragraphs makes people brace for bad news, and by the time they reach "what do you want for lunch" they are annoyed at you for the adrenaline.`,`Choosing well is mostly a matter of matching three variables: weight (how much this changes something), intimacy (how close you are), and the need for a response. Endings, apologies, and bad news want a call or a voice. Coordination and warmth are fine in text. Formal channels are for anything that may be forwarded.`,`And the universal fix, when you are unsure: one line naming the weight before the content. "Nothing is wrong, I just want to ask you something." It costs six words and removes the entire guessing layer.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`"Nothing is wrong — well, something is, but I am not angry at you. Read this one properly."`},{who:`You`,line:`"Logistics, not drama. Two quick things."`},{who:`You`,line:`"Can I call you in ten minutes? I would rather say the rest out loud."`}],failureModes:[`"We need to talk" with nothing after it — dread with no release, and the cruellest sentence in modern messaging.`,`Sending bad news at midnight, when you cannot answer a reply and they cannot sleep.`,`Using a group chat for something that only two people should see.`,`Putting an apology in a DM to a person you owe a call. It reads as an attempt to minimise, because it is one.`],drill:[`Send your next piece of heavy news with a weight marker in the first line and a call offered in the last.`],related:[`lib-wr3`,`lib-wr6`]},"lib-wr6":{thesis:`A voice note is warmer than text and cheaper than a call — for the first forty seconds.`,deep:[`Voice restores the thing text removes: prosody. Your hesitation, your laugh, your ordinary warmth all arrive without having to be manufactured in punctuation. For anyone you cannot see often, a forty-second note does more for the relationship than a week of well-written messages.`,`It is also the most abused format in messaging, because rambling is easier than composing. A three-minute recording is not intimacy; it is a monologue the other person cannot interrupt, and it converts a message into an appointment. Most people postpone it, which is the opposite of the intended effect.`,`Forty seconds is the working limit: long enough for a short story plus a question, short enough to be heard immediately, on a train, with one ear. Structure it like a tiny story — the thing that happened, what it made you think, one question back — and it stops being a download and becomes a conversation.`,`The last rule is a triage rule: if the message contains a decision, a date, a name or a number, put it in text as well. Voice is for warmth and story. Logistics in audio get lost, and the person has to replay you three times to extract a time, which is a small cruelty dressed as friendliness.`],script:[{who:`You`,line:`"Something happened today that you will find funny — [story]. It made me think about what you said about quitting. Did you actually do it?"`},{who:`You`,line:`"This is too long for a voice note. Are you free for ten minutes tonight?"`},{who:`You`,line:`"Voice note coming, but the actual question is: are you around Thursday?"`}],failureModes:[`Sending the rambling take. It is not intimacy, it is a monologue with no exit.`,`Burying an ask at minute two, where it will be missed and you will have to follow up anyway.`,`Re-recording five times. The fifth take sounds like a hostage statement and takes the warmth you were trying to send.`,`Voice-noting someone who has told you, directly or not, that they prefer text.`],drill:[`Send one voice note under forty seconds: a story, what it made you think, one question. Put any logistics in text.`],related:[`lib-wr2`,`lib-wr5`]}};export{s as a,t as c,l as i,p as n,a as o,d as r,r as s,h as t};